{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011FDhbfUie4P3NEoPE4bfBk","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Empty KB Placeholder\n2\t\n3\tThis placeholder exists only because the installed TITO research workflow\n4\trequires `MANIFEST.source.documents` to contain at least one Markdown document.\n5\t\n6\tThis file contains no bibliographic evidence, no project result, no engineering\n7\tmethod claim, and no source material for public report prose. It must not be\n8\tcited as evidence for blast-vibration standards, charge-window definitions,\n9\tcomponent attenuation models, waveform processing, response spectra, PLAXIS\n10\tinput requirements, or any AR-S2J2J project result.\n11\t\n12\tFor substantive claims, TITO research sessions should use external sources\n13\tfound during the research pass, or future approved KB documents that contain\n14\treal source material.\n15\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"193e9b03-f0e0-48bb-82cd-24114a1d1cbc","uuid":"a8ff081d-f59a-4b00-92ff-48f7bb2f8252","timestamp":"2026-07-06T19:41:13.648Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/averrik/Cloud/github/projects/AR-S2J2J.R/TITO/kb/EMPTY-KB.md","content":"# Empty KB Placeholder\n\nThis placeholder exists only because the installed TITO research workflow\nrequires `MANIFEST.source.documents` to contain at least one Markdown document.\n\nThis file contains no bibliographic evidence, no project result, no engineering\nmethod claim, and no source material for public report prose. It must not be\ncited as evidence for blast-vibration standards, charge-window definitions,\ncomponent attenuation models, waveform processing, response spectra, PLAXIS\ninput requirements, or any AR-S2J2J project result.\n\nFor substantive claims, TITO research sessions should use external sources\nfound during the research pass, or future approved KB documents that contain\nreal source material.\n","numLines":15,"startLine":1,"totalLines":15}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LCpwpVhnU4kFBMpiL9q1CC","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t### SLOT 1: Relevant Standards and Guidance Documents\n2\t\n3\tFor a South African mining or tailings storage facility report, the standards hierarchy should be stated conservatively: local legal approvals, permit conditions, mine blasting procedures, and any project-specified South African National Standard control compliance wording, while external standards can support measurement practice, interpretation, and benchmarking. Where no project record supplies the local standard text or a project-specific acceptance criterion, the report should avoid presenting an externally sourced number as a South African compliance criterion. The strongest directly cited international baseline for fixed-structure vibration is ISO 4866:2010, which establishes principles for vibration measurement and data processing for evaluation of effects on structures, applies to structures above and below ground including earth structures such as dykes and embankments, and addresses sources including explosions and construction activities, while excluding some special structures including dams. [@Ref001][@Ref002][@Ref003]\n4\t\n5\tThe most relevant international documents for report wording are ISO 4866:2010 for structural-vibration measurement and evaluation principles, BS 7385-2:1993 for building damage assessment from groundborne vibration, BS 6472-2:2008 for human exposure to blast-induced vibration in buildings, ISO 2631-2:2026 for human comfort and annoyance from whole-body vibration in buildings, the U.S. Office of Surface Mining Reclamation and Enforcement rules in 30 CFR 816.67 and 30 CFR 816.68 as a clear regulatory example of PPV, scaled-distance, monitoring, and blast-record requirements, the IFC Environmental, Health, and Safety Guidelines for Mining as good international industry practice for mining noise, vibration, blasting, and tailings management, and the Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management for TSF knowledge-base, design, governance, and monitoring context. [@Ref003][@Ref004][@Ref005][@Ref006][@Ref007][@Ref008][@Ref001][@Ref002]\n6\t\n7\t### SLOT 2: Criteria and Reporting Distinctions\n8\t\n9\tThe cited sources separate structural damage criteria from human response criteria. BS 7385-2 is a building damage document: it gives guidance on assessing the possibility of vibration-induced damage in buildings, covers measurement, recording, analysis, and damage records, and is limited to ground-transmitted vibration rather than vibration from machinery inside a building. BS 6472-2 is a human-exposure document for blast-induced vibration in occupied buildings, especially periodic blasting associated with mineral extraction, and it expressly does not address equipment malfunction, building damage, occupant injury, legal liability, or vibration minimization. ISO 2631-2:2026 similarly concerns comfort and annoyance from whole-body vibration in buildings, states that structural damage is discussed in ISO 4866 rather than in ISO 2631-2, and states that acceptable magnitudes are not set in that document. [@Ref004][@Ref005][@Ref006]\n10\t\n11\tGeotechnical effects and TSF performance should be reported as a separate engineering question rather than folded into building-damage or human-annoyance criteria. The IFC mining guidance treats waste dumps, tailings impoundments, and dams as facilities whose geotechnical risks and environmental impacts require risk assessment, recognized tailings standards, independent review, and ongoing physical and water-quality monitoring, while the Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management requires an interdisciplinary knowledge base including geotechnical and seismicity information, documented design criteria, and integrated performance monitoring. By contrast, 30 CFR 816.67 gives protected-structure PPV and scaled-distance provisions for specified occupied structures and separately requires other structures such as dams, impoundments, utilities, tunnels, and underground mines to be protected through maximum allowable vibration limits submitted in the blasting plan and approved by the regulator. [@Ref001][@Ref002][@Ref007]\n12\t\n13\tMonitoring and reporting requirements are another distinct category. ISO 4866 supports measurement and processing principles for structural-vibration evaluation, BS 7385-2 supports measurement, recording, analysis, and damage-record procedures for buildings, and 30 CFR 816.68 provides a concrete blast-record template that includes location, date and time, blaster details, distance to protected structures, blast pattern sketches, burden, spacing, decks, delay pattern, hole dimensions, explosive type, explosive weight per hole, maximum weight detonated in an 8-millisecond period, initiation system, stemming, protective measures, and seismographic and airblast record details where required. [@Ref003][@Ref004][@Ref008]\n14\t\n15\t### SLOT 3: Charge Per Delay and Charge Window Definitions\n16\t\n17\tMaximum charge per delay and maximum instantaneous charge should not be treated as self-defining terms in professional report text. In the clearest cited regulatory formulation, 30 CFR 816.67 defines the charge term used in its scaled-distance equation as the maximum weight of explosives detonated in any 8-millisecond period, and 30 CFR 816.68 requires the blast record to include the maximum weight of explosives detonated in an 8-millisecond period. This anchors the reported charge to a defined short-delay time window rather than to the total blast mass, the total explosive mass in a blast design, or a nominal delay number that may contain more than one hole or deck. [@Ref007][@Ref008]\n18\t\n19\tFor report purposes, charge per delay should therefore be defined explicitly as the summed explosive mass assigned to the relevant delay or to the relevant rolling charge window, with the chosen convention stated in the methods. Maximum instantaneous charge can be used as a synonym only if the report defines it as the maximum explosive mass assumed to detonate together under that same window rule. A short-delay charge window should also state whether the calculation uses nominal design delays, measured firing times, or vendor timing tolerances; whether the window is rolling or grouped by labelled delay; and whether endpoints are included, because those choices can change the maximum reported charge without changing the physical blast. [@Ref007][@Ref008]\n20\t\n21\t### SLOT 4: Status of a 9 ms Charge Window\n22\t\n23\tThe cited standards and regulatory sources support an 8-millisecond charge period, not an independent 9 ms charge-window standard. The 8-millisecond period appears in both the 30 CFR 816.67 scaled-distance provision and the 30 CFR 816.68 blast-record provision, whereas the cited ISO, BSI, IFC, and GISTM sources do not establish a 9 ms charge-window definition for blast-vibration reporting. On that evidence, a 9 ms charge window should be treated as project-specific, software-specific, or vendor-specific unless the project blasting procedure, contract, permit, or monitoring method statement expressly defines it. [@Ref007][@Ref008][@Ref003][@Ref004][@Ref005][@Ref006][@Ref001][@Ref002]\n24\t\n25\tA 9 ms separation rule is not the same thing as a 9 ms charge window. If a method applies an 8-millisecond rolling window to integer-millisecond nominal delays, charges separated by at least 9 ms may fall outside a single 8-millisecond grouping under that implementation; however, that is a boundary convention derived from the 8-millisecond rule, not evidence that all charges in a 9 ms period must be summed. Report text should therefore say \"maximum charge within any 8 ms period\" when that is the adopted basis, or should define the alternative 9 ms calculation as a project assumption without calling it a standard criterion. [@Ref007][@Ref008]\n26\t\n27\t### SLOT 5: Dual-Blast or Multi-Design Events\n28\t\n29\tDual-blast or multi-design events should be described by pairing charge and distance within each blast design before any comparison, scaled-distance calculation, or source attribution is made. The eCFR scaled-distance provision defines the charge variable and distance variable in the same equation, and the blast-record provision requires the record to include the blast location, distance to protected structures, pattern sketches, delay pattern, explosive weights, initiation system, and seismographic records where required. That structure supports report tables in which each design or blast source has its own distance to the monitor or protected structure, its own maximum charge in the stated delay window, and its own timing relationship to the measured vibration record. [@Ref007][@Ref008]\n30\t\n31\tWhere two blast designs contribute to one monitoring event, the measured PPV or other vibration output can be reported as an event-level measurement, but source-specific interpretation should be made only where timing, trigger records, waveform arrivals, or blast logs support attribution. It is not methodologically sound to combine the largest charge from one design with the shortest distance from another design as though they formed a physical source-receptor pair, unless the report labels the result as a deliberately conservative envelope rather than as the scaled distance of an actual blast design. If delay schedules overlap at the receptor, the charge-window calculation should be checked for each individual design and, where timing information supports it, for the combined event across the same stated rolling window. [@Ref007][@Ref008]\n32\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"193e9b03-f0e0-48bb-82cd-24114a1d1cbc","uuid":"42c7fa09-b6d4-42e6-ac68-7afe2b931b9f","timestamp":"2026-07-06T19:41:13.653Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/averrik/Cloud/github/projects/AR-S2J2J.R/TITO/kb/T1.compile.research.md","content":"### SLOT 1: Relevant Standards and Guidance Documents\n\nFor a South African mining or tailings storage facility report, the standards hierarchy should be stated conservatively: local legal approvals, permit conditions, mine blasting procedures, and any project-specified South African National Standard control compliance wording, while external standards can support measurement practice, interpretation, and benchmarking. Where no project record supplies the local standard text or a project-specific acceptance criterion, the report should avoid presenting an externally sourced number as a South African compliance criterion. The strongest directly cited international baseline for fixed-structure vibration is ISO 4866:2010, which establishes principles for vibration measurement and data processing for evaluation of effects on structures, applies to structures above and below ground including earth structures such as dykes and embankments, and addresses sources including explosions and construction activities, while excluding some special structures including dams. [@Ref001][@Ref002][@Ref003]\n\nThe most relevant international documents for report wording are ISO 4866:2010 for structural-vibration measurement and evaluation principles, BS 7385-2:1993 for building damage assessment from groundborne vibration, BS 6472-2:2008 for human exposure to blast-induced vibration in buildings, ISO 2631-2:2026 for human comfort and annoyance from whole-body vibration in buildings, the U.S. Office of Surface Mining Reclamation and Enforcement rules in 30 CFR 816.67 and 30 CFR 816.68 as a clear regulatory example of PPV, scaled-distance, monitoring, and blast-record requirements, the IFC Environmental, Health, and Safety Guidelines for Mining as good international industry practice for mining noise, vibration, blasting, and tailings management, and the Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management for TSF knowledge-base, design, governance, and monitoring context. [@Ref003][@Ref004][@Ref005][@Ref006][@Ref007][@Ref008][@Ref001][@Ref002]\n\n### SLOT 2: Criteria and Reporting Distinctions\n\nThe cited sources separate structural damage criteria from human response criteria. BS 7385-2 is a building damage document: it gives guidance on assessing the possibility of vibration-induced damage in buildings, covers measurement, recording, analysis, and damage records, and is limited to ground-transmitted vibration rather than vibration from machinery inside a building. BS 6472-2 is a human-exposure document for blast-induced vibration in occupied buildings, especially periodic blasting associated with mineral extraction, and it expressly does not address equipment malfunction, building damage, occupant injury, legal liability, or vibration minimization. ISO 2631-2:2026 similarly concerns comfort and annoyance from whole-body vibration in buildings, states that structural damage is discussed in ISO 4866 rather than in ISO 2631-2, and states that acceptable magnitudes are not set in that document. [@Ref004][@Ref005][@Ref006]\n\nGeotechnical effects and TSF performance should be reported as a separate engineering question rather than folded into building-damage or human-annoyance criteria. The IFC mining guidance treats waste dumps, tailings impoundments, and dams as facilities whose geotechnical risks and environmental impacts require risk assessment, recognized tailings standards, independent review, and ongoing physical and water-quality monitoring, while the Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management requires an interdisciplinary knowledge base including geotechnical and seismicity information, documented design criteria, and integrated performance monitoring. By contrast, 30 CFR 816.67 gives protected-structure PPV and scaled-distance provisions for specified occupied structures and separately requires other structures such as dams, impoundments, utilities, tunnels, and underground mines to be protected through maximum allowable vibration limits submitted in the blasting plan and approved by the regulator. [@Ref001][@Ref002][@Ref007]\n\nMonitoring and reporting requirements are another distinct category. ISO 4866 supports measurement and processing principles for structural-vibration evaluation, BS 7385-2 supports measurement, recording, analysis, and damage-record procedures for buildings, and 30 CFR 816.68 provides a concrete blast-record template that includes location, date and time, blaster details, distance to protected structures, blast pattern sketches, burden, spacing, decks, delay pattern, hole dimensions, explosive type, explosive weight per hole, maximum weight detonated in an 8-millisecond period, initiation system, stemming, protective measures, and seismographic and airblast record details where required. [@Ref003][@Ref004][@Ref008]\n\n### SLOT 3: Charge Per Delay and Charge Window Definitions\n\nMaximum charge per delay and maximum instantaneous charge should not be treated as self-defining terms in professional report text. In the clearest cited regulatory formulation, 30 CFR 816.67 defines the charge term used in its scaled-distance equation as the maximum weight of explosives detonated in any 8-millisecond period, and 30 CFR 816.68 requires the blast record to include the maximum weight of explosives detonated in an 8-millisecond period. This anchors the reported charge to a defined short-delay time window rather than to the total blast mass, the total explosive mass in a blast design, or a nominal delay number that may contain more than one hole or deck. [@Ref007][@Ref008]\n\nFor report purposes, charge per delay should therefore be defined explicitly as the summed explosive mass assigned to the relevant delay or to the relevant rolling charge window, with the chosen convention stated in the methods. Maximum instantaneous charge can be used as a synonym only if the report defines it as the maximum explosive mass assumed to detonate together under that same window rule. A short-delay charge window should also state whether the calculation uses nominal design delays, measured firing times, or vendor timing tolerances; whether the window is rolling or grouped by labelled delay; and whether endpoints are included, because those choices can change the maximum reported charge without changing the physical blast. [@Ref007][@Ref008]\n\n### SLOT 4: Status of a 9 ms Charge Window\n\nThe cited standards and regulatory sources support an 8-millisecond charge period, not an independent 9 ms charge-window standard. The 8-millisecond period appears in both the 30 CFR 816.67 scaled-distance provision and the 30 CFR 816.68 blast-record provision, whereas the cited ISO, BSI, IFC, and GISTM sources do not establish a 9 ms charge-window definition for blast-vibration reporting. On that evidence, a 9 ms charge window should be treated as project-specific, software-specific, or vendor-specific unless the project blasting procedure, contract, permit, or monitoring method statement expressly defines it. [@Ref007][@Ref008][@Ref003][@Ref004][@Ref005][@Ref006][@Ref001][@Ref002]\n\nA 9 ms separation rule is not the same thing as a 9 ms charge window. If a method applies an 8-millisecond rolling window to integer-millisecond nominal delays, charges separated by at least 9 ms may fall outside a single 8-millisecond grouping under that implementation; however, that is a boundary convention derived from the 8-millisecond rule, not evidence that all charges in a 9 ms period must be summed. Report text should therefore say \"maximum charge within any 8 ms period\" when that is the adopted basis, or should define the alternative 9 ms calculation as a project assumption without calling it a standard criterion. [@Ref007][@Ref008]\n\n### SLOT 5: Dual-Blast or Multi-Design Events\n\nDual-blast or multi-design events should be described by pairing charge and distance within each blast design before any comparison, scaled-distance calculation, or source attribution is made. The eCFR scaled-distance provision defines the charge variable and distance variable in the same equation, and the blast-record provision requires the record to include the blast location, distance to protected structures, pattern sketches, delay pattern, explosive weights, initiation system, and seismographic records where required. That structure supports report tables in which each design or blast source has its own distance to the monitor or protected structure, its own maximum charge in the stated delay window, and its own timing relationship to the measured vibration record. [@Ref007][@Ref008]\n\nWhere two blast designs contribute to one monitoring event, the measured PPV or other vibration output can be reported as an event-level measurement, but source-specific interpretation should be made only where timing, trigger records, waveform arrivals, or blast logs support attribution. It is not methodologically sound to combine the largest charge from one design with the shortest distance from another design as though they formed a physical source-receptor pair, unless the report labels the result as a deliberately conservative envelope rather than as the scaled distance of an actual blast design. If delay schedules overlap at the receptor, the charge-window calculation should be checked for each individual design and, where timing information supports it, for the combined event across the same stated rolling window. [@Ref007][@Ref008]\n","numLines":32,"startLine":1,"totalLines":32}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YUnJ6mhAaQF5s1kHBbA2yV","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t### SLOT 1: Authoritative EMD/EEMD Filtering References\n2\t\n3\tThe most authoritative starting point is Huang et al. (1998), because it introduced empirical mode decomposition as the decomposition stage of the Hilbert-Huang transform for nonlinear and non-stationary records and established the intrinsic mode function and residual reconstruction framework used by later filtering studies. This source supports EMD as an adaptive decomposition of a measured record, but it does not by itself justify treating any retained intrinsic mode function as a uniquely physical engineering component. [@Ref001] The central EEMD reference is Wu and Huang (2009), because it defines ensemble empirical mode decomposition as a noise-assisted method in which finite-amplitude white noise is added over repeated trials and ensemble averaging is used to reduce mode mixing and obtain a scale-organized decomposition. [@Ref002] Flandrin, Rilling, and Goncalves (2004) and Wu and Huang (2004) are also authoritative for filtering claims because they connect EMD behavior to filter-bank-like and noise-scale behavior, which is the main basis for describing IMFs as adaptive scale bands rather than preassigned physical modes. [@Ref003][@Ref004]\n4\t\n5\tFor engineering filtering rather than basic decomposition, Boudraa and Cexus (2007) and Kopsinis and McLaughlin (2009) are more directly relevant because they treat EMD as a signal filtering or denoising tool and address component selection or thresholding in measurement and signal-processing contexts. [@Ref005][@Ref006] Huang and Wu (2008) is a useful review source because it summarizes Hilbert-Huang transform applications in geophysical records and discusses open problems, making it appropriate for limitation language in an engineering report. [@Ref007] For transient acceleration histories, the EMD/EEMD sources are best paired with strong-motion processing references such as Boore and Bommer (2005), Boore (2005), and Boore and Akkar (2003), because those papers document the consequences of filtering, baseline correction, and filter choices on accelerograms and response spectra. [@Ref008][@Ref009][@Ref010]\n6\t\n7\t### SLOT 2: Supported Component Removal Practices\n8\t\n9\tThe literature supports component removal only as a documented filtering or detrending operation followed by reconstruction, not as automatic evidence that the removed or retained IMFs are physically unique blast-wave components. EMD/EEMD decompositions are adaptive and reconstructive, so a processed history can be formed by summing selected IMFs and, when appropriate, a residual term; however, the selection criterion has to be stated as an engineering or signal-processing rule, such as removal of components dominated by high-frequency noise, removal of a low-frequency trend, or retention of components inside a target response band. [@Ref001][@Ref002][@Ref003] A defensible report therefore presents IMF removal as scale-selective filtering based on evidence from the record, not as a decomposition into uniquely named source mechanisms. [@Ref007]\n10\t\n11\tRemoval of early, high-frequency IMFs is supported when those IMFs are shown to be dominated by measurement noise or by frequencies outside the engineering response range of interest. Boudraa and Cexus support EMD-based signal filtering, and Kopsinis and McLaughlin support denoising approaches that treat IMF content with thresholding logic rather than a blanket rule that the first IMF is always noise. [@Ref005][@Ref006] Removal of a residual or trend term can be defensible when the residual represents baseline drift or nonphysical low-frequency contamination, but the same operation can also remove real long-period motion; in acceleration-history applications it therefore has to be treated as a high-pass or trend-removal choice whose consequences are checked in integrated velocity, displacement, and response spectra. [@Ref008][@Ref009][@Ref010]\n12\t\n13\tSelection of nonadjacent or middle IMFs is also supported only when the selection is tied to a stated diagnostic, such as frequency content, energy contribution, correlation with the target signal, response-spectral relevance, or comparison with independent instrumentation or physics. Rilling and Flandrin show that EMD separation of nearby frequency content depends on signal conditions, so component selection cannot be assumed to isolate every true modal contribution in a short transient record. [@Ref011] EEMD reduces mode mixing by adding noise-assisted ensemble averaging, but the method still depends on ensemble size, added-noise amplitude, sifting choices, and endpoint treatment, so retained IMF groups are better described as selected scale bands than as verified physical modes. [@Ref002][@Ref007]\n14\t\n15\t### SLOT 3: Known Limitations To Report\n16\t\n17\tMode mixing is a primary limitation to report. EEMD was proposed specifically to reduce the tendency of EMD to place disparate scales in the same IMF or split a similar scale across several IMFs, but its remedy uses added white noise and ensemble averaging rather than a deterministic physical separation. [@Ref002] This means that the reported processing has to identify the added-noise level, ensemble size, sifting or stopping criterion, randomization policy when relevant, and any post-processing or alignment method used to compare IMFs across trials. [@Ref002][@Ref007]\n18\t\n19\tEnd effects, non-uniqueness, and parameter sensitivity are also material limitations. EMD envelopes are built from local extrema, so behavior near the beginning and end of a short record can be sensitive to interpolation and boundary extension choices; this is especially important for transient vibration histories in which the main pulse, decay tail, or baseline segment may occur near a boundary. [@Ref001][@Ref007] The decomposition is not a fixed orthogonal transform with a predeclared basis, and different sifting, stopping, noise, and endpoint choices can change the IMF set; therefore, the same measured history can yield different but plausible component partitions. [@Ref003][@Ref002]\n20\t\n21\tLoss of physical interpretability is a limitation whenever IMFs are used for report-level engineering claims. EMD and EEMD components are derived from local time-scale behavior, so the existence of an IMF group does not by itself demonstrate a separate blast source, wave path, soil layer response, sensor artifact, or structural mode. [@Ref001][@Ref011] Filtering can also alter peak values, apparent pulse duration, low-frequency drift, and response demands; those consequences are well documented in strong-motion filtering and baseline processing literature and are relevant when processed acceleration histories are used downstream. [@Ref008][@Ref010]\n22\t\n23\t### SLOT 4: Validation Checks For EEMD-Filtered Histories\n24\t\n25\tValidation of an EEMD-filtered acceleration history is strongest when it compares the raw record, the retained reconstruction, and the removed residual content in both time and frequency domains. Peak preservation checks include the signed and absolute peak acceleration, peak timing, pulse polarity, and local pulse shape near the peak, because a filter that improves visual smoothness can still alter the controlling demand in a transient history. [@Ref008][@Ref009] Baseline and drift checks include numerical integration to velocity and displacement, comparison of terminal velocity and displacement trends, and review of any low-frequency residual removed from the reconstruction. [@Ref008]\n26\t\n27\tEnergy and frequency-content checks are defensible because EMD filtering is scale-selective. The report can compare root-mean-square acceleration, summed squared acceleration, cumulative signal energy, Fourier or power spectral density estimates, Hilbert marginal spectra when used, and the frequency content of removed IMFs to confirm that the operation removed the intended scale range rather than suppressing engineering demand. [@Ref005][@Ref006][@Ref004] Response-spectrum checks are important when the processed acceleration is used as an engineering input, because response spectra can be sensitive to filtering choices and the difference may be consequential even when the acceleration trace appears acceptable. [@Ref010][@Ref008]\n28\t\n29\tRepeatability and visual diagnostics are also necessary for EEMD-filtered histories. Because EEMD depends on added-noise amplitude and ensemble averaging, the filtered result can be checked by rerunning the decomposition with the same documented parameters and, where relevant, with alternative random seeds or nearby ensemble settings to confirm that retained scale bands and validation metrics are stable. [@Ref002][@Ref007] Visual review includes overlaid raw and filtered acceleration traces, removed-component traces, endpoint behavior, pre-event or post-event baseline segments when available, and plots of cumulative residuals or integrated histories so that the report shows whether the reconstruction preserves the features that control engineering response. [@Ref008][@Ref009]\n30\t\n31\t### SLOT 5: Description Without Overclaiming\n32\t\n33\tEEMD filtering is best described as an adaptive, data-driven filtering and reconstruction procedure applied to a measured vibration history. A conservative description states that selected IMFs were retained or removed according to documented engineering criteria, and that the resulting reconstruction was accepted only after checks on peak response, energy, frequency content, baseline behavior, and response spectra. [@Ref002][@Ref005][@Ref008] The description avoids stating that retained modes are physically unique blast-wave components unless separate evidence from instrumentation, site physics, wave-propagation modeling, or independent measurements supports that interpretation. [@Ref001][@Ref011]\n34\t\n35\tAppropriate terminology includes \"retained IMF group,\" \"removed scale band,\" \"EEMD-filtered reconstruction,\" \"adaptive denoising,\" and \"trend or residual removal,\" because those terms describe the processing operation without asserting a source mechanism. [@Ref003][@Ref006] Terms such as \"true blast component,\" \"physical mode,\" or \"isolated wave type\" require additional support and are not justified by the decomposition alone, because EMD separation depends on signal conditions and EEMD depends on noise-assisted ensemble choices. [@Ref011][@Ref002]\n36\t\n37\tA publication-ready limitation statement can say that EEMD was used to reduce unwanted scale content while preserving defined engineering measures, and that the filtered record is a processed representation of the measured history rather than a recovered unique physical waveform. [@Ref005][@Ref008] The report can further state that conclusions based on the filtered history are conditional on the documented EEMD parameters, component-selection rule, and validation results, and that raw and filtered histories remain distinguishable in the record archive and subsequent calculations. [@Ref002][@Ref007]\n38\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"193e9b03-f0e0-48bb-82cd-24114a1d1cbc","uuid":"c2d0bc72-0c1d-4a85-a6bc-fac8839f4c37","timestamp":"2026-07-06T19:41:13.656Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/averrik/Cloud/github/projects/AR-S2J2J.R/TITO/kb/T3.compile.research.md","content":"### SLOT 1: Authoritative EMD/EEMD Filtering References\n\nThe most authoritative starting point is Huang et al. (1998), because it introduced empirical mode decomposition as the decomposition stage of the Hilbert-Huang transform for nonlinear and non-stationary records and established the intrinsic mode function and residual reconstruction framework used by later filtering studies. This source supports EMD as an adaptive decomposition of a measured record, but it does not by itself justify treating any retained intrinsic mode function as a uniquely physical engineering component. [@Ref001] The central EEMD reference is Wu and Huang (2009), because it defines ensemble empirical mode decomposition as a noise-assisted method in which finite-amplitude white noise is added over repeated trials and ensemble averaging is used to reduce mode mixing and obtain a scale-organized decomposition. [@Ref002] Flandrin, Rilling, and Goncalves (2004) and Wu and Huang (2004) are also authoritative for filtering claims because they connect EMD behavior to filter-bank-like and noise-scale behavior, which is the main basis for describing IMFs as adaptive scale bands rather than preassigned physical modes. [@Ref003][@Ref004]\n\nFor engineering filtering rather than basic decomposition, Boudraa and Cexus (2007) and Kopsinis and McLaughlin (2009) are more directly relevant because they treat EMD as a signal filtering or denoising tool and address component selection or thresholding in measurement and signal-processing contexts. [@Ref005][@Ref006] Huang and Wu (2008) is a useful review source because it summarizes Hilbert-Huang transform applications in geophysical records and discusses open problems, making it appropriate for limitation language in an engineering report. [@Ref007] For transient acceleration histories, the EMD/EEMD sources are best paired with strong-motion processing references such as Boore and Bommer (2005), Boore (2005), and Boore and Akkar (2003), because those papers document the consequences of filtering, baseline correction, and filter choices on accelerograms and response spectra. [@Ref008][@Ref009][@Ref010]\n\n### SLOT 2: Supported Component Removal Practices\n\nThe literature supports component removal only as a documented filtering or detrending operation followed by reconstruction, not as automatic evidence that the removed or retained IMFs are physically unique blast-wave components. EMD/EEMD decompositions are adaptive and reconstructive, so a processed history can be formed by summing selected IMFs and, when appropriate, a residual term; however, the selection criterion has to be stated as an engineering or signal-processing rule, such as removal of components dominated by high-frequency noise, removal of a low-frequency trend, or retention of components inside a target response band. [@Ref001][@Ref002][@Ref003] A defensible report therefore presents IMF removal as scale-selective filtering based on evidence from the record, not as a decomposition into uniquely named source mechanisms. [@Ref007]\n\nRemoval of early, high-frequency IMFs is supported when those IMFs are shown to be dominated by measurement noise or by frequencies outside the engineering response range of interest. Boudraa and Cexus support EMD-based signal filtering, and Kopsinis and McLaughlin support denoising approaches that treat IMF content with thresholding logic rather than a blanket rule that the first IMF is always noise. [@Ref005][@Ref006] Removal of a residual or trend term can be defensible when the residual represents baseline drift or nonphysical low-frequency contamination, but the same operation can also remove real long-period motion; in acceleration-history applications it therefore has to be treated as a high-pass or trend-removal choice whose consequences are checked in integrated velocity, displacement, and response spectra. [@Ref008][@Ref009][@Ref010]\n\nSelection of nonadjacent or middle IMFs is also supported only when the selection is tied to a stated diagnostic, such as frequency content, energy contribution, correlation with the target signal, response-spectral relevance, or comparison with independent instrumentation or physics. Rilling and Flandrin show that EMD separation of nearby frequency content depends on signal conditions, so component selection cannot be assumed to isolate every true modal contribution in a short transient record. [@Ref011] EEMD reduces mode mixing by adding noise-assisted ensemble averaging, but the method still depends on ensemble size, added-noise amplitude, sifting choices, and endpoint treatment, so retained IMF groups are better described as selected scale bands than as verified physical modes. [@Ref002][@Ref007]\n\n### SLOT 3: Known Limitations To Report\n\nMode mixing is a primary limitation to report. EEMD was proposed specifically to reduce the tendency of EMD to place disparate scales in the same IMF or split a similar scale across several IMFs, but its remedy uses added white noise and ensemble averaging rather than a deterministic physical separation. [@Ref002] This means that the reported processing has to identify the added-noise level, ensemble size, sifting or stopping criterion, randomization policy when relevant, and any post-processing or alignment method used to compare IMFs across trials. [@Ref002][@Ref007]\n\nEnd effects, non-uniqueness, and parameter sensitivity are also material limitations. EMD envelopes are built from local extrema, so behavior near the beginning and end of a short record can be sensitive to interpolation and boundary extension choices; this is especially important for transient vibration histories in which the main pulse, decay tail, or baseline segment may occur near a boundary. [@Ref001][@Ref007] The decomposition is not a fixed orthogonal transform with a predeclared basis, and different sifting, stopping, noise, and endpoint choices can change the IMF set; therefore, the same measured history can yield different but plausible component partitions. [@Ref003][@Ref002]\n\nLoss of physical interpretability is a limitation whenever IMFs are used for report-level engineering claims. EMD and EEMD components are derived from local time-scale behavior, so the existence of an IMF group does not by itself demonstrate a separate blast source, wave path, soil layer response, sensor artifact, or structural mode. [@Ref001][@Ref011] Filtering can also alter peak values, apparent pulse duration, low-frequency drift, and response demands; those consequences are well documented in strong-motion filtering and baseline processing literature and are relevant when processed acceleration histories are used downstream. [@Ref008][@Ref010]\n\n### SLOT 4: Validation Checks For EEMD-Filtered Histories\n\nValidation of an EEMD-filtered acceleration history is strongest when it compares the raw record, the retained reconstruction, and the removed residual content in both time and frequency domains. Peak preservation checks include the signed and absolute peak acceleration, peak timing, pulse polarity, and local pulse shape near the peak, because a filter that improves visual smoothness can still alter the controlling demand in a transient history. [@Ref008][@Ref009] Baseline and drift checks include numerical integration to velocity and displacement, comparison of terminal velocity and displacement trends, and review of any low-frequency residual removed from the reconstruction. [@Ref008]\n\nEnergy and frequency-content checks are defensible because EMD filtering is scale-selective. The report can compare root-mean-square acceleration, summed squared acceleration, cumulative signal energy, Fourier or power spectral density estimates, Hilbert marginal spectra when used, and the frequency content of removed IMFs to confirm that the operation removed the intended scale range rather than suppressing engineering demand. [@Ref005][@Ref006][@Ref004] Response-spectrum checks are important when the processed acceleration is used as an engineering input, because response spectra can be sensitive to filtering choices and the difference may be consequential even when the acceleration trace appears acceptable. [@Ref010][@Ref008]\n\nRepeatability and visual diagnostics are also necessary for EEMD-filtered histories. Because EEMD depends on added-noise amplitude and ensemble averaging, the filtered result can be checked by rerunning the decomposition with the same documented parameters and, where relevant, with alternative random seeds or nearby ensemble settings to confirm that retained scale bands and validation metrics are stable. [@Ref002][@Ref007] Visual review includes overlaid raw and filtered acceleration traces, removed-component traces, endpoint behavior, pre-event or post-event baseline segments when available, and plots of cumulative residuals or integrated histories so that the report shows whether the reconstruction preserves the features that control engineering response. [@Ref008][@Ref009]\n\n### SLOT 5: Description Without Overclaiming\n\nEEMD filtering is best described as an adaptive, data-driven filtering and reconstruction procedure applied to a measured vibration history. A conservative description states that selected IMFs were retained or removed according to documented engineering criteria, and that the resulting reconstruction was accepted only after checks on peak response, energy, frequency content, baseline behavior, and response spectra. [@Ref002][@Ref005][@Ref008] The description avoids stating that retained modes are physically unique blast-wave components unless separate evidence from instrumentation, site physics, wave-propagation modeling, or independent measurements supports that interpretation. [@Ref001][@Ref011]\n\nAppropriate terminology includes \"retained IMF group,\" \"removed scale band,\" \"EEMD-filtered reconstruction,\" \"adaptive denoising,\" and \"trend or residual removal,\" because those terms describe the processing operation without asserting a source mechanism. [@Ref003][@Ref006] Terms such as \"true blast component,\" \"physical mode,\" or \"isolated wave type\" require additional support and are not justified by the decomposition alone, because EMD separation depends on signal conditions and EEMD depends on noise-assisted ensemble choices. [@Ref011][@Ref002]\n\nA publication-ready limitation statement can say that EEMD was used to reduce unwanted scale content while preserving defined engineering measures, and that the filtered record is a processed representation of the measured history rather than a recovered unique physical waveform. [@Ref005][@Ref008] The report can further state that conclusions based on the filtered history are conditional on the documented EEMD parameters, component-selection rule, and validation results, and that raw and filtered histories remain distinguishable in the record archive and subsequent calculations. [@Ref002][@Ref007]\n","numLines":38,"startLine":1,"totalLines":38}}}
