{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AFBHyPdGPvxiiQxxhinJH1","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"ANCOLD guidelines seismic design tailings storage facility dams 2019\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Ancold - Guidelines On Tailings Dam | PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.scribd.com/document/581384623/Ancold-guidelines-on-Tailings-Dam\"},{\"title\":\"Guidelines on Tailings Dams-Planning, Design, Construction, Operation and Closure-REVISION 1 (2019) - HARDCOPY - ANCOLD\",\"url\":\"https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/\"},{\"title\":\"ANCOLD Rev 1 Guidelines On Tailings Dam (Planning, Design, Construction, Operation and Closure, 2019) | PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.scribd.com/document/884155994/ANCOLD-Rev-1-Guidelines-on-Tailings-Dam-Planning-Design-Construction-Operation-and-Closure-2019\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Guidelines and recommendations on minimum factors of safety for slope stability of tailings dams\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/347741840_Guidelines_and_recommendations_on_minimum_factors_of_safety_for_slope_stability_of_tailings_dams\"},{\"title\":\"ESG briefing: Tailings dams Escondida June 2019\",\"url\":\"https://www.bhp.com/-/media/documents/media/reports-and-presentations/2019/190607_esgbriefingtailingsdams.pdf?la=en\"},{\"title\":\"AUSTRALIAN NATIONAL COMMITTEE ON LARGE DAMS GUIDELINES ON TAILINGS DAMS\",\"url\":\"https://www.ancold.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Tailings-Guideline-Addendum-July-2019_v2.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Stability assessment of a tailings storage facility using a non-local   constitutive model accounting for anisotropic strain-softening\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2010.03128\"},{\"title\":\"Screening of seismic records to perform time-history dynamic analyses of   tailings dams: a power-spectral based approach\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2009.06426\"}]\n\nI've completed the web search for \"ANCOLD guidelines seismic design tailings storage facility dams 2019\". Here are the key findings:\n\nANCOLD published the Guidelines on Tailings Dams in May 2012, and Revision 1, dated July 2019, has added clarifying information to make the Tailings Guideline compatible with current design loadings for earthquake as detailed in the ANCOLD Guidelines for Design of Dams and Appurtenant Structures for Earthquake (2019).\n\nSome of the important matters covered in this revision include reinforcement of the need for robust management practices, updates on earthquake considerations to align with the ANCOLD Guidelines for Design of Dams and Appurtenant Structures for Earthquake (2019) and additional information on static liquefaction.\n\nSince 2012, there have been a number of tragic tailings dam failures which have attracted world-wide attention. This revision is ANCOLD's initial response to these events and other relevant matters.\n\nThe search results indicate that the 2019 revision emphasized the importance of aligning tailings dam seismic design criteria with the updated ANCOLD earthquake design guidelines, and included enhanced focus on management practices and static liquefaction considerations for tailings storage facilities.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"193e9b03-f0e0-48bb-82cd-24114a1d1cbc","uuid":"ecf5ec97-c0bf-41c4-87aa-347cc0ffe50f","timestamp":"2026-07-06T19:42:09.632Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"ANCOLD guidelines seismic design tailings storage facility dams 2019","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01FWKDHwe3Y1gv5TBZ4v9gam","content":[{"title":"Ancold - Guidelines On Tailings Dam | PDF","url":"https://www.scribd.com/document/581384623/Ancold-guidelines-on-Tailings-Dam"},{"title":"Guidelines on Tailings Dams-Planning, Design, Construction, Operation and Closure-REVISION 1 (2019) - HARDCOPY - ANCOLD","url":"https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/"},{"title":"ANCOLD Rev 1 Guidelines On Tailings Dam (Planning, Design, Construction, Operation and Closure, 2019) | PDF","url":"https://www.scribd.com/document/884155994/ANCOLD-Rev-1-Guidelines-on-Tailings-Dam-Planning-Design-Construction-Operation-and-Closure-2019"},{"title":"(PDF) Guidelines and recommendations on minimum factors of safety for slope stability of tailings dams","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/347741840_Guidelines_and_recommendations_on_minimum_factors_of_safety_for_slope_stability_of_tailings_dams"},{"title":"ESG briefing: Tailings dams Escondida June 2019","url":"https://www.bhp.com/-/media/documents/media/reports-and-presentations/2019/190607_esgbriefingtailingsdams.pdf?la=en"},{"title":"AUSTRALIAN NATIONAL COMMITTEE ON LARGE DAMS GUIDELINES ON TAILINGS DAMS","url":"https://www.ancold.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Tailings-Guideline-Addendum-July-2019_v2.pdf"},{"title":"Stability assessment of a tailings storage facility using a non-local   constitutive model accounting for anisotropic strain-softening","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2010.03128"},{"title":"Screening of seismic records to perform time-history dynamic analyses of   tailings dams: a power-spectral based approach","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2009.06426"}]},"I've completed the web search for \"ANCOLD guidelines seismic design tailings storage facility dams 2019\". Here are the key findings:\n\nANCOLD published the Guidelines on Tailings Dams in May 2012, and Revision 1, dated July 2019, has added clarifying information to make the Tailings Guideline compatible with current design loadings for earthquake as detailed in the ANCOLD Guidelines for Design of Dams and Appurtenant Structures for Earthquake (2019).\n\nSome of the important matters covered in this revision include reinforcement of the need for robust management practices, updates on earthquake considerations to align with the ANCOLD Guidelines for Design of Dams and Appurtenant Structures for Earthquake (2019) and additional information on static liquefaction.\n\nSince 2012, there have been a number of tragic tailings dam failures which have attracted world-wide attention. This revision is ANCOLD's initial response to these events and other relevant matters.\n\nThe search results indicate that the 2019 revision emphasized the importance of aligning tailings dam seismic design criteria with the updated ANCOLD earthquake design guidelines, and included enhanced focus on management practices and static liquefaction considerations for tailings storage facilities."],"durationSeconds":9.517567958000006,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012jBGwd3xQ5iUG59NswGRdY","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"ICOLD bulletin tailings dams seismic liquefaction assessment\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"TAILINGS LIQUEFACTION AND DAM STABILITY\",\"url\":\"https://remunom.ojsbr.com/multidisciplinar/article/download/5120/4838/18955\"},{\"title\":\"CDA technical bulletin on tailings dam breach analyses V. Martin\",\"url\":\"https://www.knightpiesold.com/sites/en/assets/File/ICOLD%202019%20-%20CDA%20Tailings%20Dam%20Breach.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Icoldchile\",\"url\":\"https://www.icoldchile.cl/boletines/121.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"TAILINGS LIQUEFACTION AND DAM STABILITY: ADVANCES IN DIAGNOSIS, MONITORING, STANDARDS AND MITIGATION – A CRITICAL REVIEW | REMUNOM\",\"url\":\"https://remunom.ojsbr.com/multidisciplinar/article/view/5120\"},{\"title\":\"A Review of Tailings Dam Safety Monitoring Guidelines ...\",\"url\":\"https://stacks.cdc.gov/view/cdc/208183/cdc_208183_DS1.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"ICOLD Bulletin No. 194 TAILINGS DAM SAFETY\",\"url\":\"https://unece.org/sites/default/files/2023-04/S4-1Andy%20Small_ENG.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Seismic safety evaluation of existing dams M. Wieland\",\"url\":\"https://www.cbip.org/ISRM-2022/ICOLD2021/Data/Workshop/Seismic%20Analysis%20for%20Dam%20Design%20and%20Safety%20Evaluation%20of%20Existing%20Dams/Paper%20Wieland%20seismic%20workshop%20existing%20dams%20rev%20final%202119-12-2020-16-43-44.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"A high-fidelity seismic intensity measure to assess dynamic liquefaction   in tailings\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2204.11393\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) ICOLD's Revised Seismic Design and Performance Criteria for Large Storage Dams\",\"url\":\"https://www.academia.edu/109887424/ICOLDs_Revised_Seismic_Design_and_Performance_Criteria_for_Large_Storage_Dams\"},{\"title\":\"Modeling liquefaction-induced runout of a tailings dam using a hybrid   finite element and material point method approach\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2404.15860\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here are the key findings related to ICOLD bulletin guidance on tailings dams and seismic liquefaction assessment:\n\n## ICOLD Bulletin 194 and Tailings Dam Safety\n\nICOLD Bulletin 194 unified guidance on governance and engineering practices, emphasizing the need for consistent, auditable procedures related to tailings dam safety. This bulletin, published in 2022, addresses tailings dam safety governance.\n\n## Seismic and Liquefaction Assessment\n\nCyclic triggers remain important in seismically active areas, as earthquakes cause repeated loading that can create excess pore pressures in tailings with low permeability or limited dilatancy, potentially leading to cyclic liquefaction.\n\n## Historical ICOLD Bulletins on Related Topics\n\nICOLD has issued several specialized bulletins on tailings dams and seismic considerations, including Bulletin 98: Tailings Dams and Seismicity (1995) and Bulletin 104: Monitoring of Tailings Dams (1996).\n\n## Recent Advances\n\nProgress in CPTu/SCPTu interpretation, shear-wave velocity–based correlations and multi-parameter diagnostic frameworks has strengthened field evaluation for assessing liquefaction susceptibility in tailings.\n\nThe search results indicate that ICOLD bulletins provide authoritative guidance on tailings dam assessment, including specific considerations for seismic liquefaction, though the most current comprehensive guidance is found in Bulletin 194 (2022).\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"193e9b03-f0e0-48bb-82cd-24114a1d1cbc","uuid":"3078ad3f-a02a-4488-ba0d-ad3d032047eb","timestamp":"2026-07-06T19:42:10.318Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"ICOLD bulletin tailings dams seismic liquefaction assessment","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01L7pi8ctbypdzEjqABuqeZM","content":[{"title":"TAILINGS LIQUEFACTION AND DAM STABILITY","url":"https://remunom.ojsbr.com/multidisciplinar/article/download/5120/4838/18955"},{"title":"CDA technical bulletin on tailings dam breach analyses V. Martin","url":"https://www.knightpiesold.com/sites/en/assets/File/ICOLD%202019%20-%20CDA%20Tailings%20Dam%20Breach.pdf"},{"title":"Icoldchile","url":"https://www.icoldchile.cl/boletines/121.pdf"},{"title":"TAILINGS LIQUEFACTION AND DAM STABILITY: ADVANCES IN DIAGNOSIS, MONITORING, STANDARDS AND MITIGATION – A CRITICAL REVIEW | REMUNOM","url":"https://remunom.ojsbr.com/multidisciplinar/article/view/5120"},{"title":"A Review of Tailings Dam Safety Monitoring Guidelines ...","url":"https://stacks.cdc.gov/view/cdc/208183/cdc_208183_DS1.pdf"},{"title":"ICOLD Bulletin No. 194 TAILINGS DAM SAFETY","url":"https://unece.org/sites/default/files/2023-04/S4-1Andy%20Small_ENG.pdf"},{"title":"Seismic safety evaluation of existing dams M. Wieland","url":"https://www.cbip.org/ISRM-2022/ICOLD2021/Data/Workshop/Seismic%20Analysis%20for%20Dam%20Design%20and%20Safety%20Evaluation%20of%20Existing%20Dams/Paper%20Wieland%20seismic%20workshop%20existing%20dams%20rev%20final%202119-12-2020-16-43-44.pdf"},{"title":"A high-fidelity seismic intensity measure to assess dynamic liquefaction   in tailings","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2204.11393"},{"title":"(PDF) ICOLD's Revised Seismic Design and Performance Criteria for Large Storage Dams","url":"https://www.academia.edu/109887424/ICOLDs_Revised_Seismic_Design_and_Performance_Criteria_for_Large_Storage_Dams"},{"title":"Modeling liquefaction-induced runout of a tailings dam using a hybrid   finite element and material point method approach","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2404.15860"}]},"Based on the search results, here are the key findings related to ICOLD bulletin guidance on tailings dams and seismic liquefaction assessment:\n\n## ICOLD Bulletin 194 and Tailings Dam Safety\n\nICOLD Bulletin 194 unified guidance on governance and engineering practices, emphasizing the need for consistent, auditable procedures related to tailings dam safety. This bulletin, published in 2022, addresses tailings dam safety governance.\n\n## Seismic and Liquefaction Assessment\n\nCyclic triggers remain important in seismically active areas, as earthquakes cause repeated loading that can create excess pore pressures in tailings with low permeability or limited dilatancy, potentially leading to cyclic liquefaction.\n\n## Historical ICOLD Bulletins on Related Topics\n\nICOLD has issued several specialized bulletins on tailings dams and seismic considerations, including Bulletin 98: Tailings Dams and Seismicity (1995) and Bulletin 104: Monitoring of Tailings Dams (1996).\n\n## Recent Advances\n\nProgress in CPTu/SCPTu interpretation, shear-wave velocity–based correlations and multi-parameter diagnostic frameworks has strengthened field evaluation for assessing liquefaction susceptibility in tailings.\n\nThe search results indicate that ICOLD bulletins provide authoritative guidance on tailings dam assessment, including specific considerations for seismic liquefaction, though the most current comprehensive guidance is found in Bulletin 194 (2022)."],"durationSeconds":8.723792292000006,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016pMaGcgmADd5xAcoFZRN64","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"GISTM Global Industry Standard Tailings Management blasting vibration dynamic loading\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Practical steps to Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management (GISTM) compliance for operational tailings storage facilities in South Africa\",\"url\":\"https://scielo.org.za/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S2225-62532022000600006\"},{\"title\":\"ICMM - Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management\",\"url\":\"https://www.icmm.com/en-gb/our-principles/tailings/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management\"},{\"title\":\"Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management\",\"url\":\"https://www.teck.com/sustainability/sustainability-topics/tailings-management/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management/\"},{\"title\":\"Understanding the Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management (GISTM): A Complete Overview\",\"url\":\"https://blog.gistm.net/blog/a1-understanding-gistm-complete-overview/\"},{\"title\":\"Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management (GISTM)\",\"url\":\"https://www.goldfields.com/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management-gistm.php\"},{\"title\":\"Mine-tailings standards - Barr Engineering\",\"url\":\"https://www.barr.com/insights/navigating-the-expanding-landscape-of-mine-tailings-standards/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found information about the Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management (GISTM), though the specific details about blasting vibration and dynamic loading weren't directly addressed in these results.\n\n## Overview of GISTM\n\nThe Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management (the Standard) strives to achieve the ultimate goal of zero harm to people and the environment. Developed after the Brumadinho tailings dam disaster in Brazil (2019), which claimed 270 lives and caused immense environmental damage, the GISTM sets out to ensure that zero harm to people and the environment becomes the universal goal for every tailings facility on Earth.\n\nComprising six topic areas, 15 principles and 77 auditable requirements, the Standard will ultimately be supported by implementation protocols that will provide detailed guidance for certification, or assurance as applicable, and for equivalence with other standards.\n\n## Monitoring and Technical Aspects\n\nThe search results do mention monitoring practices relevant to tailings facilities, including installation of vibrating wire piezometers at critical areas for measuring pore pressure, but the specific information about blasting vibrations and dynamic loading requirements under GISTM was not found in these results. \n\nFor more detailed information about how GISTM addresses dynamic loading and blasting vibrations specifically, you may want to consult the official GISTM documentation or implementation protocols directly.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"193e9b03-f0e0-48bb-82cd-24114a1d1cbc","uuid":"688d765f-77fa-4c52-a05d-bb79dba12c19","timestamp":"2026-07-06T19:42:11.408Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"GISTM Global Industry Standard Tailings Management blasting vibration dynamic loading","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01YZ9P4PosAZQTLgroMGE1dg","content":[{"title":"Practical steps to Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management (GISTM) compliance for operational tailings storage facilities in South Africa","url":"https://scielo.org.za/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S2225-62532022000600006"},{"title":"ICMM - Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management","url":"https://www.icmm.com/en-gb/our-principles/tailings/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management"},{"title":"Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management","url":"https://www.teck.com/sustainability/sustainability-topics/tailings-management/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management/"},{"title":"Understanding the Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management (GISTM): A Complete Overview","url":"https://blog.gistm.net/blog/a1-understanding-gistm-complete-overview/"},{"title":"Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management (GISTM)","url":"https://www.goldfields.com/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management-gistm.php"},{"title":"Mine-tailings standards - Barr Engineering","url":"https://www.barr.com/insights/navigating-the-expanding-landscape-of-mine-tailings-standards/"}]},"Based on the search results, I found information about the Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management (GISTM), though the specific details about blasting vibration and dynamic loading weren't directly addressed in these results.\n\n## Overview of GISTM\n\nThe Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management (the Standard) strives to achieve the ultimate goal of zero harm to people and the environment. Developed after the Brumadinho tailings dam disaster in Brazil (2019), which claimed 270 lives and caused immense environmental damage, the GISTM sets out to ensure that zero harm to people and the environment becomes the universal goal for every tailings facility on Earth.\n\nComprising six topic areas, 15 principles and 77 auditable requirements, the Standard will ultimately be supported by implementation protocols that will provide detailed guidance for certification, or assurance as applicable, and for equivalence with other standards.\n\n## Monitoring and Technical Aspects\n\nThe search results do mention monitoring practices relevant to tailings facilities, including installation of vibrating wire piezometers at critical areas for measuring pore pressure, but the specific information about blasting vibrations and dynamic loading requirements under GISTM was not found in these results. \n\nFor more detailed information about how GISTM addresses dynamic loading and blasting vibrations specifically, you may want to consult the official GISTM documentation or implementation protocols directly."],"durationSeconds":10.662209625000003,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014VidYjP1matPjpH4oTaBnY","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"IFC EHS guidelines mining tailings blasting vibration pore pressure liquefaction\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Consolidated ESMS Project Standards\",\"url\":\"https://www.eib.org/attachments/registers/79810686.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"OSMRE National Technology Transfer Team (NTTT) Applied Science Final Report\",\"url\":\"https://www.osmre.gov/sites/default/files/asp-files/S16AC20074-FinalReport.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Mining & Milling Underground [Guidelines]\",\"url\":\"https://www.ifc.org/content/dam/ifc/doc/1990/mining-underground.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"of blasting on pore pressure\",\"url\":\"https://files.dep.state.pa.us/Mining/BureauOfMiningPrograms/BMPPortalFiles/Blasting_Research_Papers/Slope%20Stability/BoR%20Blasting%20and%20dams.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"2007-construction-materials-extraction-ehs-guidelines-en. ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.ifc.org/content/dam/ifc/doc/2000/2007-construction-materials-extraction-ehs-guidelines-en.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"EHS Guidelines: Oil, Gas, and Mining\",\"url\":\"https://www.ifc.org/en/insights-reports/general-environmental-health-and-safety-guidelines/ehs-guidelines-oil-gas-mining\"},{\"title\":\"Development law and growth model of dynamic pore water pressure of tailings under different consolidation conditions\",\"url\":\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9621461/\"},{\"title\":\"Screening of seismic records to perform time-history dynamic analyses of   tailings dams: a power-spectral based approach\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2009.06426\"},{\"title\":\"Environmental, Health, and Safety Guidelines MINING DECEMBER 10, 2007\",\"url\":\"https://www.ifc.org/content/dam/ifc/doc/2000/2007-mining-ehs-guidelines-en.pdf\"}]\n\nI've completed the web search for your query on IFC EHS guidelines related to mining tailings, blasting vibration, pore pressure, and liquefaction. Here are the key findings:\n\n## IFC EHS Guidelines on Mining and Tailings\n\nThe EHS Guidelines for Mining are applicable to underground and open-pit mining, alluvial mining, solution mining (brines), and dredging.\n\n### Tailings Management Requirements\n\nOn-land tailings impoundment systems must be designed and constructed in accordance with internationally recognized engineering practices, local seismic conditions, and precipitation conditions.\n\nStructures such as waste dumps, tailing impoundments/dams, and containment facilities should be planned, designed, and operated such that geotechnical risks and environmental impacts are appropriately assessed and managed throughout the entire mine cycle.\n\n### Blasting Operations\n\nAll blasting operations must be carried out only by qualified and certified personnel. Additionally, specific warning devices (e.g. horn signals and flashing lights) and procedures should be implemented before each blasting activity to alert all workers and third parties in the surrounding areas.\n\n### Technical Aspects of Blasting Effects\n\nResearch shows that peak particle velocity (PPV) is often used to determine the blast loading imposed on infrastructures and to evaluate the excess pore water pressure ratio. Furthermore, blast-induced liquefaction has received substantial attention.\n\nThe search results provide access to the official IFC Mining EHS Guidelines documents from 2007, which contain detailed guidance on these topics.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"193e9b03-f0e0-48bb-82cd-24114a1d1cbc","uuid":"d396c6d0-b138-483d-8f70-ac4997179584","timestamp":"2026-07-06T19:42:12.763Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"IFC EHS guidelines mining tailings blasting vibration pore pressure liquefaction","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01DCzdQAyrPr9R4Uy5Ku8UQL","content":[{"title":"Consolidated ESMS Project Standards","url":"https://www.eib.org/attachments/registers/79810686.pdf"},{"title":"OSMRE National Technology Transfer Team (NTTT) Applied Science Final Report","url":"https://www.osmre.gov/sites/default/files/asp-files/S16AC20074-FinalReport.pdf"},{"title":"Mining & Milling Underground [Guidelines]","url":"https://www.ifc.org/content/dam/ifc/doc/1990/mining-underground.pdf"},{"title":"of blasting on pore pressure","url":"https://files.dep.state.pa.us/Mining/BureauOfMiningPrograms/BMPPortalFiles/Blasting_Research_Papers/Slope%20Stability/BoR%20Blasting%20and%20dams.pdf"},{"title":"2007-construction-materials-extraction-ehs-guidelines-en. ...","url":"https://www.ifc.org/content/dam/ifc/doc/2000/2007-construction-materials-extraction-ehs-guidelines-en.pdf"},{"title":"EHS Guidelines: Oil, Gas, and Mining","url":"https://www.ifc.org/en/insights-reports/general-environmental-health-and-safety-guidelines/ehs-guidelines-oil-gas-mining"},{"title":"Development law and growth model of dynamic pore water pressure of tailings under different consolidation conditions","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9621461/"},{"title":"Screening of seismic records to perform time-history dynamic analyses of   tailings dams: a power-spectral based approach","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2009.06426"},{"title":"Environmental, Health, and Safety Guidelines MINING DECEMBER 10, 2007","url":"https://www.ifc.org/content/dam/ifc/doc/2000/2007-mining-ehs-guidelines-en.pdf"}]},"I've completed the web search for your query on IFC EHS guidelines related to mining tailings, blasting vibration, pore pressure, and liquefaction. Here are the key findings:\n\n## IFC EHS Guidelines on Mining and Tailings\n\nThe EHS Guidelines for Mining are applicable to underground and open-pit mining, alluvial mining, solution mining (brines), and dredging.\n\n### Tailings Management Requirements\n\nOn-land tailings impoundment systems must be designed and constructed in accordance with internationally recognized engineering practices, local seismic conditions, and precipitation conditions.\n\nStructures such as waste dumps, tailing impoundments/dams, and containment facilities should be planned, designed, and operated such that geotechnical risks and environmental impacts are appropriately assessed and managed throughout the entire mine cycle.\n\n### Blasting Operations\n\nAll blasting operations must be carried out only by qualified and certified personnel. Additionally, specific warning devices (e.g. horn signals and flashing lights) and procedures should be implemented before each blasting activity to alert all workers and third parties in the surrounding areas.\n\n### Technical Aspects of Blasting Effects\n\nResearch shows that peak particle velocity (PPV) is often used to determine the blast loading imposed on infrastructures and to evaluate the excess pore water pressure ratio. Furthermore, blast-induced liquefaction has received substantial attention.\n\nThe search results provide access to the official IFC Mining EHS Guidelines documents from 2007, which contain detailed guidance on these topics."],"durationSeconds":9.874953749999992,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015253HERxuonEjgvsxmA5bj","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"South Africa mining blasting regulation MHSA pore pressure TSF tailings\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"New South African Mining Regulations - Bishop Fraser Attorneys\",\"url\":\"https://bishopfraser.co.za/new-south-african-mining-regulations/\"},{\"title\":\"Tailings Storage Facility (TSF) Terminologies: A Practical Engineering Reference - Mining Doc\",\"url\":\"https://www.miningdoc.tech/question/tailings-storage-facility-tsf-terminologies-a-practical-engineering-reference/\"},{\"title\":\"Tailings Management System | Pan African Resources\",\"url\":\"https://www.panafricanresources.com/tailings-management-system/\"},{\"title\":\"Dust Deposition Impacts at a Liquidated Gold Mine Village: Gauteng Province in South Africa\",\"url\":\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7400412/\"},{\"title\":\"Blast Impact Assessment Final Blast Management and Consulting (PTY) LTD\",\"url\":\"https://altavandykenvironmental.co.za/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/App-C4-Blast-Impact-Assessment.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"This gazette is also available free online at www.gpwonline.co.za\",\"url\":\"https://www.gov.za/sites/default/files/gcis_document/202408/51003gon5097.pdf\"}]\n\nI've found search results related to your query. Here's what the information reveals:\n\n## South African Mining Blasting Regulations\n\nMining in South Africa is regulated under the Mine Health and Safety Act (MHSA), which mandates strict adherence to a Code of Practice (COP). The August 2, 2024 Government Gazette issued the Guideline for a Mandatory Code of Practice for Minimum Standards on Ground Vibrations, Noise, Air-blast, and Flyrock near Surface Structures and Communities to be Protected.\n\nSection 9(2) of the MHSA requires employers to develop and implement a COP addressing health and safety risks related to mining operations. Employers are responsible for ensuring that their blasting operations do not pose a risk to nearby communities, and adherence to ground vibration and flyrock regulations is a key part of that responsibility.\n\n## Pore Pressure in Tailings Storage Facilities\n\nPore water pressure is the pressure exerted by water within the void spaces of tailings, which influences effective stress and strength. This is an important parameter in TSF stability analysis, as seepage analysis involves the study of water flow through tailings to evaluate pore pressure distribution.\n\nThe search results indicate that blasting regulations and tailings management are separate but interrelated aspects of South African mining operations, with the MHSA providing the overarching regulatory framework.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"193e9b03-f0e0-48bb-82cd-24114a1d1cbc","uuid":"b6b26c3d-41fb-4ae6-9134-8a2f52266881","timestamp":"2026-07-06T19:42:13.963Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"South Africa mining blasting regulation MHSA pore pressure TSF tailings","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01KRzRBVw9yoeHSKhQFqMk9S","content":[{"title":"New South African Mining Regulations - Bishop Fraser Attorneys","url":"https://bishopfraser.co.za/new-south-african-mining-regulations/"},{"title":"Tailings Storage Facility (TSF) Terminologies: A Practical Engineering Reference - Mining Doc","url":"https://www.miningdoc.tech/question/tailings-storage-facility-tsf-terminologies-a-practical-engineering-reference/"},{"title":"Tailings Management System | Pan African Resources","url":"https://www.panafricanresources.com/tailings-management-system/"},{"title":"Dust Deposition Impacts at a Liquidated Gold Mine Village: Gauteng Province in South Africa","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7400412/"},{"title":"Blast Impact Assessment Final Blast Management and Consulting (PTY) LTD","url":"https://altavandykenvironmental.co.za/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/App-C4-Blast-Impact-Assessment.pdf"},{"title":"This gazette is also available free online at www.gpwonline.co.za","url":"https://www.gov.za/sites/default/files/gcis_document/202408/51003gon5097.pdf"}]},"I've found search results related to your query. Here's what the information reveals:\n\n## South African Mining Blasting Regulations\n\nMining in South Africa is regulated under the Mine Health and Safety Act (MHSA), which mandates strict adherence to a Code of Practice (COP). The August 2, 2024 Government Gazette issued the Guideline for a Mandatory Code of Practice for Minimum Standards on Ground Vibrations, Noise, Air-blast, and Flyrock near Surface Structures and Communities to be Protected.\n\nSection 9(2) of the MHSA requires employers to develop and implement a COP addressing health and safety risks related to mining operations. Employers are responsible for ensuring that their blasting operations do not pose a risk to nearby communities, and adherence to ground vibration and flyrock regulations is a key part of that responsibility.\n\n## Pore Pressure in Tailings Storage Facilities\n\nPore water pressure is the pressure exerted by water within the void spaces of tailings, which influences effective stress and strength. This is an important parameter in TSF stability analysis, as seepage analysis involves the study of water flow through tailings to evaluate pore pressure distribution.\n\nThe search results indicate that blasting regulations and tailings management are separate but interrelated aspects of South African mining operations, with the MHSA providing the overarching regulatory framework."],"durationSeconds":11.518283209000002,"searchCount":1}}
