{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014Wgy4NUY7Up9fkeKWYmxpN","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"ANCOLD \"Guidelines on Tailings Dams\" 2019 seismic liquefaction blasting dynamic loading chapters\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"TAILINGS LIQUEFACTION AND DAM STABILITY: ADVANCES IN DIAGNOSIS, MONITORING, STANDARDS AND MITIGATION – A CRITICAL REVIEW | REMUNOM\",\"url\":\"https://remunom.ojsbr.com/multidisciplinar/article/view/5120\"},{\"title\":\"Guidelines on Tailings Dams-Planning, Design, Construction, Operation and Closure-REVISION 1 (2019) - HARDCOPY - ANCOLD\",\"url\":\"https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Guidelines and recommendations on minimum factors of safety for slope stability of tailings dams\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/347741840_Guidelines_and_recommendations_on_minimum_factors_of_safety_for_slope_stability_of_tailings_dams\"},{\"title\":\"ANCOLD Rev 1 Guidelines On Tailings Dam (Planning, Design, Construction, Operation and Closure, 2019) | PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.scribd.com/document/884155994/ANCOLD-Rev-1-Guidelines-on-Tailings-Dam-Planning-Design-Construction-Operation-and-Closure-2019\"},{\"title\":\"AUSTRALIAN NATIONAL COMMITTEE ON LARGE DAMS GUIDELINES ON TAILINGS DAMS\",\"url\":\"https://www.ancold.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Tailings-Guideline-Addendum-July-2019_v2.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"ESG briefing: Tailings dams Escondida June 2019\",\"url\":\"https://www.bhp.com/-/media/documents/media/reports-and-presentations/2019/190607_esgbriefingtailingsdams.pdf?la=en\"},{\"title\":\"Runout of liquefaction-induced tailings dam failure: Influence of   earthquake motions and residual strength\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.12727\"},{\"title\":\"Stability assessment of a tailings storage facility using a non-local   constitutive model accounting for anisotropic strain-softening\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2010.03128\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about the ANCOLD 2019 Guidelines on Tailings Dams:\n\nThe 2019 revision includes updates on earthquake considerations to align with the ANCOLD Guidelines for Design of Dams and Appurtenant Structures for Earthquake (2019) and additional information on static liquefaction.\n\nThe guidelines address key topics related to your search query:\n\n- **Seismic and Liquefaction Issues**: Where liquefaction of tailings is likely to affect the stability of the dam, the design may need to consider a 1:10,000 AEP earthquake, unless an appropriate risk assessment is undertaken to confirm that an adequate risk profile is achieved with a lower earthquake loading.\n\n- **Dynamic Loading Considerations**: The guidelines reference cyclically reduced undrained/drained shear strength and/or liquefied residual shear strength for potentially liquefiable materials.\n\n- **Static Liquefaction**: ANCOLD (2019) recommends to conservatively assume that static liquefaction will occur for brittle/contractive saturated tailings.\n\nThe document appears to cover comprehensive guidance on planning, design, construction, operation, and closure of tailings dams with specific chapters addressing these dynamic and geotechnical considerations. The full guidelines document is available through ANCOLD's official website.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"193e9b03-f0e0-48bb-82cd-24114a1d1cbc","uuid":"4dbeeddf-4e61-469d-967b-3bbfd96006ea","timestamp":"2026-07-06T19:42:27.499Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"ANCOLD \"Guidelines on Tailings Dams\" 2019 seismic liquefaction blasting dynamic loading chapters","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01GkiqV43rLtyNoduPspjw5c","content":[{"title":"TAILINGS LIQUEFACTION AND DAM STABILITY: ADVANCES IN DIAGNOSIS, MONITORING, STANDARDS AND MITIGATION – A CRITICAL REVIEW | REMUNOM","url":"https://remunom.ojsbr.com/multidisciplinar/article/view/5120"},{"title":"Guidelines on Tailings Dams-Planning, Design, Construction, Operation and Closure-REVISION 1 (2019) - HARDCOPY - ANCOLD","url":"https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/"},{"title":"(PDF) Guidelines and recommendations on minimum factors of safety for slope stability of tailings dams","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/347741840_Guidelines_and_recommendations_on_minimum_factors_of_safety_for_slope_stability_of_tailings_dams"},{"title":"ANCOLD Rev 1 Guidelines On Tailings Dam (Planning, Design, Construction, Operation and Closure, 2019) | PDF","url":"https://www.scribd.com/document/884155994/ANCOLD-Rev-1-Guidelines-on-Tailings-Dam-Planning-Design-Construction-Operation-and-Closure-2019"},{"title":"AUSTRALIAN NATIONAL COMMITTEE ON LARGE DAMS GUIDELINES ON TAILINGS DAMS","url":"https://www.ancold.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Tailings-Guideline-Addendum-July-2019_v2.pdf"},{"title":"ESG briefing: Tailings dams Escondida June 2019","url":"https://www.bhp.com/-/media/documents/media/reports-and-presentations/2019/190607_esgbriefingtailingsdams.pdf?la=en"},{"title":"Runout of liquefaction-induced tailings dam failure: Influence of   earthquake motions and residual strength","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.12727"},{"title":"Stability assessment of a tailings storage facility using a non-local   constitutive model accounting for anisotropic strain-softening","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2010.03128"}]},"Based on the search results, I found relevant information about the ANCOLD 2019 Guidelines on Tailings Dams:\n\nThe 2019 revision includes updates on earthquake considerations to align with the ANCOLD Guidelines for Design of Dams and Appurtenant Structures for Earthquake (2019) and additional information on static liquefaction.\n\nThe guidelines address key topics related to your search query:\n\n- **Seismic and Liquefaction Issues**: Where liquefaction of tailings is likely to affect the stability of the dam, the design may need to consider a 1:10,000 AEP earthquake, unless an appropriate risk assessment is undertaken to confirm that an adequate risk profile is achieved with a lower earthquake loading.\n\n- **Dynamic Loading Considerations**: The guidelines reference cyclically reduced undrained/drained shear strength and/or liquefied residual shear strength for potentially liquefiable materials.\n\n- **Static Liquefaction**: ANCOLD (2019) recommends to conservatively assume that static liquefaction will occur for brittle/contractive saturated tailings.\n\nThe document appears to cover comprehensive guidance on planning, design, construction, operation, and closure of tailings dams with specific chapters addressing these dynamic and geotechnical considerations. The full guidelines document is available through ANCOLD's official website."],"durationSeconds":7.7667533330000005,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0116K3qGRM27etSJaojzUJz8","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"ICOLD bulletin 98 tailings dams seismicity 1995 bulletin 104 monitoring\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"ICOLD Bulletin No. 194 TAILINGS DAM SAFETY\",\"url\":\"https://unece.org/sites/default/files/2023-04/S4-1Andy%20Small_ENG.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"ICOLD Bulletin No. 194 TAILINGS DAM SAFETY - PDF Free Download\",\"url\":\"https://docplayer.net/233941027-Icold-bulletin-no-194-tailings-dam-safety.html\"},{\"title\":\"ICOLD Tailings Dam Safety Bulletin WORKSHOP ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.barrages-cfbr.eu/IMG/pdf/icold_workshop.tailings_dam_safety_bulletin.v1_2_-_may_2022.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Icoldchile\",\"url\":\"https://www.icoldchile.cl/boletines/121.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) RISK OF DANGEROUS OCCURRENCES\",\"url\":\"https://www.academia.edu/9168393/RISK_OF_DANGEROUS_OCCURRENCES\"},{\"title\":\"United States Society on Dams Observed Performance of Dams During Earthquakes\",\"url\":\"https://damfailures.org/sites/default/files/wp-pdf/EQPerfo2_v3.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"SELECTING SEISMIC PARAMETERS FOR LARGE DAMS Guidelines\",\"url\":\"https://www.icoldchile.cl/boletines/148.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Screening of seismic records to perform time-history dynamic analyses of   tailings dams: a power-spectral based approach\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2009.06426\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about the ICOLD bulletins you're inquiring about:\n\nICOLD Bulletin No. 98: Tailings Dams and Seismicity was published in 1995, and ICOLD Bulletin No. 104: Monitoring of Tailings Dams was published in 1996.\n\nThese bulletins are part of a series of ICOLD publications focused on tailings dam safety. Other related publications include Bulletin No. 97: Tailings Dams - Design of Drainage (1994), No. 101: Tailings Dams Transport, Placement and Decantation (1995), and No. 103: Tailings Dams and Environment (1996).\n\nThe search results confirm these bulletins exist, though the detailed contents of Bulletin 98 on seismicity and Bulletin 104 on monitoring are not provided in the search results. If you need more specific information about the contents or recommendations from these particular bulletins, you may need to access them directly from ICOLD publications.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"193e9b03-f0e0-48bb-82cd-24114a1d1cbc","uuid":"f9e33fac-a0d9-4027-8250-69e18c6b6895","timestamp":"2026-07-06T19:42:28.493Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"ICOLD bulletin 98 tailings dams seismicity 1995 bulletin 104 monitoring","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01M3m6n98AMpjYJbZ2JzykK6","content":[{"title":"ICOLD Bulletin No. 194 TAILINGS DAM SAFETY","url":"https://unece.org/sites/default/files/2023-04/S4-1Andy%20Small_ENG.pdf"},{"title":"ICOLD Bulletin No. 194 TAILINGS DAM SAFETY - PDF Free Download","url":"https://docplayer.net/233941027-Icold-bulletin-no-194-tailings-dam-safety.html"},{"title":"ICOLD Tailings Dam Safety Bulletin WORKSHOP ...","url":"https://www.barrages-cfbr.eu/IMG/pdf/icold_workshop.tailings_dam_safety_bulletin.v1_2_-_may_2022.pdf"},{"title":"Icoldchile","url":"https://www.icoldchile.cl/boletines/121.pdf"},{"title":"(PDF) RISK OF DANGEROUS OCCURRENCES","url":"https://www.academia.edu/9168393/RISK_OF_DANGEROUS_OCCURRENCES"},{"title":"United States Society on Dams Observed Performance of Dams During Earthquakes","url":"https://damfailures.org/sites/default/files/wp-pdf/EQPerfo2_v3.pdf"},{"title":"SELECTING SEISMIC PARAMETERS FOR LARGE DAMS Guidelines","url":"https://www.icoldchile.cl/boletines/148.pdf"},{"title":"Screening of seismic records to perform time-history dynamic analyses of   tailings dams: a power-spectral based approach","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2009.06426"}]},"Based on the search results, I found relevant information about the ICOLD bulletins you're inquiring about:\n\nICOLD Bulletin No. 98: Tailings Dams and Seismicity was published in 1995, and ICOLD Bulletin No. 104: Monitoring of Tailings Dams was published in 1996.\n\nThese bulletins are part of a series of ICOLD publications focused on tailings dam safety. Other related publications include Bulletin No. 97: Tailings Dams - Design of Drainage (1994), No. 101: Tailings Dams Transport, Placement and Decantation (1995), and No. 103: Tailings Dams and Environment (1996).\n\nThe search results confirm these bulletins exist, though the detailed contents of Bulletin 98 on seismicity and Bulletin 104 on monitoring are not provided in the search results. If you need more specific information about the contents or recommendations from these particular bulletins, you may need to access them directly from ICOLD publications."],"durationSeconds":7.759287707999989,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RKtKZSvwb3SXW6oBtfHUxE","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"South Africa DMRE tailings facility guidelines seismic blasting piezometer monitoring\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Tailings dam monitoring: In-ground and above-ground technologies - RST Instruments Ltd\",\"url\":\"https://rstinstruments.com/blog/tailings-dam-monitoring-in-ground-and-above-ground-technologies/\"},{\"title\":\"A Review of Tailings Dam Safety Monitoring Guidelines and Systems\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2075-163X/14/6/551\"},{\"title\":\"i YOUR TAILINGS DAM SOUTH AFRICAN NATIONAL COMMITTEE ON LARGE DAMS (SANCOLD)\",\"url\":\"https://sancold.org.za/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/SANCOLD-Your-Tailings-Dam-2020.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Tailings Storage Facility Monitoring\",\"url\":\"https://www.orica.com/industries-and-solutions/surface-metal/tailings-storage-facility-monitoring\"},{\"title\":\"Tailings Management System | Pan African Resources\",\"url\":\"https://www.panafricanresources.com/tailings-management-system/\"},{\"title\":\"Piezoceramic-Based Damage Monitoring of Concrete Structure for Underwater Blasting\",\"url\":\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7146326/\"},{\"title\":\"Peak particle velocity data acquisition for monitoring blast induced earthquakes in quarry sites\",\"url\":\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5997587/\"}]\n\nI've performed the web search for your query. The results provide relevant information about tailings facility monitoring, seismic considerations, and piezometer monitoring, though they don't specifically focus on DMRE (Department of Mineral Resources and Energy) guidelines alone.\n\n**Key findings from the search:**\n\n**Piezometer Monitoring:** Piezometers installed into tailings storage facility (TSF) slopes can highlight perched water level tables and areas of trapped water, which is important because increased pore pressure reduces shear strength of soil and is a precursor to failure.\n\n**Seismic Considerations:** For transient or seismic (earthquake) conditions, a minimum factor of safety of 1.1 is required. In South Africa, the severity or frequency (return period) of a seismic event which a tailings dam must be able to withstand is not prescribed in legislation and is somewhat subjective for the designer and owner.\n\n**Blasting and Seismic Monitoring:** Seismic sensors can produce false positives when used near active mine sites since they will also register movement of heavy equipment and activity caused by blasting operations, and effective use of seismic sensors in a monitoring system at an active site requires taking false positives into account when analyzing data sets.\n\n**South African Legislation:** South African legislation still needs to be adhered to for compliance, and the South African legislation does not yet directly address the Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management (GISTM).\n\nThe search results did not return specific DMRE tailings facility guidelines documents, which may require searching for official DMRE publications directly.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"193e9b03-f0e0-48bb-82cd-24114a1d1cbc","uuid":"2adc2a09-d9f0-4808-b10e-4cd745ec91ea","timestamp":"2026-07-06T19:42:31.562Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"South Africa DMRE tailings facility guidelines seismic blasting piezometer monitoring","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01QcMQBCpRDM9gCR4sEoLo6W","content":[{"title":"Tailings dam monitoring: In-ground and above-ground technologies - RST Instruments Ltd","url":"https://rstinstruments.com/blog/tailings-dam-monitoring-in-ground-and-above-ground-technologies/"},{"title":"A Review of Tailings Dam Safety Monitoring Guidelines and Systems","url":"https://www.mdpi.com/2075-163X/14/6/551"},{"title":"i YOUR TAILINGS DAM SOUTH AFRICAN NATIONAL COMMITTEE ON LARGE DAMS (SANCOLD)","url":"https://sancold.org.za/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/SANCOLD-Your-Tailings-Dam-2020.pdf"},{"title":"Tailings Storage Facility Monitoring","url":"https://www.orica.com/industries-and-solutions/surface-metal/tailings-storage-facility-monitoring"},{"title":"Tailings Management System | Pan African Resources","url":"https://www.panafricanresources.com/tailings-management-system/"},{"title":"Piezoceramic-Based Damage Monitoring of Concrete Structure for Underwater Blasting","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7146326/"},{"title":"Peak particle velocity data acquisition for monitoring blast induced earthquakes in quarry sites","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5997587/"}]},"I've performed the web search for your query. The results provide relevant information about tailings facility monitoring, seismic considerations, and piezometer monitoring, though they don't specifically focus on DMRE (Department of Mineral Resources and Energy) guidelines alone.\n\n**Key findings from the search:**\n\n**Piezometer Monitoring:** Piezometers installed into tailings storage facility (TSF) slopes can highlight perched water level tables and areas of trapped water, which is important because increased pore pressure reduces shear strength of soil and is a precursor to failure.\n\n**Seismic Considerations:** For transient or seismic (earthquake) conditions, a minimum factor of safety of 1.1 is required. In South Africa, the severity or frequency (return period) of a seismic event which a tailings dam must be able to withstand is not prescribed in legislation and is somewhat subjective for the designer and owner.\n\n**Blasting and Seismic Monitoring:** Seismic sensors can produce false positives when used near active mine sites since they will also register movement of heavy equipment and activity caused by blasting operations, and effective use of seismic sensors in a monitoring system at an active site requires taking false positives into account when analyzing data sets.\n\n**South African Legislation:** South African legislation still needs to be adhered to for compliance, and the South African legislation does not yet directly address the Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management (GISTM).\n\nThe search results did not return specific DMRE tailings facility guidelines documents, which may require searching for official DMRE publications directly."],"durationSeconds":9.887932207999999,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RCzNj6S6BsXrNxUi4RAkaF","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Youd liquefaction resistance soils 2001 NCEER cyclic stress ratio blasting induced\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"INTERNATIONAL SOCIETY FOR SOIL MECHANICS AND GEOTECHNICAL ENGINEERING\",\"url\":\"https://www.issmge.org/uploads/publications/1/2/1515-1518.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Regulatory Guide 1.198 \\\"Procedures and Criteria for ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.nrc.gov/docs/ML0332/ML033280143.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"17th World Conference on Earthquake Engineering, 17WCEE\",\"url\":\"https://wcee.nicee.org/wcee/article/17WCEE/4b-0023.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"4 The Simplified Stress-Based Approach to Triggering Assessment | State of the Art and Practice in the Assessment of Earthquake-Induced Soil Liquefaction and Its Consequences | The National Academies Press\",\"url\":\"https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/23474/chapter/6\"},{\"title\":\"SPT-BASED LIQUEFACTION TRIGGERING PROCEDURES\",\"url\":\"https://faculty.engineering.ucdavis.edu/boulanger/wp-content/uploads/sites/71/2014/09/Idriss_Boulanger_SPT_Liquefaction_CGM-10-02.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"© 2025 Rocscience Inc. Settle3 Liquefaction Theory Manual\",\"url\":\"https://static.rocscience.cloud/assets/verification-and-theory/Settle3/Settle3-Liquefaction-Theory-Manual.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"The correction factor of Monterey No. 0/30 sample with fines content liquefaction resistance between cyclic triaxial and cyclic hollow cylinder tests\",\"url\":\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9508088/\"},{\"title\":\"Assessment of liquefaction resistance of soil with fines using cyclic hollow cylinder testing\",\"url\":\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC12327689/\"}]\n\nI've completed the web search for your query about Youd liquefaction resistance soils, NCEER, cyclic stress ratio, and blasting-induced effects. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Main Results\n\nThe Youd et al. 2001 method (also referred to as the NCEER 2001 method) is a result of the 1996 National Center for Construction Education and Research (NCEER) and the 1998 NCEER/National Science Foundation (NSF) workshops on evaluation of liquefaction resistance of soils, and is still widely used in practice today to provide criteria for evaluation of liquefaction triggering of a saturated soil layer using CPT data.\n\nThe method generally involves the following steps: (1) evaluation of the cyclic stress ratio (CSR) based on seismic loading and soil stresses, (2) evaluation of the cyclic resistance ratio (CRR) based on CPT (or other) data, (3) calculation of the factor of safety against liquefaction based on the CSR and CRR, and (4) applying scaling factors to consider earthquake design magnitude, high overburden stresses, and static shear stresses to determine the final factor of safety against liquefaction.\n\nThe most commonly used relationships to predict CRR from SPT blow count are those proposed by Youd and colleagues (2001), and the Youd and colleagues (2001) relationship was agreed to by consensus at NCEER/NSF workshops in 1996 and 1998.\n\nHowever, the search results do not contain specific information about blasting-induced cyclic stress ratios or how the Youd/NCEER method applies to blasting scenarios. The results primarily focus on earthquake-induced liquefaction assessment.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"193e9b03-f0e0-48bb-82cd-24114a1d1cbc","uuid":"10ae3a14-e34c-437f-8d9a-dc04db78341b","timestamp":"2026-07-06T19:42:32.294Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Youd liquefaction resistance soils 2001 NCEER cyclic stress ratio blasting induced","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01CLP7wsKjBNjpRbL9X5cUYF","content":[{"title":"INTERNATIONAL SOCIETY FOR SOIL MECHANICS AND GEOTECHNICAL ENGINEERING","url":"https://www.issmge.org/uploads/publications/1/2/1515-1518.pdf"},{"title":"Regulatory Guide 1.198 \"Procedures and Criteria for ...","url":"https://www.nrc.gov/docs/ML0332/ML033280143.pdf"},{"title":"17th World Conference on Earthquake Engineering, 17WCEE","url":"https://wcee.nicee.org/wcee/article/17WCEE/4b-0023.pdf"},{"title":"4 The Simplified Stress-Based Approach to Triggering Assessment | State of the Art and Practice in the Assessment of Earthquake-Induced Soil Liquefaction and Its Consequences | The National Academies Press","url":"https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/23474/chapter/6"},{"title":"SPT-BASED LIQUEFACTION TRIGGERING PROCEDURES","url":"https://faculty.engineering.ucdavis.edu/boulanger/wp-content/uploads/sites/71/2014/09/Idriss_Boulanger_SPT_Liquefaction_CGM-10-02.pdf"},{"title":"© 2025 Rocscience Inc. Settle3 Liquefaction Theory Manual","url":"https://static.rocscience.cloud/assets/verification-and-theory/Settle3/Settle3-Liquefaction-Theory-Manual.pdf"},{"title":"The correction factor of Monterey No. 0/30 sample with fines content liquefaction resistance between cyclic triaxial and cyclic hollow cylinder tests","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9508088/"},{"title":"Assessment of liquefaction resistance of soil with fines using cyclic hollow cylinder testing","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC12327689/"}]},"I've completed the web search for your query about Youd liquefaction resistance soils, NCEER, cyclic stress ratio, and blasting-induced effects. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Main Results\n\nThe Youd et al. 2001 method (also referred to as the NCEER 2001 method) is a result of the 1996 National Center for Construction Education and Research (NCEER) and the 1998 NCEER/National Science Foundation (NSF) workshops on evaluation of liquefaction resistance of soils, and is still widely used in practice today to provide criteria for evaluation of liquefaction triggering of a saturated soil layer using CPT data.\n\nThe method generally involves the following steps: (1) evaluation of the cyclic stress ratio (CSR) based on seismic loading and soil stresses, (2) evaluation of the cyclic resistance ratio (CRR) based on CPT (or other) data, (3) calculation of the factor of safety against liquefaction based on the CSR and CRR, and (4) applying scaling factors to consider earthquake design magnitude, high overburden stresses, and static shear stresses to determine the final factor of safety against liquefaction.\n\nThe most commonly used relationships to predict CRR from SPT blow count are those proposed by Youd and colleagues (2001), and the Youd and colleagues (2001) relationship was agreed to by consensus at NCEER/NSF workshops in 1996 and 1998.\n\nHowever, the search results do not contain specific information about blasting-induced cyclic stress ratios or how the Youd/NCEER method applies to blasting scenarios. The results primarily focus on earthquake-induced liquefaction assessment."],"durationSeconds":9.87584700000001,"searchCount":1}}
