{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014jnVa3TLRgb2fSVZbAE9ku","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"MAC \"A Guide to the Management of Tailings Facilities\" 2019 blasting seismic dynamic loading\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"A Guide to the Management of Tailings Facilities, Version 3.1 (2019) - The Mining Association of Canada\",\"url\":\"https://mining.ca/resources/guides-manuals/a-guide-to-the-management-of-tailings-facilities-version-3-1-2019/\"},{\"title\":\"Tailings Guide - The Mining Association of Canada\",\"url\":\"https://mining.ca/our-focus/tailings-management/tailings-guide/\"},{\"title\":\"A Guide to the Management of Tailings Facilities Resource | Embedding Project\",\"url\":\"https://embeddingproject.org/resources/a-guide-to-the-management-of-tailings-facilities/\"},{\"title\":\"A Guide to the Management of Tailings Facilities | Resolution Copper Project and Land Exchange Environmental Impact Statement\",\"url\":\"https://www.resolutionmineeis.us/documents/mac-2019\"},{\"title\":\"A Guide to the Management of Tailings Facilities third Edition\",\"url\":\"https://s23.q4cdn.com/405985100/files/doc_downloads/MAC-Guide-to-the-Management-of-Tailings-Facilities-2017.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"A Guide to the Management of Tailings Facilities VERSION 3.1\",\"url\":\"https://mining.ca/wp-content/uploads/dlm_uploads/2021/06/MAC-Tailings-Guide_2019.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"A Guide to the Management of Tailings Facilities version 3.2\",\"url\":\"https://mining.ca/wp-content/uploads/dlm_uploads/2021/06/MAC-Tailings-Guide-Version-3-2-March-2021.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Tailings Management - The Mining Association of Canada\",\"url\":\"https://mining.ca/towards-sustainable-mining/protocols-guides/tailings-management-protocol/\"}]\n\nI found the search results for MAC's \"A Guide to the Management of Tailings Facilities\" Version 3.1 (2019). The search results confirm the existence and publication of this guide. However, the search results provided do not contain specific information about blasting, seismic, or dynamic loading sections within the document. \n\nThe search results show that Version 3.1 is an update to the third edition of A Guide to the Management of Tailings Facilities, released in November 2017, to reflect the second edition of MAC's Developing an Operation, Maintenance, and Surveillance Manual for Tailings and Water Management Facilities (the OMS Guide), and that the most significant change is a re-write of Section 5.2 Emergency Preparedness, which now provides more detailed guidance for the preparation of emergency response plans and emergency preparedness plans.\n\nTo access the specific content regarding blasting, seismic dynamic loading, and related topics within the 2019 guide, you would need to consult the full PDF document directly from the Mining Association of Canada's website.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"193e9b03-f0e0-48bb-82cd-24114a1d1cbc","uuid":"90e1d7db-07da-4d7e-96b8-47b8da0d5a95","timestamp":"2026-07-06T19:42:47.381Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"MAC \"A Guide to the Management of Tailings Facilities\" 2019 blasting seismic dynamic loading","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_018qyiraEg2AYxeTNYUGHBya","content":[{"title":"A Guide to the Management of Tailings Facilities, Version 3.1 (2019) - The Mining Association of Canada","url":"https://mining.ca/resources/guides-manuals/a-guide-to-the-management-of-tailings-facilities-version-3-1-2019/"},{"title":"Tailings Guide - The Mining Association of Canada","url":"https://mining.ca/our-focus/tailings-management/tailings-guide/"},{"title":"A Guide to the Management of Tailings Facilities Resource | Embedding Project","url":"https://embeddingproject.org/resources/a-guide-to-the-management-of-tailings-facilities/"},{"title":"A Guide to the Management of Tailings Facilities | Resolution Copper Project and Land Exchange Environmental Impact Statement","url":"https://www.resolutionmineeis.us/documents/mac-2019"},{"title":"A Guide to the Management of Tailings Facilities third Edition","url":"https://s23.q4cdn.com/405985100/files/doc_downloads/MAC-Guide-to-the-Management-of-Tailings-Facilities-2017.pdf"},{"title":"A Guide to the Management of Tailings Facilities VERSION 3.1","url":"https://mining.ca/wp-content/uploads/dlm_uploads/2021/06/MAC-Tailings-Guide_2019.pdf"},{"title":"A Guide to the Management of Tailings Facilities version 3.2","url":"https://mining.ca/wp-content/uploads/dlm_uploads/2021/06/MAC-Tailings-Guide-Version-3-2-March-2021.pdf"},{"title":"Tailings Management - The Mining Association of Canada","url":"https://mining.ca/towards-sustainable-mining/protocols-guides/tailings-management-protocol/"}]},"I found the search results for MAC's \"A Guide to the Management of Tailings Facilities\" Version 3.1 (2019). The search results confirm the existence and publication of this guide. However, the search results provided do not contain specific information about blasting, seismic, or dynamic loading sections within the document. \n\nThe search results show that Version 3.1 is an update to the third edition of A Guide to the Management of Tailings Facilities, released in November 2017, to reflect the second edition of MAC's Developing an Operation, Maintenance, and Surveillance Manual for Tailings and Water Management Facilities (the OMS Guide), and that the most significant change is a re-write of Section 5.2 Emergency Preparedness, which now provides more detailed guidance for the preparation of emergency response plans and emergency preparedness plans.\n\nTo access the specific content regarding blasting, seismic dynamic loading, and related topics within the 2019 guide, you would need to consult the full PDF document directly from the Mining Association of Canada's website."],"durationSeconds":6.808402332999991,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0185yRCRHVFDFVREq8oH8WWp","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"blast induced pore pressure tailings dam liquefaction screening cyclic mobility dynamic loading authoritative\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"(PDF) Dynamic liquefaction analysis of a tailings dam in Peru\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/354641262_Dynamic_liquefaction_analysis_of_a_tailings_dam_in_Peru\"},{\"title\":\"Blast-Induced Pore Pressure and Liquefaction of Saturated Sand\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/277684217_Blast-Induced_Pore_Pressure_and_Liquefaction_of_Saturated_Sand\"},{\"title\":\"ENGINEERING EVALUATION OF POST-LIQUEFACTION RESIDUAL STRENGTH\",\"url\":\"https://geotechnical.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/UCB-GT_22-01_Vol1.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"TAILINGS LIQUEFACTION AND DAM STABILITY\",\"url\":\"https://remunom.ojsbr.com/multidisciplinar/article/download/5120/4838/18955\"},{\"title\":\"When Cyclic Mobility Isn't Flow Liquefaction - Itasca Software\",\"url\":\"https://itascasoftware.com/resources/when-cyclic-mobility-isnt-flow-liquefaction/\"},{\"title\":\"Pore Pressure Measurement in Blast-Induced Liquefaction Experiments | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/245561710_Pore_Pressure_Measurement_in_Blast-Induced_Liquefaction_Experiments\"},{\"title\":\"Dynamic Response Characteristics and Liquefaction Analysis of a Phosphogypsum Tailings Dam | International Journal of Geomechanics | Vol 25, No 1\",\"url\":\"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/abs/10.1061/IJGNAI.GMENG-9229\"},{\"title\":\"Pore Pressure Measurement in Blast-Induced Liquefaction Experiments - Kyle M. Rollins, J. Dusty Lane, Emily Dibb, Scott A. Ashford, A. Gray Mullins, 2005\",\"url\":\"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/0361198105193600124\"},{\"title\":\"Screening of seismic records to perform time-history dynamic analyses of   tailings dams: a power-spectral based approach\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2009.06426\"}]\n\nI've performed the web search for your query on blast-induced pore pressure, tailings dam liquefaction, screening, cyclic mobility, and dynamic loading. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Key Research Areas\n\n**Blast-Induced Pore Pressure and Tailings Dams:**\nResearch has focused on the potential performance of earthfill and tailings dams, and other saturated earthen structures, subjected to blast vibrations. Studies have examined the impact of vibration waves induced in a tailings dam and their potential to generate excessive pore pressure.\n\n**Cyclic Loading and Liquefaction:**\nDense sandy tailings can be contractive under cyclic loading, generating excess pore pressure. Cyclic liquefaction relates to repeated loading and gradual pore-pressure buildup, which is more common in seismic environments.\n\n**Cyclic Mobility vs. Flow Liquefaction:**\nFlow liquefaction involves catastrophic loss of shear resistance, while cyclic mobility produces large deformations without complete strength loss.\n\n**Dynamic Analysis and Screening:**\nNumerical analysis has been performed to verify the liquefaction potential of tailings, considering various constitutive models for cyclic loading generated by seismic events. Screening can be performed in two stages: an initial review of titles and abstracts to eliminate unrelated works, followed by a full-text assessment based on predefined eligibility criteria.\n\n**Pore Pressure Measurement:**\nPore pressure sensors make it possible to define the extent of the liquefied zone during blast liquefaction experiments and to understand the soil behavior during cyclic loading.\n\nThe search results include authoritative sources from peer-reviewed journals, academic institutions (Berkeley), and professional organizations in geotechnical engineering.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"193e9b03-f0e0-48bb-82cd-24114a1d1cbc","uuid":"c1d0bbc4-5259-4d20-877c-924006453a6b","timestamp":"2026-07-06T19:42:49.386Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"blast induced pore pressure tailings dam liquefaction screening cyclic mobility dynamic loading authoritative","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_0196HdxtzGdy9vkFcJqJ9TFj","content":[{"title":"(PDF) Dynamic liquefaction analysis of a tailings dam in Peru","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/354641262_Dynamic_liquefaction_analysis_of_a_tailings_dam_in_Peru"},{"title":"Blast-Induced Pore Pressure and Liquefaction of Saturated Sand","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/277684217_Blast-Induced_Pore_Pressure_and_Liquefaction_of_Saturated_Sand"},{"title":"ENGINEERING EVALUATION OF POST-LIQUEFACTION RESIDUAL STRENGTH","url":"https://geotechnical.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/UCB-GT_22-01_Vol1.pdf"},{"title":"TAILINGS LIQUEFACTION AND DAM STABILITY","url":"https://remunom.ojsbr.com/multidisciplinar/article/download/5120/4838/18955"},{"title":"When Cyclic Mobility Isn't Flow Liquefaction - Itasca Software","url":"https://itascasoftware.com/resources/when-cyclic-mobility-isnt-flow-liquefaction/"},{"title":"Pore Pressure Measurement in Blast-Induced Liquefaction Experiments | Request PDF","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/245561710_Pore_Pressure_Measurement_in_Blast-Induced_Liquefaction_Experiments"},{"title":"Dynamic Response Characteristics and Liquefaction Analysis of a Phosphogypsum Tailings Dam | International Journal of Geomechanics | Vol 25, No 1","url":"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/abs/10.1061/IJGNAI.GMENG-9229"},{"title":"Pore Pressure Measurement in Blast-Induced Liquefaction Experiments - Kyle M. Rollins, J. Dusty Lane, Emily Dibb, Scott A. Ashford, A. Gray Mullins, 2005","url":"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/0361198105193600124"},{"title":"Screening of seismic records to perform time-history dynamic analyses of   tailings dams: a power-spectral based approach","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2009.06426"}]},"I've performed the web search for your query on blast-induced pore pressure, tailings dam liquefaction, screening, cyclic mobility, and dynamic loading. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Key Research Areas\n\n**Blast-Induced Pore Pressure and Tailings Dams:**\nResearch has focused on the potential performance of earthfill and tailings dams, and other saturated earthen structures, subjected to blast vibrations. Studies have examined the impact of vibration waves induced in a tailings dam and their potential to generate excessive pore pressure.\n\n**Cyclic Loading and Liquefaction:**\nDense sandy tailings can be contractive under cyclic loading, generating excess pore pressure. Cyclic liquefaction relates to repeated loading and gradual pore-pressure buildup, which is more common in seismic environments.\n\n**Cyclic Mobility vs. Flow Liquefaction:**\nFlow liquefaction involves catastrophic loss of shear resistance, while cyclic mobility produces large deformations without complete strength loss.\n\n**Dynamic Analysis and Screening:**\nNumerical analysis has been performed to verify the liquefaction potential of tailings, considering various constitutive models for cyclic loading generated by seismic events. Screening can be performed in two stages: an initial review of titles and abstracts to eliminate unrelated works, followed by a full-text assessment based on predefined eligibility criteria.\n\n**Pore Pressure Measurement:**\nPore pressure sensors make it possible to define the extent of the liquefied zone during blast liquefaction experiments and to understand the soil behavior during cyclic loading.\n\nThe search results include authoritative sources from peer-reviewed journals, academic institutions (Berkeley), and professional organizations in geotechnical engineering."],"durationSeconds":9.748887375000006,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FF2wBP8B6dUh6sa9KyLkfp","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"South Africa MHSA \"code of practice\" blasting tailings dam seismic vibration limits\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"New South African Mining Regulations - Bishop Fraser Attorneys\",\"url\":\"https://bishopfraser.co.za/new-south-african-mining-regulations/\"},{\"title\":\"A review of EN 16907 on earthworks (extractive waste) in the context of South African mine residues\",\"url\":\"https://scielo.org.za/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S2225-62532022000600007\"},{\"title\":\"i YOUR TAILINGS DAM SOUTH AFRICAN NATIONAL COMMITTEE ON LARGE DAMS (SANCOLD)\",\"url\":\"https://sancold.org.za/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/SANCOLD-Your-Tailings-Dam-2020.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Guideline for a Mandatory Code for Practice for Minimum ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.gov.za/sites/default/files/gcis_document/202408/51003gon5097.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Mine Blasting Standards: Code of Practice for Minimum Standards on Ground Vibrations, Noise, Air-Blast and Flyrock Near Surface Structures and Communities to be Protected (2024) – Centre for Environmental Rights\",\"url\":\"https://cer.org.za/virtual-library/legislation/national/mining/mine-blasting-guideline-code-of-practice-for-minimum-standards-on-ground-vibrations-noise-air-blast-and-flyrock-near-surface-structures-and-communities-to-be-protected-2024\"},{\"title\":\"An In Toto Approach to Radon Dispersion Modelling from a South African Gold Mine Tailings\",\"url\":\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9266810/\"},{\"title\":\"This gazette is also available free online at www.gpwonline.co.za\",\"url\":\"https://www.ensafrica.com/uploads/newsarticles/0_gg%2051003,%20genn%205097.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Screening of seismic records to perform time-history dynamic analyses of   tailings dams: a power-spectral based approach\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2009.06426\"}]\n\nI've performed the web search for information about South Africa's MHSA Code of Practice regarding blasting tailings dam seismic vibration limits. Here are the key findings:\n\nThe Department of Mineral Resources and Energy issued a Guideline for a Mandatory Code of Practice for Minimum Standards on Ground Vibrations, Noise, Air-blast, and Flyrock near Surface Structures and Communities to be Protected, which appeared in the Government Gazette dated 2 August 2024.\n\nThis Code of Practice outlines the minimum standards for mitigating risks related to ground vibration, noise, and flyrock. This is a mandatory COP in terms of sections 9(2) and 9(3) of the MHSA.\n\nRegarding tailings dams specifically, the search results show that for tailings dam design, flood management considers storm events up to the Probable Maximum Precipitation (PMP), and seismic events up to the Maximum Credible Earthquake (MCE) are considered. Additionally, for transient or seismic (earthquake) conditions, a minimum factor of safety of 1.1 is required.\n\nHowever, the search results do not contain the specific seismic vibration limit values that may be specified in the Code of Practice for blasting-induced ground vibrations affecting tailings dams. To find those specific numerical limits, you would need to access the full document directly from the official government source or consult mining regulatory authorities.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"193e9b03-f0e0-48bb-82cd-24114a1d1cbc","uuid":"d45eb673-32d6-4fb3-9663-1cb9c1793f51","timestamp":"2026-07-06T19:42:51.328Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"South Africa MHSA \"code of practice\" blasting tailings dam seismic vibration limits","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01928qwhc3tZPnUp4zoeCRHk","content":[{"title":"New South African Mining Regulations - Bishop Fraser Attorneys","url":"https://bishopfraser.co.za/new-south-african-mining-regulations/"},{"title":"A review of EN 16907 on earthworks (extractive waste) in the context of South African mine residues","url":"https://scielo.org.za/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S2225-62532022000600007"},{"title":"i YOUR TAILINGS DAM SOUTH AFRICAN NATIONAL COMMITTEE ON LARGE DAMS (SANCOLD)","url":"https://sancold.org.za/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/SANCOLD-Your-Tailings-Dam-2020.pdf"},{"title":"Guideline for a Mandatory Code for Practice for Minimum ...","url":"https://www.gov.za/sites/default/files/gcis_document/202408/51003gon5097.pdf"},{"title":"Mine Blasting Standards: Code of Practice for Minimum Standards on Ground Vibrations, Noise, Air-Blast and Flyrock Near Surface Structures and Communities to be Protected (2024) – Centre for Environmental Rights","url":"https://cer.org.za/virtual-library/legislation/national/mining/mine-blasting-guideline-code-of-practice-for-minimum-standards-on-ground-vibrations-noise-air-blast-and-flyrock-near-surface-structures-and-communities-to-be-protected-2024"},{"title":"An In Toto Approach to Radon Dispersion Modelling from a South African Gold Mine Tailings","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9266810/"},{"title":"This gazette is also available free online at www.gpwonline.co.za","url":"https://www.ensafrica.com/uploads/newsarticles/0_gg%2051003,%20genn%205097.pdf"},{"title":"Screening of seismic records to perform time-history dynamic analyses of   tailings dams: a power-spectral based approach","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2009.06426"}]},"I've performed the web search for information about South Africa's MHSA Code of Practice regarding blasting tailings dam seismic vibration limits. Here are the key findings:\n\nThe Department of Mineral Resources and Energy issued a Guideline for a Mandatory Code of Practice for Minimum Standards on Ground Vibrations, Noise, Air-blast, and Flyrock near Surface Structures and Communities to be Protected, which appeared in the Government Gazette dated 2 August 2024.\n\nThis Code of Practice outlines the minimum standards for mitigating risks related to ground vibration, noise, and flyrock. This is a mandatory COP in terms of sections 9(2) and 9(3) of the MHSA.\n\nRegarding tailings dams specifically, the search results show that for tailings dam design, flood management considers storm events up to the Probable Maximum Precipitation (PMP), and seismic events up to the Maximum Credible Earthquake (MCE) are considered. Additionally, for transient or seismic (earthquake) conditions, a minimum factor of safety of 1.1 is required.\n\nHowever, the search results do not contain the specific seismic vibration limit values that may be specified in the Code of Practice for blasting-induced ground vibrations affecting tailings dams. To find those specific numerical limits, you would need to access the full document directly from the official government source or consult mining regulatory authorities."],"durationSeconds":10.256202417000008,"searchCount":1}}
