{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01VZ7HtEUCqp4qFr3EAk1yie","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0185atMi3uwpRD4r69VaLWiA","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/averrik/Cloud/github/projects/AR-S2J2J.R/TITO/sessions/run-20260706_164057_11000/T8.research.md","old_string":"Liquefaction screening for saturated tailings under dynamic loading is performed within the simplified stress-based framework of Youd et al. (2001) or Idriss and Boulanger (2008), in which the cyclic stress ratio (CSR), representing the demand imposed by the dynamic event, is compared with the cyclic resistance ratio (CRR), representing the soil resistance to liquefaction triggering derived from in-situ penetration tests such as the SPT or CPT. [WEB:https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/23474/chapter/6] [WEB:https://www.researchgate.net/publication/288624104_Soil_liquefaction_during_earthquakes] The factor of safety against liquefaction is defined as:\n\n$$FS_L = \\frac{CRR_{7.5} \\cdot MSF \\cdot K_\\sigma}{CSR}$$\n\nwhere $CRR_{7.5}$ is the cyclic resistance ratio normalised to a reference earthquake magnitude of 7.5, $MSF$ is the magnitude scaling factor correcting for earthquake duration, and $K_\\sigma$ is the overburden correction factor. When blast-induced loading rather than earthquake loading is the triggering mechanism, the CSR must be estimated from the blast-induced ground motion waveform and a representative equivalent number of uniform loading cycles must be assigned to the transient event - a parameter that is far smaller for a blast than for a sustained earthquake of similar peak amplitude. This adaptation of the earthquake-based framework to blast loading requires project-specific assumptions on loading characterisation that are not codified in any universally adopted standard, making $FS_L$ a conditional engineering output dependent on those documented choices.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The Youd et al. (2001) and Idriss and Boulanger (2008) frameworks and the definition of $FS_L$ in terms of CRR, MSF, $K_\\sigma$, and CSR are confirmed by multiple independent web sources including the National Academies Press, University of California Davis faculty reports, and published monograph summaries. The formula as written is consistent with the standard presentation of these methods. The qualification about blast loading adaptation is based on the documented difference in loading duration between blasting and earthquake events, which is reflected in the geotechnical literature on blast-induced liquefaction experiments referenced in the web search results.]","new_string":"Liquefaction screening for saturated tailings under dynamic loading is performed within the simplified stress-based framework of Youd et al. (2001) or Idriss and Boulanger (2008), in which the cyclic stress ratio (CSR), representing the demand imposed by the dynamic event, is compared with the cyclic resistance ratio (CRR), representing the soil resistance to liquefaction triggering derived from in-situ penetration tests such as the SPT or CPT. [WEB:https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/23474/chapter/6] [WEB:https://www.researchgate.net/publication/288624104_Soil_liquefaction_during_earthquakes] The factor of safety against liquefaction is defined as:^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The simplified stress-based liquefaction screening framework of Youd et al. (2001) and Idriss and Boulanger (2008) is confirmed by multiple web search results including the National Academies Press and University of California Davis faculty documents. The description of SPT and CPT as the standard in-situ test methods for deriving CRR is consistent with those sources. This paragraph introduces the display equation below and contains no independent factual claim beyond the framework description, which is well-supported.]\n\n$$FS_L = \\frac{CRR_{7.5} \\cdot MSF \\cdot K_\\sigma}{CSR}$$\n\nwhere $CRR_{7.5}$ is the cyclic resistance ratio normalised to a reference earthquake magnitude of 7.5, $MSF$ is the magnitude scaling factor correcting for earthquake duration, and $K_\\sigma$ is the overburden correction factor. When blast-induced loading rather than earthquake loading is the triggering mechanism, the CSR must be estimated from the blast-induced ground motion waveform and a representative equivalent number of uniform loading cycles must be assigned to the transient event - a parameter that is far smaller for a blast than for a sustained earthquake of similar peak amplitude. This adaptation of the earthquake-based framework to blast loading requires project-specific assumptions on loading characterisation that are not codified in any universally adopted standard, making $FS_L$ a conditional engineering output dependent on those documented choices.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The definition of $FS_L$ with $CRR_{7.5}$, $MSF$, $K_\\sigma$, and CSR is consistent with the standard presentation in Youd et al. (2001) and Idriss and Boulanger (2008), confirmed by web search results. The qualification that blast loading requires adaptation of the number-of-cycles parameter is consistent with the documented shorter duration of blast events compared with earthquake events, as referenced in the blast-induced liquefaction literature found in the web search results. No codified universal blast-liquefaction screening procedure was identified in any source retrieved.]"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11198,"cache_read_input_tokens":100960,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":11198,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":26,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"193e9b03-f0e0-48bb-82cd-24114a1d1cbc","uuid":"ef34ecb1-bc93-4a35-a4c5-1fc72aa6bed4","request_id":"req_011CcmPWcrm7X2AR62oMWSMr"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0185atMi3uwpRD4r69VaLWiA","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/averrik/Cloud/github/projects/AR-S2J2J.R/TITO/sessions/run-20260706_164057_11000/T8.research.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"193e9b03-f0e0-48bb-82cd-24114a1d1cbc","uuid":"bd3fa41c-1c13-4932-a8c3-d67ea55e0fdc","timestamp":"2026-07-06T19:54:16.355Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"/Users/averrik/Cloud/github/projects/AR-S2J2J.R/TITO/sessions/run-20260706_164057_11000/T8.research.md","oldString":"Liquefaction screening for saturated tailings under dynamic loading is performed within the simplified stress-based framework of Youd et al. (2001) or Idriss and Boulanger (2008), in which the cyclic stress ratio (CSR), representing the demand imposed by the dynamic event, is compared with the cyclic resistance ratio (CRR), representing the soil resistance to liquefaction triggering derived from in-situ penetration tests such as the SPT or CPT. [WEB:https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/23474/chapter/6] [WEB:https://www.researchgate.net/publication/288624104_Soil_liquefaction_during_earthquakes] The factor of safety against liquefaction is defined as:\n\n$$FS_L = \\frac{CRR_{7.5} \\cdot MSF \\cdot K_\\sigma}{CSR}$$\n\nwhere $CRR_{7.5}$ is the cyclic resistance ratio normalised to a reference earthquake magnitude of 7.5, $MSF$ is the magnitude scaling factor correcting for earthquake duration, and $K_\\sigma$ is the overburden correction factor. When blast-induced loading rather than earthquake loading is the triggering mechanism, the CSR must be estimated from the blast-induced ground motion waveform and a representative equivalent number of uniform loading cycles must be assigned to the transient event - a parameter that is far smaller for a blast than for a sustained earthquake of similar peak amplitude. This adaptation of the earthquake-based framework to blast loading requires project-specific assumptions on loading characterisation that are not codified in any universally adopted standard, making $FS_L$ a conditional engineering output dependent on those documented choices.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The Youd et al. (2001) and Idriss and Boulanger (2008) frameworks and the definition of $FS_L$ in terms of CRR, MSF, $K_\\sigma$, and CSR are confirmed by multiple independent web sources including the National Academies Press, University of California Davis faculty reports, and published monograph summaries. The formula as written is consistent with the standard presentation of these methods. The qualification about blast loading adaptation is based on the documented difference in loading duration between blasting and earthquake events, which is reflected in the geotechnical literature on blast-induced liquefaction experiments referenced in the web search results.]","newString":"Liquefaction screening for saturated tailings under dynamic loading is performed within the simplified stress-based framework of Youd et al. (2001) or Idriss and Boulanger (2008), in which the cyclic stress ratio (CSR), representing the demand imposed by the dynamic event, is compared with the cyclic resistance ratio (CRR), representing the soil resistance to liquefaction triggering derived from in-situ penetration tests such as the SPT or CPT. [WEB:https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/23474/chapter/6] [WEB:https://www.researchgate.net/publication/288624104_Soil_liquefaction_during_earthquakes] The factor of safety against liquefaction is defined as:^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The simplified stress-based liquefaction screening framework of Youd et al. (2001) and Idriss and Boulanger (2008) is confirmed by multiple web search results including the National Academies Press and University of California Davis faculty documents. The description of SPT and CPT as the standard in-situ test methods for deriving CRR is consistent with those sources. This paragraph introduces the display equation below and contains no independent factual claim beyond the framework description, which is well-supported.]\n\n$$FS_L = \\frac{CRR_{7.5} \\cdot MSF \\cdot K_\\sigma}{CSR}$$\n\nwhere $CRR_{7.5}$ is the cyclic resistance ratio normalised to a reference earthquake magnitude of 7.5, $MSF$ is the magnitude scaling factor correcting for earthquake duration, and $K_\\sigma$ is the overburden correction factor. When blast-induced loading rather than earthquake loading is the triggering mechanism, the CSR must be estimated from the blast-induced ground motion waveform and a representative equivalent number of uniform loading cycles must be assigned to the transient event - a parameter that is far smaller for a blast than for a sustained earthquake of similar peak amplitude. This adaptation of the earthquake-based framework to blast loading requires project-specific assumptions on loading characterisation that are not codified in any universally adopted standard, making $FS_L$ a conditional engineering output dependent on those documented choices.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The definition of $FS_L$ with $CRR_{7.5}$, $MSF$, $K_\\sigma$, and CSR is consistent with the standard presentation in Youd et al. (2001) and Idriss and Boulanger (2008), confirmed by web search results. The qualification that blast loading requires adaptation of the number-of-cycles parameter is consistent with the documented shorter duration of blast events compared with earthquake events, as referenced in the blast-induced liquefaction literature found in the web search results. No codified universal blast-liquefaction screening procedure was identified in any source retrieved.]","originalFile":"# Guidance Documents for Blasting Effects on Tailings Storage Facilities: AR-S2J2J.R\n\n---\n\n### SLOT 1: Authoritative Guidance Documents Covering Blasting Effects on TSFs\n\nThe international industry standards that most directly address dynamic loading, vibration monitoring, pore-pressure response, and liquefaction screening for tailings storage facilities are the Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management (GISTM, 2020) and the IFC Environmental, Health, and Safety Guidelines for Mining (2007). The GISTM, developed under the auspices of the United Nations Environment Programme, the International Council on Mining and Metals, and the Principles for Responsible Investment, requires operators to establish an interdisciplinary knowledge base encompassing geotechnical and seismicity information, to document design criteria explicitly, and to implement integrated performance monitoring throughout the facility life cycle. [KB:T1.compile.research.md] The standard does not prescribe fixed numerical vibration thresholds for blast-induced loading on TSFs; it frames safety as a risk-management obligation demanding project-specific assessment and continuous monitoring as the means of demonstrating performance. The IFC EHS Guidelines for Mining (2007) require that tailings impoundments and dams be planned, designed, and operated such that geotechnical risks are assessed and managed throughout the mine life cycle, and they require designs to reflect internationally recognised engineering practices and local seismic conditions, treating geotechnical risk assessment - including blast-induced ground motion effects on tailings facilities - as a distinct discipline rather than an extension of building-vibration compliance. [KB:T1.compile.research.md]^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: Both GISTM and IFC EHS Mining Guidelines are confirmed in the KB (T1.compile.research.md) with content that is consistent with established characterisations of these documents. The claim that neither prescribes fixed blast vibration thresholds for TSFs is directly supported by the KB statement that GISTM requires an interdisciplinary knowledge base and performance monitoring rather than fixed limits, and by the IFC guidance treating tailings facilities as requiring risk assessment and internationally recognised engineering practice.]\n\nThe ANCOLD Guidelines on Tailings Dams, Revision 1 (2019), published by the Australian National Committee on Large Dams, provide the most comprehensive single-document treatment of dynamic and seismic loading for tailings storage facilities among the available authoritative sources. [WEB:https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/] The 2019 revision explicitly aligns tailings dam seismic design criteria with the companion ANCOLD Guidelines for Design of Dams and Appurtenant Structures for Earthquake (2019), addresses both static and cyclic liquefaction of saturated tailings, and recommends conservatively assuming that static liquefaction will occur for brittle, contractive, saturated tailings unless site-specific evidence supports a less conservative classification. The document addresses planning, design, construction, operation, and closure, treating earthquake loading and factor-of-safety requirements under dynamic conditions as subjects requiring engineering analysis throughout the facility life cycle rather than as compliance thresholds satisfied by reference to a look-up table.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The ANCOLD Guidelines on Tailings Dams Revision 1 (2019) are confirmed by web search results including the official ANCOLD product page and the publicly available 2019 addendum text. The specific content claims - alignment with earthquake design guidelines, treatment of static and cyclic liquefaction, conservative assumption of static liquefaction for contractive tailings - are each corroborated by the search results, which quote these provisions directly.]\n\nICOLD has produced a series of bulletins that collectively constitute the authoritative international technical foundation for seismic assessment of tailings dams. ICOLD Bulletin 98 (1995), Tailings Dams and Seismicity, addresses the seismic behavior of tailings facilities specifically, covering liquefaction potential, dynamic stability, and modes of failure associated with cyclic ground motion. ICOLD Bulletin 104 (1996), Monitoring of Tailings Dams, establishes the framework for instrumented monitoring programs, including piezometric surveillance of pore-pressure response in TSF embankments. ICOLD Bulletin 121 (2001), Tailings Dams - Risk of Dangerous Occurrences, documents lessons from actual failures including seismically triggered and liquefaction-related events. ICOLD Bulletin 148 (2011), Selecting Seismic Parameters for Large Dams, provides guidance on seismic hazard characterisation and the selection of design earthquake parameters applicable to large storage facilities including tailings dams. [WEB:https://www.icoldchile.cl/boletines/148.pdf] ICOLD Bulletin 194 (2022), Tailings Dam Safety, is the most recent unified ICOLD statement on tailings dam governance and engineering, consolidating seismic requirements alongside operational and monitoring obligations in a form intended to supersede and integrate the earlier bulletins for contemporary professional practice. [WEB:https://klohn.com/wp-content/uploads/B194-ICOLD-Tailings-Dam-Safety.pdf]^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The ICOLD bulletins listed (98, 104, 121, 148, 194) are confirmed through multiple independent web search results. Bulletins 98 and 104 are specifically named in the ICOLD bulletin sequence summaries returned by the search. Bulletin 148 is available as a PDF from the ICOLD Chile chapter. Bulletin 194 is referenced in a UNECE presentation and available through the Klohn Crippen Berger website. The attribution of topics (seismicity, monitoring, risk of dangerous occurrences, seismic parameters, tailings safety) to the respective bulletins is consistent across multiple search results.]\n\nISO 4866:2010, Mechanical Vibration and Shock - Vibration of Fixed Structures, provides the foundational measurement methodology for blast-vibration monitoring programs. [KB:T1.compile.research.md] The standard applies to structures above and below ground including earth structures such as dykes and embankments, and addresses sources including explosions and construction activities; however, it explicitly excludes certain special structures, including dams, from its scope. Because a TSF is typically classified as a dam for regulatory and engineering purposes, ISO 4866:2010 is directly applicable as the technical framework for blast-vibration transducer placement, waveform acquisition, and frequency-content characterisation in TSF monitoring programs, while falling short of providing structural acceptance criteria for the facility itself. The standard's exclusion of dams from its direct scope is relevant to report framing: ISO 4866:2010 supports the measurement methodology but does not establish whether a measured vibration level is acceptable for a particular TSF, which remains a matter of project-specific engineering assessment.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The characterisation of ISO 4866:2010 - its applicability to earth structures including dykes and embankments, its coverage of explosion sources, and its specific exclusion of dams - is drawn directly from the KB (T1.compile.research.md), which is treated as authoritative for this audit step. The inference that a TSF classified as a dam falls outside the direct compliance scope of ISO 4866 follows from the stated scope exclusion in the standard itself and is not contradicted by any other source cited in this document.]\n\nThe geotechnical liquefaction-assessment literature constitutes an integral part of the analytical framework for evaluating the response of saturated tailings to blast-induced dynamic loading, even though that literature was developed primarily for earthquake loading scenarios. Youd et al. (2001), summarising the consensus reached at the 1996 NCEER and 1998 NCEER/NSF workshops on evaluation of liquefaction resistance of soils, established the simplified stress-based framework in which the cyclic stress ratio (CSR) representing dynamic demand is compared with the cyclic resistance ratio (CRR) representing soil resistance to produce a factor of safety against liquefaction triggering. [WEB:https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/23474/chapter/6] Idriss and Boulanger (2008), Soil Liquefaction During Earthquakes, updated and refined those procedures with revised magnitude scaling factors, improved handling of low-plasticity fine-grained soils of the type commonly encountered in tailings, and updated CPT and SPT-based CRR correlations. [WEB:https://www.researchgate.net/publication/288624104_Soil_liquefaction_during_earthquakes] Both frameworks are designed for earthquake-induced cyclic loading, and their application to blast-induced ground motion requires project-specific adaptation of the loading characterisation, particularly the estimation of equivalent uniform cycles associated with a transient blast event.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The Youd et al. (2001) and Idriss and Boulanger (2008) frameworks are widely documented authoritative references in the geotechnical engineering literature. Their content - CSR/CRR framework, magnitude scaling factors, CPT and SPT correlations, treatment of fine-grained soils - is confirmed by multiple web search results, including the National Academies Press summary and multiple faculty PDFs. The qualification that adaptation is needed for blasting scenarios is standard geotechnical practice and is consistent with the absence of any blast-specific version of these frameworks in the search results.]\n\nThe Mining Association of Canada's Guide to the Management of Tailings Facilities, Version 3.1 (2019), provides an operations-oriented framework for TSF safety that complements the design-phase guidance of ANCOLD and ICOLD. [WEB:https://mining.ca/resources/guides-manuals/a-guide-to-the-management-of-tailings-facilities-version-3-1-2019/] The MAC guide structures TSF management through an Operation, Maintenance and Surveillance (OMS) manual framework that requires documented monitoring of all identified performance indicators, including instrumented pore-pressure and vibration responses where blasting occurs in proximity to a TSF. While the MAC guide does not prescribe fixed quantitative vibration thresholds for blast events, its OMS framework establishes the operational mechanism by which project-specific monitoring trigger levels and alarm thresholds are defined, communicated to operators, and updated in response to observed performance.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The MAC Guide to the Management of Tailings Facilities Version 3.1 (2019) is confirmed via the official Mining Association of Canada website URL found in the web search results. The OMS framework characterisation is consistent with publicly available descriptions of the MAC guide and with the general practice in tailings management. The claim that the guide does not prescribe fixed vibration thresholds is consistent with its governance-and-management focus, which is confirmed by the search result descriptions of its content.]\n\n---\n\n### SLOT 2: South African versus International Applicability of Sources\n\nWithin the South African regulatory environment, the primary legislative instrument governing mining blasting is the Mine Health and Safety Act 29 of 1996 (MHSA), which requires mine operators to prepare and implement a site-specific mandatory Code of Practice addressing health and safety risks associated with their particular operations. The Department of Mineral Resources and Energy issued the Guideline for a Mandatory Code of Practice for Minimum Standards on Ground Vibrations, Noise, Air-blast, and Flyrock near Surface Structures and Communities to be Protected in August 2024, establishing the current national baseline for blast-vibration control in South African mining under sections 9(2) and 9(3) of the MHSA. [WEB:https://cer.org.za/virtual-library/legislation/national/mining/mine-blasting-guideline-code-of-practice-for-minimum-standards-on-ground-vibrations-noise-air-blast-and-flyrock-near-surface-structures-and-communities-to-be-protected-2024] This guideline defines protected structures and communities in the context of surface mining, and operators must demonstrate compliance through a site-specific COP that describes monitoring arrangements and acceptable vibration levels for identified sensitive receivers. The 2024 DMRE blasting guideline is South African in origin and legally mandated, making it the primary regulatory compliance reference for blasting vibration limits in a South African mining project, including projects where blasting occurs in proximity to a TSF.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The existence and content of the South African MHSA (1996) and the DMRE August 2024 Guideline for a Mandatory Code of Practice are confirmed by web search results, including the Centre for Environmental Rights website entry and the Government Gazette reference. The MHSA sections cited (9(2) and 9(3)) are consistent with multiple South African legal references found in the search results. The characterisation of the guideline as defining protected structures and requiring site-specific COPs is consistent with the regulatory framework described in the search results.]\n\nThe South African National Committee on Large Dams (SANCOLD) has produced guidance specifically directed at the South African context for tailings dam management. The SANCOLD document \"Your Tailings Dam\" (2020) addresses design, operation, and safety monitoring of tailings facilities within the South African legislative environment and explicitly notes two significant gaps in the local regulatory framework: first, that South African legislation does not prescribe the severity or return period of a seismic event that a tailings dam must withstand, leaving that determination as a matter of engineering judgement by the designer and owner; and second, that South African legislation does not yet directly address the GISTM requirements. [WEB:https://sancold.org.za/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/SANCOLD-Your-Tailings-Dam-2020.pdf] These observations are directly relevant to report framing: a project operating under South African jurisdiction cannot assume that the seismic or dynamic-loading acceptance criteria in any international standard have been adopted directly into South African law, and the engineer of record must state the basis on which any seismic performance target has been chosen.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The SANCOLD \"Your Tailings Dam\" (2020) document is confirmed by a web search result citing the SANCOLD website URL. The specific claims - that South African legislation does not prescribe seismic return periods for tailings dams and that it does not yet address GISTM - are drawn from the web search result text, which directly quotes or paraphrases these findings from the SANCOLD document. These are material observations specific to the South African regulatory context.]\n\nThe international sources - GISTM, ANCOLD (2019), ICOLD bulletins 98, 104, 121, 148, and 194, the IFC EHS Guidelines for Mining, and ISO 4866:2010 - are applicable across jurisdictions as statements of good international practice and are accepted as technical reference documents for independent review in South Africa. [KB:T1.compile.research.md] [WEB:https://www.icmm.com/en-gb/our-principles/tailings/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management] Their adoption in a South African TSF report is appropriate as technical framing for engineering assessment, and lenders, financiers, and independent review panels routinely require alignment with GISTM and ICOLD guidance. However, the specific numerical targets in those documents - such as the ANCOLD minimum factor of safety of 1.1 under seismic loading, or earthquake return periods referenced in ICOLD Bulletin 148 - derive from Australian or international regulatory contexts and should be treated as engineering references rather than as South African legal requirements unless the project permit, lender technical schedules, or the Engineer of Record explicitly adopts them as binding project standards.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The categorisation of GISTM, ANCOLD, ICOLD, IFC EHS, and ISO 4866 as international references applicable across jurisdictions is well-established and confirmed by multiple web sources. The SANCOLD search result specifically confirms that South African legislation does not adopt GISTM directly, supporting the qualification that international numerical criteria are not automatically legally binding in South Africa. The mention of lender requirements for GISTM alignment is consistent with general knowledge of equator principles and DFI practice, though no specific URL confirming that point appears in the search results; the confidence rating reflects the direct documentary support for the primary regulatory gap claim.]\n\nThe U.S. Office of Surface Mining Reclamation and Enforcement rules in 30 CFR Part 816 (Sections 816.67 and 816.68) are a mature regulatory example of how a blasting framework distinguishes fixed PPV thresholds for standard occupied structures from a project-specific approval process for other structures, including dams, impoundments, utilities, and underground mines. [KB:T1.compile.research.md] Under 30 CFR 816.67, the vibration limits for dams and impoundments are not automatically derived from the tabulated building thresholds but are submitted in the blasting plan and approved by the regulator on a project-specific basis. This regulatory architecture - separate fixed limits for standard receivers and a project-specific submission process for engineered earth structures - illustrates the principle that South African practice approximates through the MHSA COP process, even though the South African framework is articulated differently and does not reproduce the specific numerical values or definitional conventions of the U.S. regulations. Citing 30 CFR in a South African TSF report is appropriate as an international regulatory analogue illustrating best-practice structure, provided the report clearly identifies it as a U.S. federal rule rather than as a standard applicable in South Africa.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The characterisation of 30 CFR 816.67 and 816.68 is drawn directly from the KB (T1.compile.research.md), which is treated as authoritative for this audit step. The KB explicitly states that 30 CFR 816.67 provides project-specific limits for dams and impoundments submitted in the blasting plan rather than adopting the building-level thresholds. The inference that the South African COP process approximates this architecture is a supported analogy, not a legally equivalent statement, and is appropriately qualified.]\n\n---\n\n### SLOT 3: Quantitative Acceptance Criteria versus Project-Specific Assessment\n\nNone of the primary authoritative sources for TSF safety - GISTM, ANCOLD (2019), ICOLD bulletins 98, 104, 121, 148, and 194, or the IFC EHS Guidelines for Mining - prescribes a universal fixed blast-vibration threshold expressed as a limiting peak particle velocity for protecting a tailings storage facility. The absence of a universal vibration limit for TSFs reflects the established recognition in geotechnical dam engineering that the dynamic response of a saturated tailings facility depends on material properties, fabric, degree of saturation, drainage conditions, confining stress, pore-pressure history, the geometry of the embankment, and the proximity and characteristics of the blasting source. None of these site-specific factors are captured by a generic PPV criterion derived from research on vibration effects in rigid or masonry building structures. [KB:T1.compile.research.md] [WEB:https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/]^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The claim that none of the listed authoritative TSF documents prescribes a universal blast PPV limit is strongly supported. The KB (T1.compile.research.md) states that 30 CFR requires project-specific plan submission for dams and impoundments rather than a tabulated threshold; GISTM and IFC EHS are characterised as requiring risk assessment and project-specific criteria. The ANCOLD web search results describe factor-of-safety and earthquake-return-period criteria but no universal PPV limit for blasting on TSFs. No web source retrieved a fixed PPV acceptance criterion for TSFs from any of the listed documents.]\n\nANCOLD (2019) provides the most explicit quantitative framing available for TSF seismic performance, but its criteria are expressed in terms of structural stability factors of safety and design earthquake return periods rather than in terms of blast-vibration amplitude. The guidelines prescribe a minimum factor of safety of 1.1 for transient and seismic loading conditions, and recommend evaluating the facility against a 1:10,000 annual exceedance probability earthquake where liquefaction of tailings is likely to affect the stability of the embankment, unless a site-specific risk assessment demonstrates that an adequate risk profile is achieved with a lower seismic loading. [WEB:https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/] These quantitative targets are outputs of a project-specific engineering process - geotechnical characterisation, in-situ testing, liquefaction potential assessment, and dynamic stability analysis - and are not simplified look-up values. Applying them to blast-induced loading requires translating the blast-vibration record into equivalent dynamic demand parameters consistent with the geotechnical analysis framework, which involves project-specific assumptions about wave attenuation, loading duration, and equivalent cyclic cycles.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The ANCOLD (2019) factor-of-safety value of 1.1 for seismic conditions and the 1:10,000 AEP earthquake recommendation for liquefiable tailings are confirmed by two independent web search results: the ANCOLD product page summary and the SANCOLD web search result, which cites a minimum FOS of 1.1 for transient/seismic conditions in South African guidance citing ANCOLD practice. The conclusion that these criteria presuppose project-specific geotechnical analysis follows directly from the stated content of ANCOLD.]\n\nLiquefaction screening for saturated tailings under dynamic loading is performed within the simplified stress-based framework of Youd et al. (2001) or Idriss and Boulanger (2008), in which the cyclic stress ratio (CSR), representing the demand imposed by the dynamic event, is compared with the cyclic resistance ratio (CRR), representing the soil resistance to liquefaction triggering derived from in-situ penetration tests such as the SPT or CPT. [WEB:https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/23474/chapter/6] [WEB:https://www.researchgate.net/publication/288624104_Soil_liquefaction_during_earthquakes] The factor of safety against liquefaction is defined as:\n\n$$FS_L = \\frac{CRR_{7.5} \\cdot MSF \\cdot K_\\sigma}{CSR}$$\n\nwhere $CRR_{7.5}$ is the cyclic resistance ratio normalised to a reference earthquake magnitude of 7.5, $MSF$ is the magnitude scaling factor correcting for earthquake duration, and $K_\\sigma$ is the overburden correction factor. When blast-induced loading rather than earthquake loading is the triggering mechanism, the CSR must be estimated from the blast-induced ground motion waveform and a representative equivalent number of uniform loading cycles must be assigned to the transient event - a parameter that is far smaller for a blast than for a sustained earthquake of similar peak amplitude. This adaptation of the earthquake-based framework to blast loading requires project-specific assumptions on loading characterisation that are not codified in any universally adopted standard, making $FS_L$ a conditional engineering output dependent on those documented choices.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The Youd et al. (2001) and Idriss and Boulanger (2008) frameworks and the definition of $FS_L$ in terms of CRR, MSF, $K_\\sigma$, and CSR are confirmed by multiple independent web sources including the National Academies Press, University of California Davis faculty reports, and published monograph summaries. The formula as written is consistent with the standard presentation of these methods. The qualification about blast loading adaptation is based on the documented difference in loading duration between blasting and earthquake events, which is reflected in the geotechnical literature on blast-induced liquefaction experiments referenced in the web search results.]\n\nPore-pressure monitoring programs serve as the empirical counterpart to analytical liquefaction screening. ICOLD Bulletin 104 (1996) and the MAC OMS framework require piezometer instrumentation within TSF embankments and tailings deposits to measure in-situ pore pressure, detect excess pore-pressure generation following dynamic events, and track pore-pressure dissipation rates. [WEB:https://mining.ca/resources/guides-manuals/a-guide-to-the-management-of-tailings-facilities-version-3-1-2019/] The monitoring data serve to calibrate and validate analytical models, to detect anomalous pore-pressure responses that may indicate incipient instability, and to confirm that measured vibration events do not produce lasting pore-pressure accumulation above the thresholds defined in the OMS manual. Acceptance criteria for pore-pressure response are project-specific, defined within the OMS manual or instrumentation response plan as trigger values and alarm thresholds derived from site-specific stability calculations rather than adopted from any generic standard or building-vibration criterion.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: ICOLD Bulletin 104 (1996) as a monitoring guideline for tailings dams is confirmed by the web search results. The MAC OMS framework and its requirement for documented monitoring including piezometric response is confirmed by the MAC Guide URL and summary descriptions. The characterisation of acceptance criteria as project-specific is consistent with all surveyed guidance documents, none of which supply a generic pore-pressure threshold for blast-event monitoring at TSFs.]\n\n---\n\n### SLOT 4: Report Framing to Distinguish Criteria, Monitoring Requirements, and Assumptions\n\nA professional technical report on the effects of nearby blasting on a TSF must clearly delineate four distinct categories of statement: published reference criteria drawn from identified standards; project-specific criteria established for the facility in question by the Engineer of Record; monitoring requirements arising from regulatory obligations, best-practice standards, and design documentation; and analysis assumptions made in the assessment. Conflating these categories - in particular, applying a criterion developed for a different receiver type, such as a building-damage PPV threshold from a vibration standard, as though it governs TSF structural performance - misrepresents the state of professional practice and may produce technically indefensible conclusions that expose the report to challenge during regulatory review or independent assessment. [KB:T1.compile.research.md] [WEB:https://klohn.com/wp-content/uploads/B194-ICOLD-Tailings-Dam-Safety.pdf]^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The four-category framework for report organisation is consistent with the practice distinctions described throughout the cited sources: the KB (T1.compile.research.md) explicitly distinguishes structural damage criteria from human-exposure criteria and from monitoring/reporting requirements, and from project-specific plan submissions. ICOLD Bulletin 194 (2022), referenced via the Klohn Crippen Berger URL, consolidates governance and engineering requirements in a manner that requires explicit documentation of design criteria as a distinct category. The characterisation of conflation as a reportable risk is a professional judgement consistent with the regulatory framing described in the cited sources.]\n\nPublished reference criteria from building-vibration standards such as BS 7385-2:1993 and from human-comfort and human-exposure standards such as BS 6472-2:2008 or ISO 2631-2 do not govern TSF structural performance and are outside the scope of the present assessment. Those documents are explicitly scoped to building structures and to occupant physiological response respectively; none establishes an acceptance criterion for an earth structure such as a tailings embankment or impoundment. Presenting a PPV threshold derived from those standards as the criterion for protecting a TSF implies a scope of applicability that those standards do not claim, and it obscures the absence of a published fixed vibration threshold for the engineering question actually under assessment. ISO 4866:2010, which does include earth structures within certain parts of its scope, provides measurement and evaluation methodology but explicitly excludes dams and similarly does not prescribe limiting vibration amplitudes for earth embankments or tailings facilities. [KB:T1.compile.research.md] The report should therefore name these standards only in their correct application context - as references for the building-damage or human-comfort dimensions of any broader assessment - and should present the TSF structural-performance question as governed by the geotechnical assessment framework described by ANCOLD, ICOLD, GISTM, and the site-specific engineering analysis.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The scope of BS 7385-2 (building damage), BS 6472-2 (human blast exposure in buildings), and ISO 2631-2 (human annoyance and comfort) is confirmed in the KB (T1.compile.research.md), which explicitly states that BS 6472-2 \"does not address building damage\" and that ISO 2631-2 \"concerns comfort and annoyance from whole-body vibration in buildings\" and that \"structural damage is discussed in ISO 4866.\" The KB also confirms that ISO 4866 excludes dams from its scope. These are straightforward scope characterisations from the KB, treated as authoritative for this audit step.]\n\nThe GISTM (2020) and IFC EHS Guidelines for Mining (2007) frame TSF safety as a risk-management process in which operators define performance criteria embedded in facility design documentation and the OMS manual, rather than a compliance checklist satisfied by reference to tabulated limits. [KB:T1.compile.research.md] Within that framework, a project-specific blasting acceptance criterion for the TSF - expressed as a vibration amplitude limit, a pore-pressure trigger level, or a stability factor-of-safety threshold - is established through site-specific engineering assessment, agreed with the independent tailings review board or qualified person, and documented in the OMS manual. The report should name the document in which the project-specific criterion is established, state the basis on which it was derived (for example, the geotechnical analysis from which the pore-pressure trigger value follows, or the stability calculation from which the vibration limit was back-analysed), and distinguish it from both the general guidance documents and from the MHSA COP regulatory compliance thresholds, which address different protected receivers under different legal obligations.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The characterisation of GISTM and IFC EHS Mining as requiring operator-defined, documented performance criteria rather than fixed tabulated limits is confirmed by the KB (T1.compile.research.md) and by the web search results describing GISTM as requiring documented design criteria and integrated performance monitoring. The distinction between OMS-documented project-specific criteria and general guidance documents is consistent with the MAC OMS framework, which is confirmed via the MAC website URL in the search results.]\n\nMonitoring requirements constitute a distinct reporting category that should be presented as performance-verification tools operating alongside the analytical assessment, not as substitutes for it. ISO 4866:2010 provides the technical basis for the vibration monitoring methodology, specifying transducer placement, signal acquisition requirements, frequency-content characterisation, and data analysis procedures applicable to blast events affecting earth structures. [KB:T1.compile.research.md] ICOLD Bulletin 104 provides the framework for piezometric monitoring programs at tailings dams, and the MAC OMS framework prescribes the structure of an instrumentation response plan including defined trigger and alarm levels. These documents establish what must be measured and how, and they inform the thresholds at which measurements require a response; they do not themselves generate the acceptance criteria, which arise from site-specific engineering calculation.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The characterisation of ISO 4866 as covering blast-vibration measurement methodology, and of ICOLD Bulletin 104 as covering tailings dam piezometric monitoring, is supported by KB content (ISO 4866) and web search results (ICOLD 104). The MAC OMS framework is confirmed via the MAC website. The distinction between monitoring standards and acceptance criteria is consistent with the content of all cited sources and with the structure of the analytical-monitoring relationship described in the GISTM and IFC EHS frameworks.]\n\nAnalysis assumptions constitute the fourth category requiring transparent reporting. Where the earthquake-based liquefaction-screening frameworks of Youd et al. (2001) or Idriss and Boulanger (2008) are adapted to blast-induced cyclic loading, the project-specific choices that determine the computed factor of safety include the equivalent number of uniform cycles assigned to the blast event, the attenuation relationship used to estimate ground motion at the TSF, the representative penetration resistance profile used to derive CRR, the drainage assumptions applied to the tailings under the transient loading duration, and the specific CRR correlation selected. [WEB:https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/23474/chapter/6] [WEB:https://www.researchgate.net/publication/288624104_Soil_liquefaction_during_earthquakes] The report should state these assumptions explicitly, distinguish them from established values in the referenced standards, and present the sensitivity of the conclusions to key parameter choices, so that the engineering judgement embedded in the analysis is transparent and auditable. The absence of a codified blast-liquefaction screening procedure in any of the authoritative guidance documents means that the report cannot defer responsibility for those assumptions to a named standard; they are engineering choices that require explicit justification.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The requirement to adapt earthquake-based liquefaction frameworks to blast loading by selecting equivalent cycle counts and other parameters is confirmed by the structure of the Youd et al. (2001) and Idriss and Boulanger (2008) frameworks as described in the web search results: those frameworks are explicitly earthquake-oriented, and blast-specific adaptations are not codified in any universal standard identified in this research. The list of project-specific assumptions is consistent with standard geotechnical practice for applying simplified liquefaction methods.]\n\n---\n\n### SLOT 5: Source-Backed Terminology for TSF Response to Blasting\n\nThe term \"dynamic loading\" appears in the ICOLD bulletins on tailings dams and in the ANCOLD guidelines with a consistent meaning: any time-varying force or acceleration applied to the structure, its embankment, or its foundation, encompassing earthquake-induced ground motion, blast-induced ground vibration, and in some formulations operational loads from heavy equipment. [WEB:https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/] [WEB:https://klohn.com/wp-content/uploads/B194-ICOLD-Tailings-Dam-Safety.pdf] In the tailings dam literature, dynamic loading is treated as a stability-relevant load case that must be considered alongside static loading in the design and performance assessment of the facility and that may govern design where the tailings or foundation material is susceptible to strength loss under cyclic excitation. The term does not carry an implication about the source of the loading; it is applicable to both seismic and blast events provided the dynamic nature of the loading is established.^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The use of \"dynamic loading\" in the context of ANCOLD and ICOLD tailings dam guidance is consistent with the content of those documents as confirmed by web search results. However, the specific application to both seismic and blast events in the same ANCOLD or ICOLD text is not directly quoted in the retrieved search results, which describe seismic loading primarily. The characterisation is a reasonable and professionally defensible extrapolation of the term's standard technical meaning in geotechnical engineering.]\n\nThe term \"transient vibration\" is used in the vibration measurement literature, including ISO 4866:2010, to describe a vibration event of finite duration that does not persist as continuous or steady-state excitation. [KB:T1.compile.research.md] Blast-induced ground motion constitutes a transient vibration event: it consists of a short-duration stress-wave sequence generated by detonation, characterised by a rapidly rising wavefront, a frequency spectrum determined by the charge weight, delay pattern, and geological propagation path, and a decay envelope that is a function of the scaled distance to the receptor. The term is appropriate for characterising the nature of the blast-induced ground motion as a physical input to the TSF assessment but carries no connotations about the structural response or the stability outcome; those are assessed through the geotechnical analysis.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The characterisation of ISO 4866:2010 and the term \"transient vibration\" is drawn directly from the KB (T1.compile.research.md), which is treated as authoritative. The description of blast-induced ground motion as a transient vibration event with a rising wavefront, frequency content, and decay with scaled distance is consistent with standard blast seismology practice and is supported implicitly by the ISO 4866 coverage of explosive sources described in the KB.]\n\n\"Pore-pressure generation\" and \"excess pore pressure\" are the standard geotechnical terms for the increase in pore-water pressure above the in-situ hydrostatic equilibrium produced by undrained cyclic or transient loading. ICOLD Bulletin 98 (1995) and the Idriss and Boulanger (2008) monograph use this terminology to describe the fundamental mechanism through which dynamic loading may degrade the effective stress and shear strength of saturated tailings. [WEB:https://www.researchgate.net/publication/288624104_Soil_liquefaction_during_earthquakes] The normalised excess pore-pressure ratio is defined as:\n\n$$r_u = \\frac{\\Delta u}{\\sigma'_{v0}}$$\n\nwhere $\\Delta u$ is the excess pore-water pressure generated by the dynamic event and $\\sigma'_{v0}$ is the initial effective vertical stress. The ratio $r_u$ is the key performance indicator in both instrumented piezometric monitoring and in constitutive models used for dynamic numerical analysis. As $r_u$ approaches unity, the effective stress approaches zero and the undrained shear strength is substantially reduced, with the consequence for stability depending on whether the residual state is contractive or dilative.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The terms \"pore-pressure generation\" and \"excess pore pressure\" and the definition of $r_u = \\Delta u / \\sigma'_{v0}$ are standard in geotechnical engineering and in the referenced monograph (Idriss and Boulanger, 2008). The Idriss and Boulanger (2008) reference is confirmed by multiple web search results. The interpretation of $r_u$ approaching unity as corresponding to near-zero effective stress and strength reduction is a direct consequence of the effective-stress principle and is universally accepted in the geotechnical literature.]\n\n\"Cyclic mobility\" and \"flow liquefaction\" are the two principal distinct failure modes associated with excess pore-pressure generation in saturated soils under cyclic loading, and their distinction is essential for accurate TSF reporting. Flow liquefaction occurs in contractive soils - including loose, saturated, fine-grained tailings - where the residual undrained shear strength mobilised after triggering is insufficient to maintain stability under the existing static shear stresses, resulting in a catastrophic, sustained loss of resistance and runout. Cyclic mobility occurs in soils that retain a positive residual undrained shear strength after triggering, producing transient loss of effective stress and large cyclic strains without a sustained flow failure. [WEB:https://itascasoftware.com/resources/when-cyclic-mobility-isnt-flow-liquefaction/] ICOLD Bulletin 98 and the Idriss and Boulanger (2008) framework distinguish these mechanisms, and ANCOLD (2019) recommends conservatively assuming flow liquefaction for saturated, brittle, contractive tailings absent evidence supporting a less severe classification. [WEB:https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/] In a technical report, the failure mode being assessed should be named explicitly, and the material evidence - penetration resistance profile, gradation, plasticity index - that supports the classification should be cited.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The distinction between flow liquefaction and cyclic mobility is a well-established geotechnical concept confirmed by the web search results, including the Itasca resource and the general liquefaction literature. The ANCOLD recommendation to assume flow liquefaction conservatively for contractive saturated tailings is confirmed by the ANCOLD search result, which directly quotes this provision. The ICOLD Bulletin 98 attribution is confirmed by the search results identifying that bulletin's subject matter as tailings dams and seismicity.]\n\n\"Liquefaction potential\" is used in the Youd et al. (2001) framework and in the ICOLD tailings dam bulletins to describe the susceptibility of a saturated soil deposit to liquefaction triggering under a specified dynamic loading. [WEB:https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/23474/chapter/6] The quantitative measure of liquefaction potential is the factor of safety against triggering:\n\n$$FS_L = \\frac{CRR_{7.5} \\cdot MSF \\cdot K_\\sigma}{CSR}$$\n\nwhere $CRR_{7.5}$ is the cyclic resistance ratio corrected to a reference earthquake magnitude of $M_w = 7.5$, $MSF$ is the magnitude scaling factor that adjusts for the duration and equivalent cycles of the actual event, and $K_\\sigma$ is the overburden correction factor. When a blast event rather than an earthquake provides the dynamic demand, the CSR must be estimated from the blast-induced ground motion parameters and a representative equivalent number of uniform stress cycles must be assigned to the transient loading, making $FS_L$ a conditional quantity whose value is governed by these project-specific input choices. Values of $FS_L > 1$ indicate that the resistance exceeds the demand for the stated assumptions, while $FS_L \\leq 1$ indicates a triggering condition; the margin separating these states should be reported alongside the governing assumptions.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The Youd et al. (2001) framework, the definition of $FS_L$ in terms of $CRR_{7.5}$, $MSF$, $K_\\sigma$, and CSR, and the characterisation of $FS_L$ as a conditional output are confirmed by multiple web search results including the National Academies Press and University of California Davis resources. The formula structure is consistent with standard presentations. The conditional qualification for blast loading is consistent with the absence of a codified blast-adaptation procedure in any source retrieved.]\n\n\"Deformation\" and \"stability\" are used in the ANCOLD guidelines and ICOLD bulletins as distinct but related performance parameters for tailings dam assessment under dynamic loading. Stability refers to the resistance of the TSF embankment and its foundation to slope failure, evaluated as a factor of safety against global instability; ANCOLD (2019) prescribes a minimum value of 1.1 under transient seismic conditions as a quantitative performance target. [WEB:https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/] Deformation refers to the permanent displacements accumulated during a dynamic event, evaluated through analyses such as the Newmark sliding-block method or fully coupled dynamic numerical analysis; it is possible for a structure to remain nominally stable - maintaining a factor of safety above unity throughout the event - while accumulating permanent deformations that damage the crest, impair drainage elements, or compromise the freeboard required for safe operation. A technical report should distinguish stability assessment from deformation assessment and should state explicitly which analyses have been performed, which have been omitted, and why.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The distinction between deformation and stability as performance parameters in dam engineering is a standard professional convention supported by the ANCOLD guidelines (confirmed via web search) and consistent with ICOLD practice. The ANCOLD FS of 1.1 for seismic conditions is confirmed by both the ANCOLD product page search result and the SANCOLD search result. The Newmark sliding-block method for deformation estimation is a standard geotechnical reference that is consistent with the ANCOLD search result content.]\n\n\"Monitoring\" in the TSF context encompasses instrumented surveillance programs designed to detect developing adverse conditions before they progress to a breach or failure event. [WEB:https://mining.ca/resources/guides-manuals/a-guide-to-the-management-of-tailings-facilities-version-3-1-2019/] [KB:T1.compile.research.md] The GISTM and the IFC EHS Mining Guidelines require integrated performance monitoring programs that include piezometric measurement of pore pressure within the embankment and tailings mass, geotechnical surface monitoring of crest and slope displacement, and vibration monitoring of blast events affecting the facility. In the specific context of blasting effects on a TSF, monitoring serves two distinct functions: a compliance-verification function, demonstrating that measured blast-vibration parameters do not exceed the project-specific thresholds documented in the OMS manual and required by the MHSA COP; and a geotechnical feedback function, providing measured pore-pressure response data that can be compared with model predictions and used to update or validate the analytical assessment of liquefaction potential. A report that presents monitoring results should identify which of these functions the presented data serve and should confirm that the monitoring instrumentation, acquisition parameters, and analysis method are consistent with the requirements of the applicable standard or guidance document, including ISO 4866:2010 for vibration data and ICOLD Bulletin 104 for piezometric data.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The dual function of monitoring - regulatory compliance verification and geotechnical feedback - is consistent with the combined requirements of GISTM (confirmed via KB and ICMM web search result), IFC EHS Mining (confirmed via KB and IFC website search results), MAC OMS framework (confirmed via MAC website), and the MHSA COP requirement (confirmed via DMRE 2024 guideline web search result). The reference to ISO 4866 for vibration data and ICOLD Bulletin 104 for piezometric data is consistent with the content of those documents as confirmed in the KB and web search results respectively.]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":36,"oldLines":11,"newStart":36,"newLines":11,"lines":[" "," ANCOLD (2019) provides the most explicit quantitative framing available for TSF seismic performance, but its criteria are expressed in terms of structural stability factors of safety and design earthquake return periods rather than in terms of blast-vibration amplitude. The guidelines prescribe a minimum factor of safety of 1.1 for transient and seismic loading conditions, and recommend evaluating the facility against a 1:10,000 annual exceedance probability earthquake where liquefaction of tailings is likely to affect the stability of the embankment, unless a site-specific risk assessment demonstrates that an adequate risk profile is achieved with a lower seismic loading. [WEB:https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/] These quantitative targets are outputs of a project-specific engineering process - geotechnical characterisation, in-situ testing, liquefaction potential assessment, and dynamic stability analysis - and are not simplified look-up values. Applying them to blast-induced loading requires translating the blast-vibration record into equivalent dynamic demand parameters consistent with the geotechnical analysis framework, which involves project-specific assumptions about wave attenuation, loading duration, and equivalent cyclic cycles.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The ANCOLD (2019) factor-of-safety value of 1.1 for seismic conditions and the 1:10,000 AEP earthquake recommendation for liquefiable tailings are confirmed by two independent web search results: the ANCOLD product page summary and the SANCOLD web search result, which cites a minimum FOS of 1.1 for transient/seismic conditions in South African guidance citing ANCOLD practice. The conclusion that these criteria presuppose project-specific geotechnical analysis follows directly from the stated content of ANCOLD.]"," ","-Liquefaction screening for saturated tailings under dynamic loading is performed within the simplified stress-based framework of Youd et al. (2001) or Idriss and Boulanger (2008), in which the cyclic stress ratio (CSR), representing the demand imposed by the dynamic event, is compared with the cyclic resistance ratio (CRR), representing the soil resistance to liquefaction triggering derived from in-situ penetration tests such as the SPT or CPT. [WEB:https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/23474/chapter/6] [WEB:https://www.researchgate.net/publication/288624104_Soil_liquefaction_during_earthquakes] The factor of safety against liquefaction is defined as:","+Liquefaction screening for saturated tailings under dynamic loading is performed within the simplified stress-based framework of Youd et al. (2001) or Idriss and Boulanger (2008), in which the cyclic stress ratio (CSR), representing the demand imposed by the dynamic event, is compared with the cyclic resistance ratio (CRR), representing the soil resistance to liquefaction triggering derived from in-situ penetration tests such as the SPT or CPT. [WEB:https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/23474/chapter/6] [WEB:https://www.researchgate.net/publication/288624104_Soil_liquefaction_during_earthquakes] The factor of safety against liquefaction is defined as:^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The simplified stress-based liquefaction screening framework of Youd et al. (2001) and Idriss and Boulanger (2008) is confirmed by multiple web search results including the National Academies Press and University of California Davis faculty documents. The description of SPT and CPT as the standard in-situ test methods for deriving CRR is consistent with those sources. This paragraph introduces the display equation below and contains no independent factual claim beyond the framework description, which is well-supported.]"," "," $$FS_L = \\frac{CRR_{7.5} \\cdot MSF \\cdot K_\\sigma}{CSR}$$"," ","-where $CRR_{7.5}$ is the cyclic resistance ratio normalised to a reference earthquake magnitude of 7.5, $MSF$ is the magnitude scaling factor correcting for earthquake duration, and $K_\\sigma$ is the overburden correction factor. When blast-induced loading rather than earthquake loading is the triggering mechanism, the CSR must be estimated from the blast-induced ground motion waveform and a representative equivalent number of uniform loading cycles must be assigned to the transient event - a parameter that is far smaller for a blast than for a sustained earthquake of similar peak amplitude. This adaptation of the earthquake-based framework to blast loading requires project-specific assumptions on loading characterisation that are not codified in any universally adopted standard, making $FS_L$ a conditional engineering output dependent on those documented choices.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The Youd et al. (2001) and Idriss and Boulanger (2008) frameworks and the definition of $FS_L$ in terms of CRR, MSF, $K_\\sigma$, and CSR are confirmed by multiple independent web sources including the National Academies Press, University of California Davis faculty reports, and published monograph summaries. The formula as written is consistent with the standard presentation of these methods. The qualification about blast loading adaptation is based on the documented difference in loading duration between blasting and earthquake events, which is reflected in the geotechnical literature on blast-induced liquefaction experiments referenced in the web search results.]","+where $CRR_{7.5}$ is the cyclic resistance ratio normalised to a reference earthquake magnitude of 7.5, $MSF$ is the magnitude scaling factor correcting for earthquake duration, and $K_\\sigma$ is the overburden correction factor. When blast-induced loading rather than earthquake loading is the triggering mechanism, the CSR must be estimated from the blast-induced ground motion waveform and a representative equivalent number of uniform loading cycles must be assigned to the transient event - a parameter that is far smaller for a blast than for a sustained earthquake of similar peak amplitude. This adaptation of the earthquake-based framework to blast loading requires project-specific assumptions on loading characterisation that are not codified in any universally adopted standard, making $FS_L$ a conditional engineering output dependent on those documented choices.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The definition of $FS_L$ with $CRR_{7.5}$, $MSF$, $K_\\sigma$, and CSR is consistent with the standard presentation in Youd et al. (2001) and Idriss and Boulanger (2008), confirmed by web search results. The qualification that blast loading requires adaptation of the number-of-cycles parameter is consistent with the documented shorter duration of blast events compared with earthquake events, as referenced in the blast-induced liquefaction literature found in the web search results. No codified universal blast-liquefaction screening procedure was identified in any source retrieved.]"," "," Pore-pressure monitoring programs serve as the empirical counterpart to analytical liquefaction screening. ICOLD Bulletin 104 (1996) and the MAC OMS framework require piezometer instrumentation within TSF embankments and tailings deposits to measure in-situ pore pressure, detect excess pore-pressure generation following dynamic events, and track pore-pressure dissipation rates. [WEB:https://mining.ca/resources/guides-manuals/a-guide-to-the-management-of-tailings-facilities-version-3-1-2019/] The monitoring data serve to calibrate and validate analytical models, to detect anomalous pore-pressure responses that may indicate incipient instability, and to confirm that measured vibration events do not produce lasting pore-pressure accumulation above the thresholds defined in the OMS manual. Acceptance criteria for pore-pressure response are project-specific, defined within the OMS manual or instrumentation response plan as trigger values and alarm thresholds derived from site-specific stability calculations rather than adopted from any generic standard or building-vibration criterion.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: ICOLD Bulletin 104 (1996) as a monitoring guideline for tailings dams is confirmed by the web search results. The MAC OMS framework and its requirement for documented monitoring including piezometric response is confirmed by the MAC Guide URL and summary descriptions. The characterisation of acceptance criteria as project-specific is consistent with all surveyed guidance documents, none of which supply a generic pore-pressure threshold for blast-event monitoring at TSFs.]"," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01VZ7HtEUCqp4qFr3EAk1yie","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01UQiMC1vE2MszDJMSnK2sKv","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/averrik/Cloud/github/projects/AR-S2J2J.R/TITO/sessions/run-20260706_164057_11000/T8.research.md","old_string":"\"Pore-pressure generation\" and \"excess pore pressure\" are the standard geotechnical terms for the increase in pore-water pressure above the in-situ hydrostatic equilibrium produced by undrained cyclic or transient loading. ICOLD Bulletin 98 (1995) and the Idriss and Boulanger (2008) monograph use this terminology to describe the fundamental mechanism through which dynamic loading may degrade the effective stress and shear strength of saturated tailings. [WEB:https://www.researchgate.net/publication/288624104_Soil_liquefaction_during_earthquakes] The normalised excess pore-pressure ratio is defined as:\n\n$$r_u = \\frac{\\Delta u}{\\sigma'_{v0}}$$\n\nwhere $\\Delta u$ is the excess pore-water pressure generated by the dynamic event and $\\sigma'_{v0}$ is the initial effective vertical stress. The ratio $r_u$ is the key performance indicator in both instrumented piezometric monitoring and in constitutive models used for dynamic numerical analysis. As $r_u$ approaches unity, the effective stress approaches zero and the undrained shear strength is substantially reduced, with the consequence for stability depending on whether the residual state is contractive or dilative.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The terms \"pore-pressure generation\" and \"excess pore pressure\" and the definition of $r_u = \\Delta u / \\sigma'_{v0}$ are standard in geotechnical engineering and in the referenced monograph (Idriss and Boulanger, 2008). The Idriss and Boulanger (2008) reference is confirmed by multiple web search results. The interpretation of $r_u$ approaching unity as corresponding to near-zero effective stress and strength reduction is a direct consequence of the effective-stress principle and is universally accepted in the geotechnical literature.]","new_string":"\"Pore-pressure generation\" and \"excess pore pressure\" are the standard geotechnical terms for the increase in pore-water pressure above the in-situ hydrostatic equilibrium produced by undrained cyclic or transient loading. ICOLD Bulletin 98 (1995) and the Idriss and Boulanger (2008) monograph use this terminology to describe the fundamental mechanism through which dynamic loading may degrade the effective stress and shear strength of saturated tailings, with the normalised excess pore-pressure ratio defined as:^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The terminology \"pore-pressure generation\" and \"excess pore pressure\" and their role in degrading effective stress under dynamic loading are consistent with the cited monograph (Idriss and Boulanger, 2008), confirmed by web search results. The attribution to ICOLD Bulletin 98 is consistent with that bulletin's documented subject matter (tailings dams and seismicity) as confirmed in the web search results. [WEB:https://www.researchgate.net/publication/288624104_Soil_liquefaction_during_earthquakes] This paragraph introduces the display equation below.]\n\n$$r_u = \\frac{\\Delta u}{\\sigma'_{v0}}$$\n\nwhere $\\Delta u$ is the excess pore-water pressure generated by the dynamic event and $\\sigma'_{v0}$ is the initial effective vertical stress. The ratio $r_u$ is the key performance indicator in both instrumented piezometric monitoring and in constitutive models used for dynamic numerical analysis. As $r_u$ approaches unity, the effective stress approaches zero and the undrained shear strength is substantially reduced, with the consequence for stability depending on whether the residual state is contractive or dilative.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The definition $r_u = \\Delta u / \\sigma'_{v0}$ and its interpretation as a measure of effective-stress degradation are standard in geotechnical engineering and are consistent with the Idriss and Boulanger (2008) monograph, confirmed by web search results. The physical consequence - near-zero effective stress as $r_u$ approaches unity - follows from the principle of effective stress and is universally accepted in the literature. The dependence of the stability consequence on contractive or dilative material state is consistent with the flow liquefaction versus cyclic mobility distinction addressed in the following paragraph.]"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11198,"cache_read_input_tokens":100960,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":11198,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":26,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"193e9b03-f0e0-48bb-82cd-24114a1d1cbc","uuid":"a00a4c56-c9f1-4f3e-98f7-7ee0464c7d95","request_id":"req_011CcmPWcrm7X2AR62oMWSMr"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UQiMC1vE2MszDJMSnK2sKv","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/averrik/Cloud/github/projects/AR-S2J2J.R/TITO/sessions/run-20260706_164057_11000/T8.research.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"193e9b03-f0e0-48bb-82cd-24114a1d1cbc","uuid":"edbf7c48-2841-4ce9-8b1a-d73c28ec3aa2","timestamp":"2026-07-06T19:54:32.008Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"/Users/averrik/Cloud/github/projects/AR-S2J2J.R/TITO/sessions/run-20260706_164057_11000/T8.research.md","oldString":"\"Pore-pressure generation\" and \"excess pore pressure\" are the standard geotechnical terms for the increase in pore-water pressure above the in-situ hydrostatic equilibrium produced by undrained cyclic or transient loading. ICOLD Bulletin 98 (1995) and the Idriss and Boulanger (2008) monograph use this terminology to describe the fundamental mechanism through which dynamic loading may degrade the effective stress and shear strength of saturated tailings. [WEB:https://www.researchgate.net/publication/288624104_Soil_liquefaction_during_earthquakes] The normalised excess pore-pressure ratio is defined as:\n\n$$r_u = \\frac{\\Delta u}{\\sigma'_{v0}}$$\n\nwhere $\\Delta u$ is the excess pore-water pressure generated by the dynamic event and $\\sigma'_{v0}$ is the initial effective vertical stress. The ratio $r_u$ is the key performance indicator in both instrumented piezometric monitoring and in constitutive models used for dynamic numerical analysis. As $r_u$ approaches unity, the effective stress approaches zero and the undrained shear strength is substantially reduced, with the consequence for stability depending on whether the residual state is contractive or dilative.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The terms \"pore-pressure generation\" and \"excess pore pressure\" and the definition of $r_u = \\Delta u / \\sigma'_{v0}$ are standard in geotechnical engineering and in the referenced monograph (Idriss and Boulanger, 2008). The Idriss and Boulanger (2008) reference is confirmed by multiple web search results. The interpretation of $r_u$ approaching unity as corresponding to near-zero effective stress and strength reduction is a direct consequence of the effective-stress principle and is universally accepted in the geotechnical literature.]","newString":"\"Pore-pressure generation\" and \"excess pore pressure\" are the standard geotechnical terms for the increase in pore-water pressure above the in-situ hydrostatic equilibrium produced by undrained cyclic or transient loading. ICOLD Bulletin 98 (1995) and the Idriss and Boulanger (2008) monograph use this terminology to describe the fundamental mechanism through which dynamic loading may degrade the effective stress and shear strength of saturated tailings, with the normalised excess pore-pressure ratio defined as:^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The terminology \"pore-pressure generation\" and \"excess pore pressure\" and their role in degrading effective stress under dynamic loading are consistent with the cited monograph (Idriss and Boulanger, 2008), confirmed by web search results. The attribution to ICOLD Bulletin 98 is consistent with that bulletin's documented subject matter (tailings dams and seismicity) as confirmed in the web search results. [WEB:https://www.researchgate.net/publication/288624104_Soil_liquefaction_during_earthquakes] This paragraph introduces the display equation below.]\n\n$$r_u = \\frac{\\Delta u}{\\sigma'_{v0}}$$\n\nwhere $\\Delta u$ is the excess pore-water pressure generated by the dynamic event and $\\sigma'_{v0}$ is the initial effective vertical stress. The ratio $r_u$ is the key performance indicator in both instrumented piezometric monitoring and in constitutive models used for dynamic numerical analysis. As $r_u$ approaches unity, the effective stress approaches zero and the undrained shear strength is substantially reduced, with the consequence for stability depending on whether the residual state is contractive or dilative.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The definition $r_u = \\Delta u / \\sigma'_{v0}$ and its interpretation as a measure of effective-stress degradation are standard in geotechnical engineering and are consistent with the Idriss and Boulanger (2008) monograph, confirmed by web search results. The physical consequence - near-zero effective stress as $r_u$ approaches unity - follows from the principle of effective stress and is universally accepted in the literature. The dependence of the stability consequence on contractive or dilative material state is consistent with the flow liquefaction versus cyclic mobility distinction addressed in the following paragraph.]","originalFile":"# Guidance Documents for Blasting Effects on Tailings Storage Facilities: AR-S2J2J.R\n\n---\n\n### SLOT 1: Authoritative Guidance Documents Covering Blasting Effects on TSFs\n\nThe international industry standards that most directly address dynamic loading, vibration monitoring, pore-pressure response, and liquefaction screening for tailings storage facilities are the Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management (GISTM, 2020) and the IFC Environmental, Health, and Safety Guidelines for Mining (2007). The GISTM, developed under the auspices of the United Nations Environment Programme, the International Council on Mining and Metals, and the Principles for Responsible Investment, requires operators to establish an interdisciplinary knowledge base encompassing geotechnical and seismicity information, to document design criteria explicitly, and to implement integrated performance monitoring throughout the facility life cycle. [KB:T1.compile.research.md] The standard does not prescribe fixed numerical vibration thresholds for blast-induced loading on TSFs; it frames safety as a risk-management obligation demanding project-specific assessment and continuous monitoring as the means of demonstrating performance. The IFC EHS Guidelines for Mining (2007) require that tailings impoundments and dams be planned, designed, and operated such that geotechnical risks are assessed and managed throughout the mine life cycle, and they require designs to reflect internationally recognised engineering practices and local seismic conditions, treating geotechnical risk assessment - including blast-induced ground motion effects on tailings facilities - as a distinct discipline rather than an extension of building-vibration compliance. [KB:T1.compile.research.md]^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: Both GISTM and IFC EHS Mining Guidelines are confirmed in the KB (T1.compile.research.md) with content that is consistent with established characterisations of these documents. The claim that neither prescribes fixed blast vibration thresholds for TSFs is directly supported by the KB statement that GISTM requires an interdisciplinary knowledge base and performance monitoring rather than fixed limits, and by the IFC guidance treating tailings facilities as requiring risk assessment and internationally recognised engineering practice.]\n\nThe ANCOLD Guidelines on Tailings Dams, Revision 1 (2019), published by the Australian National Committee on Large Dams, provide the most comprehensive single-document treatment of dynamic and seismic loading for tailings storage facilities among the available authoritative sources. [WEB:https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/] The 2019 revision explicitly aligns tailings dam seismic design criteria with the companion ANCOLD Guidelines for Design of Dams and Appurtenant Structures for Earthquake (2019), addresses both static and cyclic liquefaction of saturated tailings, and recommends conservatively assuming that static liquefaction will occur for brittle, contractive, saturated tailings unless site-specific evidence supports a less conservative classification. The document addresses planning, design, construction, operation, and closure, treating earthquake loading and factor-of-safety requirements under dynamic conditions as subjects requiring engineering analysis throughout the facility life cycle rather than as compliance thresholds satisfied by reference to a look-up table.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The ANCOLD Guidelines on Tailings Dams Revision 1 (2019) are confirmed by web search results including the official ANCOLD product page and the publicly available 2019 addendum text. The specific content claims - alignment with earthquake design guidelines, treatment of static and cyclic liquefaction, conservative assumption of static liquefaction for contractive tailings - are each corroborated by the search results, which quote these provisions directly.]\n\nICOLD has produced a series of bulletins that collectively constitute the authoritative international technical foundation for seismic assessment of tailings dams. ICOLD Bulletin 98 (1995), Tailings Dams and Seismicity, addresses the seismic behavior of tailings facilities specifically, covering liquefaction potential, dynamic stability, and modes of failure associated with cyclic ground motion. ICOLD Bulletin 104 (1996), Monitoring of Tailings Dams, establishes the framework for instrumented monitoring programs, including piezometric surveillance of pore-pressure response in TSF embankments. ICOLD Bulletin 121 (2001), Tailings Dams - Risk of Dangerous Occurrences, documents lessons from actual failures including seismically triggered and liquefaction-related events. ICOLD Bulletin 148 (2011), Selecting Seismic Parameters for Large Dams, provides guidance on seismic hazard characterisation and the selection of design earthquake parameters applicable to large storage facilities including tailings dams. [WEB:https://www.icoldchile.cl/boletines/148.pdf] ICOLD Bulletin 194 (2022), Tailings Dam Safety, is the most recent unified ICOLD statement on tailings dam governance and engineering, consolidating seismic requirements alongside operational and monitoring obligations in a form intended to supersede and integrate the earlier bulletins for contemporary professional practice. [WEB:https://klohn.com/wp-content/uploads/B194-ICOLD-Tailings-Dam-Safety.pdf]^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The ICOLD bulletins listed (98, 104, 121, 148, 194) are confirmed through multiple independent web search results. Bulletins 98 and 104 are specifically named in the ICOLD bulletin sequence summaries returned by the search. Bulletin 148 is available as a PDF from the ICOLD Chile chapter. Bulletin 194 is referenced in a UNECE presentation and available through the Klohn Crippen Berger website. The attribution of topics (seismicity, monitoring, risk of dangerous occurrences, seismic parameters, tailings safety) to the respective bulletins is consistent across multiple search results.]\n\nISO 4866:2010, Mechanical Vibration and Shock - Vibration of Fixed Structures, provides the foundational measurement methodology for blast-vibration monitoring programs. [KB:T1.compile.research.md] The standard applies to structures above and below ground including earth structures such as dykes and embankments, and addresses sources including explosions and construction activities; however, it explicitly excludes certain special structures, including dams, from its scope. Because a TSF is typically classified as a dam for regulatory and engineering purposes, ISO 4866:2010 is directly applicable as the technical framework for blast-vibration transducer placement, waveform acquisition, and frequency-content characterisation in TSF monitoring programs, while falling short of providing structural acceptance criteria for the facility itself. The standard's exclusion of dams from its direct scope is relevant to report framing: ISO 4866:2010 supports the measurement methodology but does not establish whether a measured vibration level is acceptable for a particular TSF, which remains a matter of project-specific engineering assessment.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The characterisation of ISO 4866:2010 - its applicability to earth structures including dykes and embankments, its coverage of explosion sources, and its specific exclusion of dams - is drawn directly from the KB (T1.compile.research.md), which is treated as authoritative for this audit step. The inference that a TSF classified as a dam falls outside the direct compliance scope of ISO 4866 follows from the stated scope exclusion in the standard itself and is not contradicted by any other source cited in this document.]\n\nThe geotechnical liquefaction-assessment literature constitutes an integral part of the analytical framework for evaluating the response of saturated tailings to blast-induced dynamic loading, even though that literature was developed primarily for earthquake loading scenarios. Youd et al. (2001), summarising the consensus reached at the 1996 NCEER and 1998 NCEER/NSF workshops on evaluation of liquefaction resistance of soils, established the simplified stress-based framework in which the cyclic stress ratio (CSR) representing dynamic demand is compared with the cyclic resistance ratio (CRR) representing soil resistance to produce a factor of safety against liquefaction triggering. [WEB:https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/23474/chapter/6] Idriss and Boulanger (2008), Soil Liquefaction During Earthquakes, updated and refined those procedures with revised magnitude scaling factors, improved handling of low-plasticity fine-grained soils of the type commonly encountered in tailings, and updated CPT and SPT-based CRR correlations. [WEB:https://www.researchgate.net/publication/288624104_Soil_liquefaction_during_earthquakes] Both frameworks are designed for earthquake-induced cyclic loading, and their application to blast-induced ground motion requires project-specific adaptation of the loading characterisation, particularly the estimation of equivalent uniform cycles associated with a transient blast event.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The Youd et al. (2001) and Idriss and Boulanger (2008) frameworks are widely documented authoritative references in the geotechnical engineering literature. Their content - CSR/CRR framework, magnitude scaling factors, CPT and SPT correlations, treatment of fine-grained soils - is confirmed by multiple web search results, including the National Academies Press summary and multiple faculty PDFs. The qualification that adaptation is needed for blasting scenarios is standard geotechnical practice and is consistent with the absence of any blast-specific version of these frameworks in the search results.]\n\nThe Mining Association of Canada's Guide to the Management of Tailings Facilities, Version 3.1 (2019), provides an operations-oriented framework for TSF safety that complements the design-phase guidance of ANCOLD and ICOLD. [WEB:https://mining.ca/resources/guides-manuals/a-guide-to-the-management-of-tailings-facilities-version-3-1-2019/] The MAC guide structures TSF management through an Operation, Maintenance and Surveillance (OMS) manual framework that requires documented monitoring of all identified performance indicators, including instrumented pore-pressure and vibration responses where blasting occurs in proximity to a TSF. While the MAC guide does not prescribe fixed quantitative vibration thresholds for blast events, its OMS framework establishes the operational mechanism by which project-specific monitoring trigger levels and alarm thresholds are defined, communicated to operators, and updated in response to observed performance.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The MAC Guide to the Management of Tailings Facilities Version 3.1 (2019) is confirmed via the official Mining Association of Canada website URL found in the web search results. The OMS framework characterisation is consistent with publicly available descriptions of the MAC guide and with the general practice in tailings management. The claim that the guide does not prescribe fixed vibration thresholds is consistent with its governance-and-management focus, which is confirmed by the search result descriptions of its content.]\n\n---\n\n### SLOT 2: South African versus International Applicability of Sources\n\nWithin the South African regulatory environment, the primary legislative instrument governing mining blasting is the Mine Health and Safety Act 29 of 1996 (MHSA), which requires mine operators to prepare and implement a site-specific mandatory Code of Practice addressing health and safety risks associated with their particular operations. The Department of Mineral Resources and Energy issued the Guideline for a Mandatory Code of Practice for Minimum Standards on Ground Vibrations, Noise, Air-blast, and Flyrock near Surface Structures and Communities to be Protected in August 2024, establishing the current national baseline for blast-vibration control in South African mining under sections 9(2) and 9(3) of the MHSA. [WEB:https://cer.org.za/virtual-library/legislation/national/mining/mine-blasting-guideline-code-of-practice-for-minimum-standards-on-ground-vibrations-noise-air-blast-and-flyrock-near-surface-structures-and-communities-to-be-protected-2024] This guideline defines protected structures and communities in the context of surface mining, and operators must demonstrate compliance through a site-specific COP that describes monitoring arrangements and acceptable vibration levels for identified sensitive receivers. The 2024 DMRE blasting guideline is South African in origin and legally mandated, making it the primary regulatory compliance reference for blasting vibration limits in a South African mining project, including projects where blasting occurs in proximity to a TSF.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The existence and content of the South African MHSA (1996) and the DMRE August 2024 Guideline for a Mandatory Code of Practice are confirmed by web search results, including the Centre for Environmental Rights website entry and the Government Gazette reference. The MHSA sections cited (9(2) and 9(3)) are consistent with multiple South African legal references found in the search results. The characterisation of the guideline as defining protected structures and requiring site-specific COPs is consistent with the regulatory framework described in the search results.]\n\nThe South African National Committee on Large Dams (SANCOLD) has produced guidance specifically directed at the South African context for tailings dam management. The SANCOLD document \"Your Tailings Dam\" (2020) addresses design, operation, and safety monitoring of tailings facilities within the South African legislative environment and explicitly notes two significant gaps in the local regulatory framework: first, that South African legislation does not prescribe the severity or return period of a seismic event that a tailings dam must withstand, leaving that determination as a matter of engineering judgement by the designer and owner; and second, that South African legislation does not yet directly address the GISTM requirements. [WEB:https://sancold.org.za/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/SANCOLD-Your-Tailings-Dam-2020.pdf] These observations are directly relevant to report framing: a project operating under South African jurisdiction cannot assume that the seismic or dynamic-loading acceptance criteria in any international standard have been adopted directly into South African law, and the engineer of record must state the basis on which any seismic performance target has been chosen.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The SANCOLD \"Your Tailings Dam\" (2020) document is confirmed by a web search result citing the SANCOLD website URL. The specific claims - that South African legislation does not prescribe seismic return periods for tailings dams and that it does not yet address GISTM - are drawn from the web search result text, which directly quotes or paraphrases these findings from the SANCOLD document. These are material observations specific to the South African regulatory context.]\n\nThe international sources - GISTM, ANCOLD (2019), ICOLD bulletins 98, 104, 121, 148, and 194, the IFC EHS Guidelines for Mining, and ISO 4866:2010 - are applicable across jurisdictions as statements of good international practice and are accepted as technical reference documents for independent review in South Africa. [KB:T1.compile.research.md] [WEB:https://www.icmm.com/en-gb/our-principles/tailings/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management] Their adoption in a South African TSF report is appropriate as technical framing for engineering assessment, and lenders, financiers, and independent review panels routinely require alignment with GISTM and ICOLD guidance. However, the specific numerical targets in those documents - such as the ANCOLD minimum factor of safety of 1.1 under seismic loading, or earthquake return periods referenced in ICOLD Bulletin 148 - derive from Australian or international regulatory contexts and should be treated as engineering references rather than as South African legal requirements unless the project permit, lender technical schedules, or the Engineer of Record explicitly adopts them as binding project standards.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The categorisation of GISTM, ANCOLD, ICOLD, IFC EHS, and ISO 4866 as international references applicable across jurisdictions is well-established and confirmed by multiple web sources. The SANCOLD search result specifically confirms that South African legislation does not adopt GISTM directly, supporting the qualification that international numerical criteria are not automatically legally binding in South Africa. The mention of lender requirements for GISTM alignment is consistent with general knowledge of equator principles and DFI practice, though no specific URL confirming that point appears in the search results; the confidence rating reflects the direct documentary support for the primary regulatory gap claim.]\n\nThe U.S. Office of Surface Mining Reclamation and Enforcement rules in 30 CFR Part 816 (Sections 816.67 and 816.68) are a mature regulatory example of how a blasting framework distinguishes fixed PPV thresholds for standard occupied structures from a project-specific approval process for other structures, including dams, impoundments, utilities, and underground mines. [KB:T1.compile.research.md] Under 30 CFR 816.67, the vibration limits for dams and impoundments are not automatically derived from the tabulated building thresholds but are submitted in the blasting plan and approved by the regulator on a project-specific basis. This regulatory architecture - separate fixed limits for standard receivers and a project-specific submission process for engineered earth structures - illustrates the principle that South African practice approximates through the MHSA COP process, even though the South African framework is articulated differently and does not reproduce the specific numerical values or definitional conventions of the U.S. regulations. Citing 30 CFR in a South African TSF report is appropriate as an international regulatory analogue illustrating best-practice structure, provided the report clearly identifies it as a U.S. federal rule rather than as a standard applicable in South Africa.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The characterisation of 30 CFR 816.67 and 816.68 is drawn directly from the KB (T1.compile.research.md), which is treated as authoritative for this audit step. The KB explicitly states that 30 CFR 816.67 provides project-specific limits for dams and impoundments submitted in the blasting plan rather than adopting the building-level thresholds. The inference that the South African COP process approximates this architecture is a supported analogy, not a legally equivalent statement, and is appropriately qualified.]\n\n---\n\n### SLOT 3: Quantitative Acceptance Criteria versus Project-Specific Assessment\n\nNone of the primary authoritative sources for TSF safety - GISTM, ANCOLD (2019), ICOLD bulletins 98, 104, 121, 148, and 194, or the IFC EHS Guidelines for Mining - prescribes a universal fixed blast-vibration threshold expressed as a limiting peak particle velocity for protecting a tailings storage facility. The absence of a universal vibration limit for TSFs reflects the established recognition in geotechnical dam engineering that the dynamic response of a saturated tailings facility depends on material properties, fabric, degree of saturation, drainage conditions, confining stress, pore-pressure history, the geometry of the embankment, and the proximity and characteristics of the blasting source. None of these site-specific factors are captured by a generic PPV criterion derived from research on vibration effects in rigid or masonry building structures. [KB:T1.compile.research.md] [WEB:https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/]^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The claim that none of the listed authoritative TSF documents prescribes a universal blast PPV limit is strongly supported. The KB (T1.compile.research.md) states that 30 CFR requires project-specific plan submission for dams and impoundments rather than a tabulated threshold; GISTM and IFC EHS are characterised as requiring risk assessment and project-specific criteria. The ANCOLD web search results describe factor-of-safety and earthquake-return-period criteria but no universal PPV limit for blasting on TSFs. No web source retrieved a fixed PPV acceptance criterion for TSFs from any of the listed documents.]\n\nANCOLD (2019) provides the most explicit quantitative framing available for TSF seismic performance, but its criteria are expressed in terms of structural stability factors of safety and design earthquake return periods rather than in terms of blast-vibration amplitude. The guidelines prescribe a minimum factor of safety of 1.1 for transient and seismic loading conditions, and recommend evaluating the facility against a 1:10,000 annual exceedance probability earthquake where liquefaction of tailings is likely to affect the stability of the embankment, unless a site-specific risk assessment demonstrates that an adequate risk profile is achieved with a lower seismic loading. [WEB:https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/] These quantitative targets are outputs of a project-specific engineering process - geotechnical characterisation, in-situ testing, liquefaction potential assessment, and dynamic stability analysis - and are not simplified look-up values. Applying them to blast-induced loading requires translating the blast-vibration record into equivalent dynamic demand parameters consistent with the geotechnical analysis framework, which involves project-specific assumptions about wave attenuation, loading duration, and equivalent cyclic cycles.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The ANCOLD (2019) factor-of-safety value of 1.1 for seismic conditions and the 1:10,000 AEP earthquake recommendation for liquefiable tailings are confirmed by two independent web search results: the ANCOLD product page summary and the SANCOLD web search result, which cites a minimum FOS of 1.1 for transient/seismic conditions in South African guidance citing ANCOLD practice. The conclusion that these criteria presuppose project-specific geotechnical analysis follows directly from the stated content of ANCOLD.]\n\nLiquefaction screening for saturated tailings under dynamic loading is performed within the simplified stress-based framework of Youd et al. (2001) or Idriss and Boulanger (2008), in which the cyclic stress ratio (CSR), representing the demand imposed by the dynamic event, is compared with the cyclic resistance ratio (CRR), representing the soil resistance to liquefaction triggering derived from in-situ penetration tests such as the SPT or CPT. [WEB:https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/23474/chapter/6] [WEB:https://www.researchgate.net/publication/288624104_Soil_liquefaction_during_earthquakes] The factor of safety against liquefaction is defined as:^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The simplified stress-based liquefaction screening framework of Youd et al. (2001) and Idriss and Boulanger (2008) is confirmed by multiple web search results including the National Academies Press and University of California Davis faculty documents. The description of SPT and CPT as the standard in-situ test methods for deriving CRR is consistent with those sources. This paragraph introduces the display equation below and contains no independent factual claim beyond the framework description, which is well-supported.]\n\n$$FS_L = \\frac{CRR_{7.5} \\cdot MSF \\cdot K_\\sigma}{CSR}$$\n\nwhere $CRR_{7.5}$ is the cyclic resistance ratio normalised to a reference earthquake magnitude of 7.5, $MSF$ is the magnitude scaling factor correcting for earthquake duration, and $K_\\sigma$ is the overburden correction factor. When blast-induced loading rather than earthquake loading is the triggering mechanism, the CSR must be estimated from the blast-induced ground motion waveform and a representative equivalent number of uniform loading cycles must be assigned to the transient event - a parameter that is far smaller for a blast than for a sustained earthquake of similar peak amplitude. This adaptation of the earthquake-based framework to blast loading requires project-specific assumptions on loading characterisation that are not codified in any universally adopted standard, making $FS_L$ a conditional engineering output dependent on those documented choices.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The definition of $FS_L$ with $CRR_{7.5}$, $MSF$, $K_\\sigma$, and CSR is consistent with the standard presentation in Youd et al. (2001) and Idriss and Boulanger (2008), confirmed by web search results. The qualification that blast loading requires adaptation of the number-of-cycles parameter is consistent with the documented shorter duration of blast events compared with earthquake events, as referenced in the blast-induced liquefaction literature found in the web search results. No codified universal blast-liquefaction screening procedure was identified in any source retrieved.]\n\nPore-pressure monitoring programs serve as the empirical counterpart to analytical liquefaction screening. ICOLD Bulletin 104 (1996) and the MAC OMS framework require piezometer instrumentation within TSF embankments and tailings deposits to measure in-situ pore pressure, detect excess pore-pressure generation following dynamic events, and track pore-pressure dissipation rates. [WEB:https://mining.ca/resources/guides-manuals/a-guide-to-the-management-of-tailings-facilities-version-3-1-2019/] The monitoring data serve to calibrate and validate analytical models, to detect anomalous pore-pressure responses that may indicate incipient instability, and to confirm that measured vibration events do not produce lasting pore-pressure accumulation above the thresholds defined in the OMS manual. Acceptance criteria for pore-pressure response are project-specific, defined within the OMS manual or instrumentation response plan as trigger values and alarm thresholds derived from site-specific stability calculations rather than adopted from any generic standard or building-vibration criterion.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: ICOLD Bulletin 104 (1996) as a monitoring guideline for tailings dams is confirmed by the web search results. The MAC OMS framework and its requirement for documented monitoring including piezometric response is confirmed by the MAC Guide URL and summary descriptions. The characterisation of acceptance criteria as project-specific is consistent with all surveyed guidance documents, none of which supply a generic pore-pressure threshold for blast-event monitoring at TSFs.]\n\n---\n\n### SLOT 4: Report Framing to Distinguish Criteria, Monitoring Requirements, and Assumptions\n\nA professional technical report on the effects of nearby blasting on a TSF must clearly delineate four distinct categories of statement: published reference criteria drawn from identified standards; project-specific criteria established for the facility in question by the Engineer of Record; monitoring requirements arising from regulatory obligations, best-practice standards, and design documentation; and analysis assumptions made in the assessment. Conflating these categories - in particular, applying a criterion developed for a different receiver type, such as a building-damage PPV threshold from a vibration standard, as though it governs TSF structural performance - misrepresents the state of professional practice and may produce technically indefensible conclusions that expose the report to challenge during regulatory review or independent assessment. [KB:T1.compile.research.md] [WEB:https://klohn.com/wp-content/uploads/B194-ICOLD-Tailings-Dam-Safety.pdf]^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The four-category framework for report organisation is consistent with the practice distinctions described throughout the cited sources: the KB (T1.compile.research.md) explicitly distinguishes structural damage criteria from human-exposure criteria and from monitoring/reporting requirements, and from project-specific plan submissions. ICOLD Bulletin 194 (2022), referenced via the Klohn Crippen Berger URL, consolidates governance and engineering requirements in a manner that requires explicit documentation of design criteria as a distinct category. The characterisation of conflation as a reportable risk is a professional judgement consistent with the regulatory framing described in the cited sources.]\n\nPublished reference criteria from building-vibration standards such as BS 7385-2:1993 and from human-comfort and human-exposure standards such as BS 6472-2:2008 or ISO 2631-2 do not govern TSF structural performance and are outside the scope of the present assessment. Those documents are explicitly scoped to building structures and to occupant physiological response respectively; none establishes an acceptance criterion for an earth structure such as a tailings embankment or impoundment. Presenting a PPV threshold derived from those standards as the criterion for protecting a TSF implies a scope of applicability that those standards do not claim, and it obscures the absence of a published fixed vibration threshold for the engineering question actually under assessment. ISO 4866:2010, which does include earth structures within certain parts of its scope, provides measurement and evaluation methodology but explicitly excludes dams and similarly does not prescribe limiting vibration amplitudes for earth embankments or tailings facilities. [KB:T1.compile.research.md] The report should therefore name these standards only in their correct application context - as references for the building-damage or human-comfort dimensions of any broader assessment - and should present the TSF structural-performance question as governed by the geotechnical assessment framework described by ANCOLD, ICOLD, GISTM, and the site-specific engineering analysis.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The scope of BS 7385-2 (building damage), BS 6472-2 (human blast exposure in buildings), and ISO 2631-2 (human annoyance and comfort) is confirmed in the KB (T1.compile.research.md), which explicitly states that BS 6472-2 \"does not address building damage\" and that ISO 2631-2 \"concerns comfort and annoyance from whole-body vibration in buildings\" and that \"structural damage is discussed in ISO 4866.\" The KB also confirms that ISO 4866 excludes dams from its scope. These are straightforward scope characterisations from the KB, treated as authoritative for this audit step.]\n\nThe GISTM (2020) and IFC EHS Guidelines for Mining (2007) frame TSF safety as a risk-management process in which operators define performance criteria embedded in facility design documentation and the OMS manual, rather than a compliance checklist satisfied by reference to tabulated limits. [KB:T1.compile.research.md] Within that framework, a project-specific blasting acceptance criterion for the TSF - expressed as a vibration amplitude limit, a pore-pressure trigger level, or a stability factor-of-safety threshold - is established through site-specific engineering assessment, agreed with the independent tailings review board or qualified person, and documented in the OMS manual. The report should name the document in which the project-specific criterion is established, state the basis on which it was derived (for example, the geotechnical analysis from which the pore-pressure trigger value follows, or the stability calculation from which the vibration limit was back-analysed), and distinguish it from both the general guidance documents and from the MHSA COP regulatory compliance thresholds, which address different protected receivers under different legal obligations.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The characterisation of GISTM and IFC EHS Mining as requiring operator-defined, documented performance criteria rather than fixed tabulated limits is confirmed by the KB (T1.compile.research.md) and by the web search results describing GISTM as requiring documented design criteria and integrated performance monitoring. The distinction between OMS-documented project-specific criteria and general guidance documents is consistent with the MAC OMS framework, which is confirmed via the MAC website URL in the search results.]\n\nMonitoring requirements constitute a distinct reporting category that should be presented as performance-verification tools operating alongside the analytical assessment, not as substitutes for it. ISO 4866:2010 provides the technical basis for the vibration monitoring methodology, specifying transducer placement, signal acquisition requirements, frequency-content characterisation, and data analysis procedures applicable to blast events affecting earth structures. [KB:T1.compile.research.md] ICOLD Bulletin 104 provides the framework for piezometric monitoring programs at tailings dams, and the MAC OMS framework prescribes the structure of an instrumentation response plan including defined trigger and alarm levels. These documents establish what must be measured and how, and they inform the thresholds at which measurements require a response; they do not themselves generate the acceptance criteria, which arise from site-specific engineering calculation.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The characterisation of ISO 4866 as covering blast-vibration measurement methodology, and of ICOLD Bulletin 104 as covering tailings dam piezometric monitoring, is supported by KB content (ISO 4866) and web search results (ICOLD 104). The MAC OMS framework is confirmed via the MAC website. The distinction between monitoring standards and acceptance criteria is consistent with the content of all cited sources and with the structure of the analytical-monitoring relationship described in the GISTM and IFC EHS frameworks.]\n\nAnalysis assumptions constitute the fourth category requiring transparent reporting. Where the earthquake-based liquefaction-screening frameworks of Youd et al. (2001) or Idriss and Boulanger (2008) are adapted to blast-induced cyclic loading, the project-specific choices that determine the computed factor of safety include the equivalent number of uniform cycles assigned to the blast event, the attenuation relationship used to estimate ground motion at the TSF, the representative penetration resistance profile used to derive CRR, the drainage assumptions applied to the tailings under the transient loading duration, and the specific CRR correlation selected. [WEB:https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/23474/chapter/6] [WEB:https://www.researchgate.net/publication/288624104_Soil_liquefaction_during_earthquakes] The report should state these assumptions explicitly, distinguish them from established values in the referenced standards, and present the sensitivity of the conclusions to key parameter choices, so that the engineering judgement embedded in the analysis is transparent and auditable. The absence of a codified blast-liquefaction screening procedure in any of the authoritative guidance documents means that the report cannot defer responsibility for those assumptions to a named standard; they are engineering choices that require explicit justification.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The requirement to adapt earthquake-based liquefaction frameworks to blast loading by selecting equivalent cycle counts and other parameters is confirmed by the structure of the Youd et al. (2001) and Idriss and Boulanger (2008) frameworks as described in the web search results: those frameworks are explicitly earthquake-oriented, and blast-specific adaptations are not codified in any universal standard identified in this research. The list of project-specific assumptions is consistent with standard geotechnical practice for applying simplified liquefaction methods.]\n\n---\n\n### SLOT 5: Source-Backed Terminology for TSF Response to Blasting\n\nThe term \"dynamic loading\" appears in the ICOLD bulletins on tailings dams and in the ANCOLD guidelines with a consistent meaning: any time-varying force or acceleration applied to the structure, its embankment, or its foundation, encompassing earthquake-induced ground motion, blast-induced ground vibration, and in some formulations operational loads from heavy equipment. [WEB:https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/] [WEB:https://klohn.com/wp-content/uploads/B194-ICOLD-Tailings-Dam-Safety.pdf] In the tailings dam literature, dynamic loading is treated as a stability-relevant load case that must be considered alongside static loading in the design and performance assessment of the facility and that may govern design where the tailings or foundation material is susceptible to strength loss under cyclic excitation. The term does not carry an implication about the source of the loading; it is applicable to both seismic and blast events provided the dynamic nature of the loading is established.^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The use of \"dynamic loading\" in the context of ANCOLD and ICOLD tailings dam guidance is consistent with the content of those documents as confirmed by web search results. However, the specific application to both seismic and blast events in the same ANCOLD or ICOLD text is not directly quoted in the retrieved search results, which describe seismic loading primarily. The characterisation is a reasonable and professionally defensible extrapolation of the term's standard technical meaning in geotechnical engineering.]\n\nThe term \"transient vibration\" is used in the vibration measurement literature, including ISO 4866:2010, to describe a vibration event of finite duration that does not persist as continuous or steady-state excitation. [KB:T1.compile.research.md] Blast-induced ground motion constitutes a transient vibration event: it consists of a short-duration stress-wave sequence generated by detonation, characterised by a rapidly rising wavefront, a frequency spectrum determined by the charge weight, delay pattern, and geological propagation path, and a decay envelope that is a function of the scaled distance to the receptor. The term is appropriate for characterising the nature of the blast-induced ground motion as a physical input to the TSF assessment but carries no connotations about the structural response or the stability outcome; those are assessed through the geotechnical analysis.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The characterisation of ISO 4866:2010 and the term \"transient vibration\" is drawn directly from the KB (T1.compile.research.md), which is treated as authoritative. The description of blast-induced ground motion as a transient vibration event with a rising wavefront, frequency content, and decay with scaled distance is consistent with standard blast seismology practice and is supported implicitly by the ISO 4866 coverage of explosive sources described in the KB.]\n\n\"Pore-pressure generation\" and \"excess pore pressure\" are the standard geotechnical terms for the increase in pore-water pressure above the in-situ hydrostatic equilibrium produced by undrained cyclic or transient loading. ICOLD Bulletin 98 (1995) and the Idriss and Boulanger (2008) monograph use this terminology to describe the fundamental mechanism through which dynamic loading may degrade the effective stress and shear strength of saturated tailings. [WEB:https://www.researchgate.net/publication/288624104_Soil_liquefaction_during_earthquakes] The normalised excess pore-pressure ratio is defined as:\n\n$$r_u = \\frac{\\Delta u}{\\sigma'_{v0}}$$\n\nwhere $\\Delta u$ is the excess pore-water pressure generated by the dynamic event and $\\sigma'_{v0}$ is the initial effective vertical stress. The ratio $r_u$ is the key performance indicator in both instrumented piezometric monitoring and in constitutive models used for dynamic numerical analysis. As $r_u$ approaches unity, the effective stress approaches zero and the undrained shear strength is substantially reduced, with the consequence for stability depending on whether the residual state is contractive or dilative.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The terms \"pore-pressure generation\" and \"excess pore pressure\" and the definition of $r_u = \\Delta u / \\sigma'_{v0}$ are standard in geotechnical engineering and in the referenced monograph (Idriss and Boulanger, 2008). The Idriss and Boulanger (2008) reference is confirmed by multiple web search results. The interpretation of $r_u$ approaching unity as corresponding to near-zero effective stress and strength reduction is a direct consequence of the effective-stress principle and is universally accepted in the geotechnical literature.]\n\n\"Cyclic mobility\" and \"flow liquefaction\" are the two principal distinct failure modes associated with excess pore-pressure generation in saturated soils under cyclic loading, and their distinction is essential for accurate TSF reporting. Flow liquefaction occurs in contractive soils - including loose, saturated, fine-grained tailings - where the residual undrained shear strength mobilised after triggering is insufficient to maintain stability under the existing static shear stresses, resulting in a catastrophic, sustained loss of resistance and runout. Cyclic mobility occurs in soils that retain a positive residual undrained shear strength after triggering, producing transient loss of effective stress and large cyclic strains without a sustained flow failure. [WEB:https://itascasoftware.com/resources/when-cyclic-mobility-isnt-flow-liquefaction/] ICOLD Bulletin 98 and the Idriss and Boulanger (2008) framework distinguish these mechanisms, and ANCOLD (2019) recommends conservatively assuming flow liquefaction for saturated, brittle, contractive tailings absent evidence supporting a less severe classification. [WEB:https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/] In a technical report, the failure mode being assessed should be named explicitly, and the material evidence - penetration resistance profile, gradation, plasticity index - that supports the classification should be cited.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The distinction between flow liquefaction and cyclic mobility is a well-established geotechnical concept confirmed by the web search results, including the Itasca resource and the general liquefaction literature. The ANCOLD recommendation to assume flow liquefaction conservatively for contractive saturated tailings is confirmed by the ANCOLD search result, which directly quotes this provision. The ICOLD Bulletin 98 attribution is confirmed by the search results identifying that bulletin's subject matter as tailings dams and seismicity.]\n\n\"Liquefaction potential\" is used in the Youd et al. (2001) framework and in the ICOLD tailings dam bulletins to describe the susceptibility of a saturated soil deposit to liquefaction triggering under a specified dynamic loading. [WEB:https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/23474/chapter/6] The quantitative measure of liquefaction potential is the factor of safety against triggering:\n\n$$FS_L = \\frac{CRR_{7.5} \\cdot MSF \\cdot K_\\sigma}{CSR}$$\n\nwhere $CRR_{7.5}$ is the cyclic resistance ratio corrected to a reference earthquake magnitude of $M_w = 7.5$, $MSF$ is the magnitude scaling factor that adjusts for the duration and equivalent cycles of the actual event, and $K_\\sigma$ is the overburden correction factor. When a blast event rather than an earthquake provides the dynamic demand, the CSR must be estimated from the blast-induced ground motion parameters and a representative equivalent number of uniform stress cycles must be assigned to the transient loading, making $FS_L$ a conditional quantity whose value is governed by these project-specific input choices. Values of $FS_L > 1$ indicate that the resistance exceeds the demand for the stated assumptions, while $FS_L \\leq 1$ indicates a triggering condition; the margin separating these states should be reported alongside the governing assumptions.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The Youd et al. (2001) framework, the definition of $FS_L$ in terms of $CRR_{7.5}$, $MSF$, $K_\\sigma$, and CSR, and the characterisation of $FS_L$ as a conditional output are confirmed by multiple web search results including the National Academies Press and University of California Davis resources. The formula structure is consistent with standard presentations. The conditional qualification for blast loading is consistent with the absence of a codified blast-adaptation procedure in any source retrieved.]\n\n\"Deformation\" and \"stability\" are used in the ANCOLD guidelines and ICOLD bulletins as distinct but related performance parameters for tailings dam assessment under dynamic loading. Stability refers to the resistance of the TSF embankment and its foundation to slope failure, evaluated as a factor of safety against global instability; ANCOLD (2019) prescribes a minimum value of 1.1 under transient seismic conditions as a quantitative performance target. [WEB:https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/] Deformation refers to the permanent displacements accumulated during a dynamic event, evaluated through analyses such as the Newmark sliding-block method or fully coupled dynamic numerical analysis; it is possible for a structure to remain nominally stable - maintaining a factor of safety above unity throughout the event - while accumulating permanent deformations that damage the crest, impair drainage elements, or compromise the freeboard required for safe operation. A technical report should distinguish stability assessment from deformation assessment and should state explicitly which analyses have been performed, which have been omitted, and why.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The distinction between deformation and stability as performance parameters in dam engineering is a standard professional convention supported by the ANCOLD guidelines (confirmed via web search) and consistent with ICOLD practice. The ANCOLD FS of 1.1 for seismic conditions is confirmed by both the ANCOLD product page search result and the SANCOLD search result. The Newmark sliding-block method for deformation estimation is a standard geotechnical reference that is consistent with the ANCOLD search result content.]\n\n\"Monitoring\" in the TSF context encompasses instrumented surveillance programs designed to detect developing adverse conditions before they progress to a breach or failure event. [WEB:https://mining.ca/resources/guides-manuals/a-guide-to-the-management-of-tailings-facilities-version-3-1-2019/] [KB:T1.compile.research.md] The GISTM and the IFC EHS Mining Guidelines require integrated performance monitoring programs that include piezometric measurement of pore pressure within the embankment and tailings mass, geotechnical surface monitoring of crest and slope displacement, and vibration monitoring of blast events affecting the facility. In the specific context of blasting effects on a TSF, monitoring serves two distinct functions: a compliance-verification function, demonstrating that measured blast-vibration parameters do not exceed the project-specific thresholds documented in the OMS manual and required by the MHSA COP; and a geotechnical feedback function, providing measured pore-pressure response data that can be compared with model predictions and used to update or validate the analytical assessment of liquefaction potential. A report that presents monitoring results should identify which of these functions the presented data serve and should confirm that the monitoring instrumentation, acquisition parameters, and analysis method are consistent with the requirements of the applicable standard or guidance document, including ISO 4866:2010 for vibration data and ICOLD Bulletin 104 for piezometric data.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The dual function of monitoring - regulatory compliance verification and geotechnical feedback - is consistent with the combined requirements of GISTM (confirmed via KB and ICMM web search result), IFC EHS Mining (confirmed via KB and IFC website search results), MAC OMS framework (confirmed via MAC website), and the MHSA COP requirement (confirmed via DMRE 2024 guideline web search result). The reference to ISO 4866 for vibration data and ICOLD Bulletin 104 for piezometric data is consistent with the content of those documents as confirmed in the KB and web search results respectively.]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":66,"oldLines":11,"newStart":66,"newLines":11,"lines":[" "," The term \"transient vibration\" is used in the vibration measurement literature, including ISO 4866:2010, to describe a vibration event of finite duration that does not persist as continuous or steady-state excitation. [KB:T1.compile.research.md] Blast-induced ground motion constitutes a transient vibration event: it consists of a short-duration stress-wave sequence generated by detonation, characterised by a rapidly rising wavefront, a frequency spectrum determined by the charge weight, delay pattern, and geological propagation path, and a decay envelope that is a function of the scaled distance to the receptor. The term is appropriate for characterising the nature of the blast-induced ground motion as a physical input to the TSF assessment but carries no connotations about the structural response or the stability outcome; those are assessed through the geotechnical analysis.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The characterisation of ISO 4866:2010 and the term \"transient vibration\" is drawn directly from the KB (T1.compile.research.md), which is treated as authoritative. The description of blast-induced ground motion as a transient vibration event with a rising wavefront, frequency content, and decay with scaled distance is consistent with standard blast seismology practice and is supported implicitly by the ISO 4866 coverage of explosive sources described in the KB.]"," ","-\"Pore-pressure generation\" and \"excess pore pressure\" are the standard geotechnical terms for the increase in pore-water pressure above the in-situ hydrostatic equilibrium produced by undrained cyclic or transient loading. ICOLD Bulletin 98 (1995) and the Idriss and Boulanger (2008) monograph use this terminology to describe the fundamental mechanism through which dynamic loading may degrade the effective stress and shear strength of saturated tailings. [WEB:https://www.researchgate.net/publication/288624104_Soil_liquefaction_during_earthquakes] The normalised excess pore-pressure ratio is defined as:","+\"Pore-pressure generation\" and \"excess pore pressure\" are the standard geotechnical terms for the increase in pore-water pressure above the in-situ hydrostatic equilibrium produced by undrained cyclic or transient loading. ICOLD Bulletin 98 (1995) and the Idriss and Boulanger (2008) monograph use this terminology to describe the fundamental mechanism through which dynamic loading may degrade the effective stress and shear strength of saturated tailings, with the normalised excess pore-pressure ratio defined as:^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The terminology \"pore-pressure generation\" and \"excess pore pressure\" and their role in degrading effective stress under dynamic loading are consistent with the cited monograph (Idriss and Boulanger, 2008), confirmed by web search results. The attribution to ICOLD Bulletin 98 is consistent with that bulletin's documented subject matter (tailings dams and seismicity) as confirmed in the web search results. [WEB:https://www.researchgate.net/publication/288624104_Soil_liquefaction_during_earthquakes] This paragraph introduces the display equation below.]"," "," $$r_u = \\frac{\\Delta u}{\\sigma'_{v0}}$$"," ","-where $\\Delta u$ is the excess pore-water pressure generated by the dynamic event and $\\sigma'_{v0}$ is the initial effective vertical stress. The ratio $r_u$ is the key performance indicator in both instrumented piezometric monitoring and in constitutive models used for dynamic numerical analysis. As $r_u$ approaches unity, the effective stress approaches zero and the undrained shear strength is substantially reduced, with the consequence for stability depending on whether the residual state is contractive or dilative.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The terms \"pore-pressure generation\" and \"excess pore pressure\" and the definition of $r_u = \\Delta u / \\sigma'_{v0}$ are standard in geotechnical engineering and in the referenced monograph (Idriss and Boulanger, 2008). The Idriss and Boulanger (2008) reference is confirmed by multiple web search results. The interpretation of $r_u$ approaching unity as corresponding to near-zero effective stress and strength reduction is a direct consequence of the effective-stress principle and is universally accepted in the geotechnical literature.]","+where $\\Delta u$ is the excess pore-water pressure generated by the dynamic event and $\\sigma'_{v0}$ is the initial effective vertical stress. The ratio $r_u$ is the key performance indicator in both instrumented piezometric monitoring and in constitutive models used for dynamic numerical analysis. As $r_u$ approaches unity, the effective stress approaches zero and the undrained shear strength is substantially reduced, with the consequence for stability depending on whether the residual state is contractive or dilative.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The definition $r_u = \\Delta u / \\sigma'_{v0}$ and its interpretation as a measure of effective-stress degradation are standard in geotechnical engineering and are consistent with the Idriss and Boulanger (2008) monograph, confirmed by web search results. The physical consequence - near-zero effective stress as $r_u$ approaches unity - follows from the principle of effective stress and is universally accepted in the literature. The dependence of the stability consequence on contractive or dilative material state is consistent with the flow liquefaction versus cyclic mobility distinction addressed in the following paragraph.]"," "," \"Cyclic mobility\" and \"flow liquefaction\" are the two principal distinct failure modes associated with excess pore-pressure generation in saturated soils under cyclic loading, and their distinction is essential for accurate TSF reporting. Flow liquefaction occurs in contractive soils - including loose, saturated, fine-grained tailings - where the residual undrained shear strength mobilised after triggering is insufficient to maintain stability under the existing static shear stresses, resulting in a catastrophic, sustained loss of resistance and runout. Cyclic mobility occurs in soils that retain a positive residual undrained shear strength after triggering, producing transient loss of effective stress and large cyclic strains without a sustained flow failure. [WEB:https://itascasoftware.com/resources/when-cyclic-mobility-isnt-flow-liquefaction/] ICOLD Bulletin 98 and the Idriss and Boulanger (2008) framework distinguish these mechanisms, and ANCOLD (2019) recommends conservatively assuming flow liquefaction for saturated, brittle, contractive tailings absent evidence supporting a less severe classification. [WEB:https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/] In a technical report, the failure mode being assessed should be named explicitly, and the material evidence - penetration resistance profile, gradation, plasticity index - that supports the classification should be cited.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The distinction between flow liquefaction and cyclic mobility is a well-established geotechnical concept confirmed by the web search results, including the Itasca resource and the general liquefaction literature. The ANCOLD recommendation to assume flow liquefaction conservatively for contractive saturated tailings is confirmed by the ANCOLD search result, which directly quotes this provision. The ICOLD Bulletin 98 attribution is confirmed by the search results identifying that bulletin's subject matter as tailings dams and seismicity.]"," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01VZ7HtEUCqp4qFr3EAk1yie","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012XyZSjpvTXYhcP19h6CaJF","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/averrik/Cloud/github/projects/AR-S2J2J.R/TITO/sessions/run-20260706_164057_11000/T8.research.md","old_string":"\"Liquefaction potential\" is used in the Youd et al. (2001) framework and in the ICOLD tailings dam bulletins to describe the susceptibility of a saturated soil deposit to liquefaction triggering under a specified dynamic loading. [WEB:https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/23474/chapter/6] The quantitative measure of liquefaction potential is the factor of safety against triggering:\n\n$$FS_L = \\frac{CRR_{7.5} \\cdot MSF \\cdot K_\\sigma}{CSR}$$\n\nwhere $CRR_{7.5}$ is the cyclic resistance ratio corrected to a reference earthquake magnitude of $M_w = 7.5$, $MSF$ is the magnitude scaling factor that adjusts for the duration and equivalent cycles of the actual event, and $K_\\sigma$ is the overburden correction factor. When a blast event rather than an earthquake provides the dynamic demand, the CSR must be estimated from the blast-induced ground motion parameters and a representative equivalent number of uniform stress cycles must be assigned to the transient loading, making $FS_L$ a conditional quantity whose value is governed by these project-specific input choices. Values of $FS_L > 1$ indicate that the resistance exceeds the demand for the stated assumptions, while $FS_L \\leq 1$ indicates a triggering condition; the margin separating these states should be reported alongside the governing assumptions.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The Youd et al. (2001) framework, the definition of $FS_L$ in terms of $CRR_{7.5}$, $MSF$, $K_\\sigma$, and CSR, and the characterisation of $FS_L$ as a conditional output are confirmed by multiple web search results including the National Academies Press and University of California Davis resources. The formula structure is consistent with standard presentations. The conditional qualification for blast loading is consistent with the absence of a codified blast-adaptation procedure in any source retrieved.]","new_string":"\"Liquefaction potential\" is used in the Youd et al. (2001) framework and in the ICOLD tailings dam bulletins to describe the susceptibility of a saturated soil deposit to liquefaction triggering under a specified dynamic loading, with the quantitative measure expressed as the factor of safety against triggering:^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The use of the term \"liquefaction potential\" in the Youd et al. (2001) framework and in ICOLD tailings bulletins is confirmed by web search results including the National Academies Press summary. The characterisation of the factor of safety as the standard quantitative measure of liquefaction potential is consistent with those sources. [WEB:https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/23474/chapter/6] This paragraph introduces the display equation below.]\n\n$$FS_L = \\frac{CRR_{7.5} \\cdot MSF \\cdot K_\\sigma}{CSR}$$\n\nwhere $CRR_{7.5}$ is the cyclic resistance ratio corrected to a reference earthquake magnitude of $M_w = 7.5$, $MSF$ is the magnitude scaling factor that adjusts for the duration and equivalent cycles of the actual event, and $K_\\sigma$ is the overburden correction factor. When a blast event rather than an earthquake provides the dynamic demand, the CSR must be estimated from the blast-induced ground motion parameters and a representative equivalent number of uniform stress cycles must be assigned to the transient loading, making $FS_L$ a conditional quantity whose value is governed by these project-specific input choices. Values of $FS_L > 1$ indicate that the resistance exceeds the demand for the stated assumptions, while $FS_L \\leq 1$ indicates a triggering condition; the margin separating these states should be reported alongside the governing assumptions.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The definition of $FS_L$ in terms of $CRR_{7.5}$, $MSF$, $K_\\sigma$, and CSR, and the characterisation of $FS_L$ as a conditional output for blast loading scenarios, are confirmed by multiple web search results including the National Academies Press and University of California Davis resources. The formula structure is consistent with standard presentations of the Youd et al. (2001) and Idriss and Boulanger (2008) frameworks. The conditional qualification for blast loading is consistent with the absence of a codified blast-specific adaptation procedure in any source retrieved during this research.]"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11198,"cache_read_input_tokens":100960,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":11198,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":26,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"193e9b03-f0e0-48bb-82cd-24114a1d1cbc","uuid":"cc838db6-1644-4cb2-b8bb-24cd5b90e9ec","request_id":"req_011CcmPWcrm7X2AR62oMWSMr"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012XyZSjpvTXYhcP19h6CaJF","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/averrik/Cloud/github/projects/AR-S2J2J.R/TITO/sessions/run-20260706_164057_11000/T8.research.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"193e9b03-f0e0-48bb-82cd-24114a1d1cbc","uuid":"41a26c36-5741-47a9-ac3a-8d081d6aa3c3","timestamp":"2026-07-06T19:54:44.954Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"/Users/averrik/Cloud/github/projects/AR-S2J2J.R/TITO/sessions/run-20260706_164057_11000/T8.research.md","oldString":"\"Liquefaction potential\" is used in the Youd et al. (2001) framework and in the ICOLD tailings dam bulletins to describe the susceptibility of a saturated soil deposit to liquefaction triggering under a specified dynamic loading. [WEB:https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/23474/chapter/6] The quantitative measure of liquefaction potential is the factor of safety against triggering:\n\n$$FS_L = \\frac{CRR_{7.5} \\cdot MSF \\cdot K_\\sigma}{CSR}$$\n\nwhere $CRR_{7.5}$ is the cyclic resistance ratio corrected to a reference earthquake magnitude of $M_w = 7.5$, $MSF$ is the magnitude scaling factor that adjusts for the duration and equivalent cycles of the actual event, and $K_\\sigma$ is the overburden correction factor. When a blast event rather than an earthquake provides the dynamic demand, the CSR must be estimated from the blast-induced ground motion parameters and a representative equivalent number of uniform stress cycles must be assigned to the transient loading, making $FS_L$ a conditional quantity whose value is governed by these project-specific input choices. Values of $FS_L > 1$ indicate that the resistance exceeds the demand for the stated assumptions, while $FS_L \\leq 1$ indicates a triggering condition; the margin separating these states should be reported alongside the governing assumptions.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The Youd et al. (2001) framework, the definition of $FS_L$ in terms of $CRR_{7.5}$, $MSF$, $K_\\sigma$, and CSR, and the characterisation of $FS_L$ as a conditional output are confirmed by multiple web search results including the National Academies Press and University of California Davis resources. The formula structure is consistent with standard presentations. The conditional qualification for blast loading is consistent with the absence of a codified blast-adaptation procedure in any source retrieved.]","newString":"\"Liquefaction potential\" is used in the Youd et al. (2001) framework and in the ICOLD tailings dam bulletins to describe the susceptibility of a saturated soil deposit to liquefaction triggering under a specified dynamic loading, with the quantitative measure expressed as the factor of safety against triggering:^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The use of the term \"liquefaction potential\" in the Youd et al. (2001) framework and in ICOLD tailings bulletins is confirmed by web search results including the National Academies Press summary. The characterisation of the factor of safety as the standard quantitative measure of liquefaction potential is consistent with those sources. [WEB:https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/23474/chapter/6] This paragraph introduces the display equation below.]\n\n$$FS_L = \\frac{CRR_{7.5} \\cdot MSF \\cdot K_\\sigma}{CSR}$$\n\nwhere $CRR_{7.5}$ is the cyclic resistance ratio corrected to a reference earthquake magnitude of $M_w = 7.5$, $MSF$ is the magnitude scaling factor that adjusts for the duration and equivalent cycles of the actual event, and $K_\\sigma$ is the overburden correction factor. When a blast event rather than an earthquake provides the dynamic demand, the CSR must be estimated from the blast-induced ground motion parameters and a representative equivalent number of uniform stress cycles must be assigned to the transient loading, making $FS_L$ a conditional quantity whose value is governed by these project-specific input choices. Values of $FS_L > 1$ indicate that the resistance exceeds the demand for the stated assumptions, while $FS_L \\leq 1$ indicates a triggering condition; the margin separating these states should be reported alongside the governing assumptions.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The definition of $FS_L$ in terms of $CRR_{7.5}$, $MSF$, $K_\\sigma$, and CSR, and the characterisation of $FS_L$ as a conditional output for blast loading scenarios, are confirmed by multiple web search results including the National Academies Press and University of California Davis resources. The formula structure is consistent with standard presentations of the Youd et al. (2001) and Idriss and Boulanger (2008) frameworks. The conditional qualification for blast loading is consistent with the absence of a codified blast-specific adaptation procedure in any source retrieved during this research.]","originalFile":"# Guidance Documents for Blasting Effects on Tailings Storage Facilities: AR-S2J2J.R\n\n---\n\n### SLOT 1: Authoritative Guidance Documents Covering Blasting Effects on TSFs\n\nThe international industry standards that most directly address dynamic loading, vibration monitoring, pore-pressure response, and liquefaction screening for tailings storage facilities are the Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management (GISTM, 2020) and the IFC Environmental, Health, and Safety Guidelines for Mining (2007). The GISTM, developed under the auspices of the United Nations Environment Programme, the International Council on Mining and Metals, and the Principles for Responsible Investment, requires operators to establish an interdisciplinary knowledge base encompassing geotechnical and seismicity information, to document design criteria explicitly, and to implement integrated performance monitoring throughout the facility life cycle. [KB:T1.compile.research.md] The standard does not prescribe fixed numerical vibration thresholds for blast-induced loading on TSFs; it frames safety as a risk-management obligation demanding project-specific assessment and continuous monitoring as the means of demonstrating performance. The IFC EHS Guidelines for Mining (2007) require that tailings impoundments and dams be planned, designed, and operated such that geotechnical risks are assessed and managed throughout the mine life cycle, and they require designs to reflect internationally recognised engineering practices and local seismic conditions, treating geotechnical risk assessment - including blast-induced ground motion effects on tailings facilities - as a distinct discipline rather than an extension of building-vibration compliance. [KB:T1.compile.research.md]^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: Both GISTM and IFC EHS Mining Guidelines are confirmed in the KB (T1.compile.research.md) with content that is consistent with established characterisations of these documents. The claim that neither prescribes fixed blast vibration thresholds for TSFs is directly supported by the KB statement that GISTM requires an interdisciplinary knowledge base and performance monitoring rather than fixed limits, and by the IFC guidance treating tailings facilities as requiring risk assessment and internationally recognised engineering practice.]\n\nThe ANCOLD Guidelines on Tailings Dams, Revision 1 (2019), published by the Australian National Committee on Large Dams, provide the most comprehensive single-document treatment of dynamic and seismic loading for tailings storage facilities among the available authoritative sources. [WEB:https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/] The 2019 revision explicitly aligns tailings dam seismic design criteria with the companion ANCOLD Guidelines for Design of Dams and Appurtenant Structures for Earthquake (2019), addresses both static and cyclic liquefaction of saturated tailings, and recommends conservatively assuming that static liquefaction will occur for brittle, contractive, saturated tailings unless site-specific evidence supports a less conservative classification. The document addresses planning, design, construction, operation, and closure, treating earthquake loading and factor-of-safety requirements under dynamic conditions as subjects requiring engineering analysis throughout the facility life cycle rather than as compliance thresholds satisfied by reference to a look-up table.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The ANCOLD Guidelines on Tailings Dams Revision 1 (2019) are confirmed by web search results including the official ANCOLD product page and the publicly available 2019 addendum text. The specific content claims - alignment with earthquake design guidelines, treatment of static and cyclic liquefaction, conservative assumption of static liquefaction for contractive tailings - are each corroborated by the search results, which quote these provisions directly.]\n\nICOLD has produced a series of bulletins that collectively constitute the authoritative international technical foundation for seismic assessment of tailings dams. ICOLD Bulletin 98 (1995), Tailings Dams and Seismicity, addresses the seismic behavior of tailings facilities specifically, covering liquefaction potential, dynamic stability, and modes of failure associated with cyclic ground motion. ICOLD Bulletin 104 (1996), Monitoring of Tailings Dams, establishes the framework for instrumented monitoring programs, including piezometric surveillance of pore-pressure response in TSF embankments. ICOLD Bulletin 121 (2001), Tailings Dams - Risk of Dangerous Occurrences, documents lessons from actual failures including seismically triggered and liquefaction-related events. ICOLD Bulletin 148 (2011), Selecting Seismic Parameters for Large Dams, provides guidance on seismic hazard characterisation and the selection of design earthquake parameters applicable to large storage facilities including tailings dams. [WEB:https://www.icoldchile.cl/boletines/148.pdf] ICOLD Bulletin 194 (2022), Tailings Dam Safety, is the most recent unified ICOLD statement on tailings dam governance and engineering, consolidating seismic requirements alongside operational and monitoring obligations in a form intended to supersede and integrate the earlier bulletins for contemporary professional practice. [WEB:https://klohn.com/wp-content/uploads/B194-ICOLD-Tailings-Dam-Safety.pdf]^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The ICOLD bulletins listed (98, 104, 121, 148, 194) are confirmed through multiple independent web search results. Bulletins 98 and 104 are specifically named in the ICOLD bulletin sequence summaries returned by the search. Bulletin 148 is available as a PDF from the ICOLD Chile chapter. Bulletin 194 is referenced in a UNECE presentation and available through the Klohn Crippen Berger website. The attribution of topics (seismicity, monitoring, risk of dangerous occurrences, seismic parameters, tailings safety) to the respective bulletins is consistent across multiple search results.]\n\nISO 4866:2010, Mechanical Vibration and Shock - Vibration of Fixed Structures, provides the foundational measurement methodology for blast-vibration monitoring programs. [KB:T1.compile.research.md] The standard applies to structures above and below ground including earth structures such as dykes and embankments, and addresses sources including explosions and construction activities; however, it explicitly excludes certain special structures, including dams, from its scope. Because a TSF is typically classified as a dam for regulatory and engineering purposes, ISO 4866:2010 is directly applicable as the technical framework for blast-vibration transducer placement, waveform acquisition, and frequency-content characterisation in TSF monitoring programs, while falling short of providing structural acceptance criteria for the facility itself. The standard's exclusion of dams from its direct scope is relevant to report framing: ISO 4866:2010 supports the measurement methodology but does not establish whether a measured vibration level is acceptable for a particular TSF, which remains a matter of project-specific engineering assessment.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The characterisation of ISO 4866:2010 - its applicability to earth structures including dykes and embankments, its coverage of explosion sources, and its specific exclusion of dams - is drawn directly from the KB (T1.compile.research.md), which is treated as authoritative for this audit step. The inference that a TSF classified as a dam falls outside the direct compliance scope of ISO 4866 follows from the stated scope exclusion in the standard itself and is not contradicted by any other source cited in this document.]\n\nThe geotechnical liquefaction-assessment literature constitutes an integral part of the analytical framework for evaluating the response of saturated tailings to blast-induced dynamic loading, even though that literature was developed primarily for earthquake loading scenarios. Youd et al. (2001), summarising the consensus reached at the 1996 NCEER and 1998 NCEER/NSF workshops on evaluation of liquefaction resistance of soils, established the simplified stress-based framework in which the cyclic stress ratio (CSR) representing dynamic demand is compared with the cyclic resistance ratio (CRR) representing soil resistance to produce a factor of safety against liquefaction triggering. [WEB:https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/23474/chapter/6] Idriss and Boulanger (2008), Soil Liquefaction During Earthquakes, updated and refined those procedures with revised magnitude scaling factors, improved handling of low-plasticity fine-grained soils of the type commonly encountered in tailings, and updated CPT and SPT-based CRR correlations. [WEB:https://www.researchgate.net/publication/288624104_Soil_liquefaction_during_earthquakes] Both frameworks are designed for earthquake-induced cyclic loading, and their application to blast-induced ground motion requires project-specific adaptation of the loading characterisation, particularly the estimation of equivalent uniform cycles associated with a transient blast event.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The Youd et al. (2001) and Idriss and Boulanger (2008) frameworks are widely documented authoritative references in the geotechnical engineering literature. Their content - CSR/CRR framework, magnitude scaling factors, CPT and SPT correlations, treatment of fine-grained soils - is confirmed by multiple web search results, including the National Academies Press summary and multiple faculty PDFs. The qualification that adaptation is needed for blasting scenarios is standard geotechnical practice and is consistent with the absence of any blast-specific version of these frameworks in the search results.]\n\nThe Mining Association of Canada's Guide to the Management of Tailings Facilities, Version 3.1 (2019), provides an operations-oriented framework for TSF safety that complements the design-phase guidance of ANCOLD and ICOLD. [WEB:https://mining.ca/resources/guides-manuals/a-guide-to-the-management-of-tailings-facilities-version-3-1-2019/] The MAC guide structures TSF management through an Operation, Maintenance and Surveillance (OMS) manual framework that requires documented monitoring of all identified performance indicators, including instrumented pore-pressure and vibration responses where blasting occurs in proximity to a TSF. While the MAC guide does not prescribe fixed quantitative vibration thresholds for blast events, its OMS framework establishes the operational mechanism by which project-specific monitoring trigger levels and alarm thresholds are defined, communicated to operators, and updated in response to observed performance.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The MAC Guide to the Management of Tailings Facilities Version 3.1 (2019) is confirmed via the official Mining Association of Canada website URL found in the web search results. The OMS framework characterisation is consistent with publicly available descriptions of the MAC guide and with the general practice in tailings management. The claim that the guide does not prescribe fixed vibration thresholds is consistent with its governance-and-management focus, which is confirmed by the search result descriptions of its content.]\n\n---\n\n### SLOT 2: South African versus International Applicability of Sources\n\nWithin the South African regulatory environment, the primary legislative instrument governing mining blasting is the Mine Health and Safety Act 29 of 1996 (MHSA), which requires mine operators to prepare and implement a site-specific mandatory Code of Practice addressing health and safety risks associated with their particular operations. The Department of Mineral Resources and Energy issued the Guideline for a Mandatory Code of Practice for Minimum Standards on Ground Vibrations, Noise, Air-blast, and Flyrock near Surface Structures and Communities to be Protected in August 2024, establishing the current national baseline for blast-vibration control in South African mining under sections 9(2) and 9(3) of the MHSA. [WEB:https://cer.org.za/virtual-library/legislation/national/mining/mine-blasting-guideline-code-of-practice-for-minimum-standards-on-ground-vibrations-noise-air-blast-and-flyrock-near-surface-structures-and-communities-to-be-protected-2024] This guideline defines protected structures and communities in the context of surface mining, and operators must demonstrate compliance through a site-specific COP that describes monitoring arrangements and acceptable vibration levels for identified sensitive receivers. The 2024 DMRE blasting guideline is South African in origin and legally mandated, making it the primary regulatory compliance reference for blasting vibration limits in a South African mining project, including projects where blasting occurs in proximity to a TSF.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The existence and content of the South African MHSA (1996) and the DMRE August 2024 Guideline for a Mandatory Code of Practice are confirmed by web search results, including the Centre for Environmental Rights website entry and the Government Gazette reference. The MHSA sections cited (9(2) and 9(3)) are consistent with multiple South African legal references found in the search results. The characterisation of the guideline as defining protected structures and requiring site-specific COPs is consistent with the regulatory framework described in the search results.]\n\nThe South African National Committee on Large Dams (SANCOLD) has produced guidance specifically directed at the South African context for tailings dam management. The SANCOLD document \"Your Tailings Dam\" (2020) addresses design, operation, and safety monitoring of tailings facilities within the South African legislative environment and explicitly notes two significant gaps in the local regulatory framework: first, that South African legislation does not prescribe the severity or return period of a seismic event that a tailings dam must withstand, leaving that determination as a matter of engineering judgement by the designer and owner; and second, that South African legislation does not yet directly address the GISTM requirements. [WEB:https://sancold.org.za/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/SANCOLD-Your-Tailings-Dam-2020.pdf] These observations are directly relevant to report framing: a project operating under South African jurisdiction cannot assume that the seismic or dynamic-loading acceptance criteria in any international standard have been adopted directly into South African law, and the engineer of record must state the basis on which any seismic performance target has been chosen.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The SANCOLD \"Your Tailings Dam\" (2020) document is confirmed by a web search result citing the SANCOLD website URL. The specific claims - that South African legislation does not prescribe seismic return periods for tailings dams and that it does not yet address GISTM - are drawn from the web search result text, which directly quotes or paraphrases these findings from the SANCOLD document. These are material observations specific to the South African regulatory context.]\n\nThe international sources - GISTM, ANCOLD (2019), ICOLD bulletins 98, 104, 121, 148, and 194, the IFC EHS Guidelines for Mining, and ISO 4866:2010 - are applicable across jurisdictions as statements of good international practice and are accepted as technical reference documents for independent review in South Africa. [KB:T1.compile.research.md] [WEB:https://www.icmm.com/en-gb/our-principles/tailings/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management] Their adoption in a South African TSF report is appropriate as technical framing for engineering assessment, and lenders, financiers, and independent review panels routinely require alignment with GISTM and ICOLD guidance. However, the specific numerical targets in those documents - such as the ANCOLD minimum factor of safety of 1.1 under seismic loading, or earthquake return periods referenced in ICOLD Bulletin 148 - derive from Australian or international regulatory contexts and should be treated as engineering references rather than as South African legal requirements unless the project permit, lender technical schedules, or the Engineer of Record explicitly adopts them as binding project standards.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The categorisation of GISTM, ANCOLD, ICOLD, IFC EHS, and ISO 4866 as international references applicable across jurisdictions is well-established and confirmed by multiple web sources. The SANCOLD search result specifically confirms that South African legislation does not adopt GISTM directly, supporting the qualification that international numerical criteria are not automatically legally binding in South Africa. The mention of lender requirements for GISTM alignment is consistent with general knowledge of equator principles and DFI practice, though no specific URL confirming that point appears in the search results; the confidence rating reflects the direct documentary support for the primary regulatory gap claim.]\n\nThe U.S. Office of Surface Mining Reclamation and Enforcement rules in 30 CFR Part 816 (Sections 816.67 and 816.68) are a mature regulatory example of how a blasting framework distinguishes fixed PPV thresholds for standard occupied structures from a project-specific approval process for other structures, including dams, impoundments, utilities, and underground mines. [KB:T1.compile.research.md] Under 30 CFR 816.67, the vibration limits for dams and impoundments are not automatically derived from the tabulated building thresholds but are submitted in the blasting plan and approved by the regulator on a project-specific basis. This regulatory architecture - separate fixed limits for standard receivers and a project-specific submission process for engineered earth structures - illustrates the principle that South African practice approximates through the MHSA COP process, even though the South African framework is articulated differently and does not reproduce the specific numerical values or definitional conventions of the U.S. regulations. Citing 30 CFR in a South African TSF report is appropriate as an international regulatory analogue illustrating best-practice structure, provided the report clearly identifies it as a U.S. federal rule rather than as a standard applicable in South Africa.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The characterisation of 30 CFR 816.67 and 816.68 is drawn directly from the KB (T1.compile.research.md), which is treated as authoritative for this audit step. The KB explicitly states that 30 CFR 816.67 provides project-specific limits for dams and impoundments submitted in the blasting plan rather than adopting the building-level thresholds. The inference that the South African COP process approximates this architecture is a supported analogy, not a legally equivalent statement, and is appropriately qualified.]\n\n---\n\n### SLOT 3: Quantitative Acceptance Criteria versus Project-Specific Assessment\n\nNone of the primary authoritative sources for TSF safety - GISTM, ANCOLD (2019), ICOLD bulletins 98, 104, 121, 148, and 194, or the IFC EHS Guidelines for Mining - prescribes a universal fixed blast-vibration threshold expressed as a limiting peak particle velocity for protecting a tailings storage facility. The absence of a universal vibration limit for TSFs reflects the established recognition in geotechnical dam engineering that the dynamic response of a saturated tailings facility depends on material properties, fabric, degree of saturation, drainage conditions, confining stress, pore-pressure history, the geometry of the embankment, and the proximity and characteristics of the blasting source. None of these site-specific factors are captured by a generic PPV criterion derived from research on vibration effects in rigid or masonry building structures. [KB:T1.compile.research.md] [WEB:https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/]^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The claim that none of the listed authoritative TSF documents prescribes a universal blast PPV limit is strongly supported. The KB (T1.compile.research.md) states that 30 CFR requires project-specific plan submission for dams and impoundments rather than a tabulated threshold; GISTM and IFC EHS are characterised as requiring risk assessment and project-specific criteria. The ANCOLD web search results describe factor-of-safety and earthquake-return-period criteria but no universal PPV limit for blasting on TSFs. No web source retrieved a fixed PPV acceptance criterion for TSFs from any of the listed documents.]\n\nANCOLD (2019) provides the most explicit quantitative framing available for TSF seismic performance, but its criteria are expressed in terms of structural stability factors of safety and design earthquake return periods rather than in terms of blast-vibration amplitude. The guidelines prescribe a minimum factor of safety of 1.1 for transient and seismic loading conditions, and recommend evaluating the facility against a 1:10,000 annual exceedance probability earthquake where liquefaction of tailings is likely to affect the stability of the embankment, unless a site-specific risk assessment demonstrates that an adequate risk profile is achieved with a lower seismic loading. [WEB:https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/] These quantitative targets are outputs of a project-specific engineering process - geotechnical characterisation, in-situ testing, liquefaction potential assessment, and dynamic stability analysis - and are not simplified look-up values. Applying them to blast-induced loading requires translating the blast-vibration record into equivalent dynamic demand parameters consistent with the geotechnical analysis framework, which involves project-specific assumptions about wave attenuation, loading duration, and equivalent cyclic cycles.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The ANCOLD (2019) factor-of-safety value of 1.1 for seismic conditions and the 1:10,000 AEP earthquake recommendation for liquefiable tailings are confirmed by two independent web search results: the ANCOLD product page summary and the SANCOLD web search result, which cites a minimum FOS of 1.1 for transient/seismic conditions in South African guidance citing ANCOLD practice. The conclusion that these criteria presuppose project-specific geotechnical analysis follows directly from the stated content of ANCOLD.]\n\nLiquefaction screening for saturated tailings under dynamic loading is performed within the simplified stress-based framework of Youd et al. (2001) or Idriss and Boulanger (2008), in which the cyclic stress ratio (CSR), representing the demand imposed by the dynamic event, is compared with the cyclic resistance ratio (CRR), representing the soil resistance to liquefaction triggering derived from in-situ penetration tests such as the SPT or CPT. [WEB:https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/23474/chapter/6] [WEB:https://www.researchgate.net/publication/288624104_Soil_liquefaction_during_earthquakes] The factor of safety against liquefaction is defined as:^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The simplified stress-based liquefaction screening framework of Youd et al. (2001) and Idriss and Boulanger (2008) is confirmed by multiple web search results including the National Academies Press and University of California Davis faculty documents. The description of SPT and CPT as the standard in-situ test methods for deriving CRR is consistent with those sources. This paragraph introduces the display equation below and contains no independent factual claim beyond the framework description, which is well-supported.]\n\n$$FS_L = \\frac{CRR_{7.5} \\cdot MSF \\cdot K_\\sigma}{CSR}$$\n\nwhere $CRR_{7.5}$ is the cyclic resistance ratio normalised to a reference earthquake magnitude of 7.5, $MSF$ is the magnitude scaling factor correcting for earthquake duration, and $K_\\sigma$ is the overburden correction factor. When blast-induced loading rather than earthquake loading is the triggering mechanism, the CSR must be estimated from the blast-induced ground motion waveform and a representative equivalent number of uniform loading cycles must be assigned to the transient event - a parameter that is far smaller for a blast than for a sustained earthquake of similar peak amplitude. This adaptation of the earthquake-based framework to blast loading requires project-specific assumptions on loading characterisation that are not codified in any universally adopted standard, making $FS_L$ a conditional engineering output dependent on those documented choices.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The definition of $FS_L$ with $CRR_{7.5}$, $MSF$, $K_\\sigma$, and CSR is consistent with the standard presentation in Youd et al. (2001) and Idriss and Boulanger (2008), confirmed by web search results. The qualification that blast loading requires adaptation of the number-of-cycles parameter is consistent with the documented shorter duration of blast events compared with earthquake events, as referenced in the blast-induced liquefaction literature found in the web search results. No codified universal blast-liquefaction screening procedure was identified in any source retrieved.]\n\nPore-pressure monitoring programs serve as the empirical counterpart to analytical liquefaction screening. ICOLD Bulletin 104 (1996) and the MAC OMS framework require piezometer instrumentation within TSF embankments and tailings deposits to measure in-situ pore pressure, detect excess pore-pressure generation following dynamic events, and track pore-pressure dissipation rates. [WEB:https://mining.ca/resources/guides-manuals/a-guide-to-the-management-of-tailings-facilities-version-3-1-2019/] The monitoring data serve to calibrate and validate analytical models, to detect anomalous pore-pressure responses that may indicate incipient instability, and to confirm that measured vibration events do not produce lasting pore-pressure accumulation above the thresholds defined in the OMS manual. Acceptance criteria for pore-pressure response are project-specific, defined within the OMS manual or instrumentation response plan as trigger values and alarm thresholds derived from site-specific stability calculations rather than adopted from any generic standard or building-vibration criterion.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: ICOLD Bulletin 104 (1996) as a monitoring guideline for tailings dams is confirmed by the web search results. The MAC OMS framework and its requirement for documented monitoring including piezometric response is confirmed by the MAC Guide URL and summary descriptions. The characterisation of acceptance criteria as project-specific is consistent with all surveyed guidance documents, none of which supply a generic pore-pressure threshold for blast-event monitoring at TSFs.]\n\n---\n\n### SLOT 4: Report Framing to Distinguish Criteria, Monitoring Requirements, and Assumptions\n\nA professional technical report on the effects of nearby blasting on a TSF must clearly delineate four distinct categories of statement: published reference criteria drawn from identified standards; project-specific criteria established for the facility in question by the Engineer of Record; monitoring requirements arising from regulatory obligations, best-practice standards, and design documentation; and analysis assumptions made in the assessment. Conflating these categories - in particular, applying a criterion developed for a different receiver type, such as a building-damage PPV threshold from a vibration standard, as though it governs TSF structural performance - misrepresents the state of professional practice and may produce technically indefensible conclusions that expose the report to challenge during regulatory review or independent assessment. [KB:T1.compile.research.md] [WEB:https://klohn.com/wp-content/uploads/B194-ICOLD-Tailings-Dam-Safety.pdf]^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The four-category framework for report organisation is consistent with the practice distinctions described throughout the cited sources: the KB (T1.compile.research.md) explicitly distinguishes structural damage criteria from human-exposure criteria and from monitoring/reporting requirements, and from project-specific plan submissions. ICOLD Bulletin 194 (2022), referenced via the Klohn Crippen Berger URL, consolidates governance and engineering requirements in a manner that requires explicit documentation of design criteria as a distinct category. The characterisation of conflation as a reportable risk is a professional judgement consistent with the regulatory framing described in the cited sources.]\n\nPublished reference criteria from building-vibration standards such as BS 7385-2:1993 and from human-comfort and human-exposure standards such as BS 6472-2:2008 or ISO 2631-2 do not govern TSF structural performance and are outside the scope of the present assessment. Those documents are explicitly scoped to building structures and to occupant physiological response respectively; none establishes an acceptance criterion for an earth structure such as a tailings embankment or impoundment. Presenting a PPV threshold derived from those standards as the criterion for protecting a TSF implies a scope of applicability that those standards do not claim, and it obscures the absence of a published fixed vibration threshold for the engineering question actually under assessment. ISO 4866:2010, which does include earth structures within certain parts of its scope, provides measurement and evaluation methodology but explicitly excludes dams and similarly does not prescribe limiting vibration amplitudes for earth embankments or tailings facilities. [KB:T1.compile.research.md] The report should therefore name these standards only in their correct application context - as references for the building-damage or human-comfort dimensions of any broader assessment - and should present the TSF structural-performance question as governed by the geotechnical assessment framework described by ANCOLD, ICOLD, GISTM, and the site-specific engineering analysis.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The scope of BS 7385-2 (building damage), BS 6472-2 (human blast exposure in buildings), and ISO 2631-2 (human annoyance and comfort) is confirmed in the KB (T1.compile.research.md), which explicitly states that BS 6472-2 \"does not address building damage\" and that ISO 2631-2 \"concerns comfort and annoyance from whole-body vibration in buildings\" and that \"structural damage is discussed in ISO 4866.\" The KB also confirms that ISO 4866 excludes dams from its scope. These are straightforward scope characterisations from the KB, treated as authoritative for this audit step.]\n\nThe GISTM (2020) and IFC EHS Guidelines for Mining (2007) frame TSF safety as a risk-management process in which operators define performance criteria embedded in facility design documentation and the OMS manual, rather than a compliance checklist satisfied by reference to tabulated limits. [KB:T1.compile.research.md] Within that framework, a project-specific blasting acceptance criterion for the TSF - expressed as a vibration amplitude limit, a pore-pressure trigger level, or a stability factor-of-safety threshold - is established through site-specific engineering assessment, agreed with the independent tailings review board or qualified person, and documented in the OMS manual. The report should name the document in which the project-specific criterion is established, state the basis on which it was derived (for example, the geotechnical analysis from which the pore-pressure trigger value follows, or the stability calculation from which the vibration limit was back-analysed), and distinguish it from both the general guidance documents and from the MHSA COP regulatory compliance thresholds, which address different protected receivers under different legal obligations.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The characterisation of GISTM and IFC EHS Mining as requiring operator-defined, documented performance criteria rather than fixed tabulated limits is confirmed by the KB (T1.compile.research.md) and by the web search results describing GISTM as requiring documented design criteria and integrated performance monitoring. The distinction between OMS-documented project-specific criteria and general guidance documents is consistent with the MAC OMS framework, which is confirmed via the MAC website URL in the search results.]\n\nMonitoring requirements constitute a distinct reporting category that should be presented as performance-verification tools operating alongside the analytical assessment, not as substitutes for it. ISO 4866:2010 provides the technical basis for the vibration monitoring methodology, specifying transducer placement, signal acquisition requirements, frequency-content characterisation, and data analysis procedures applicable to blast events affecting earth structures. [KB:T1.compile.research.md] ICOLD Bulletin 104 provides the framework for piezometric monitoring programs at tailings dams, and the MAC OMS framework prescribes the structure of an instrumentation response plan including defined trigger and alarm levels. These documents establish what must be measured and how, and they inform the thresholds at which measurements require a response; they do not themselves generate the acceptance criteria, which arise from site-specific engineering calculation.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The characterisation of ISO 4866 as covering blast-vibration measurement methodology, and of ICOLD Bulletin 104 as covering tailings dam piezometric monitoring, is supported by KB content (ISO 4866) and web search results (ICOLD 104). The MAC OMS framework is confirmed via the MAC website. The distinction between monitoring standards and acceptance criteria is consistent with the content of all cited sources and with the structure of the analytical-monitoring relationship described in the GISTM and IFC EHS frameworks.]\n\nAnalysis assumptions constitute the fourth category requiring transparent reporting. Where the earthquake-based liquefaction-screening frameworks of Youd et al. (2001) or Idriss and Boulanger (2008) are adapted to blast-induced cyclic loading, the project-specific choices that determine the computed factor of safety include the equivalent number of uniform cycles assigned to the blast event, the attenuation relationship used to estimate ground motion at the TSF, the representative penetration resistance profile used to derive CRR, the drainage assumptions applied to the tailings under the transient loading duration, and the specific CRR correlation selected. [WEB:https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/23474/chapter/6] [WEB:https://www.researchgate.net/publication/288624104_Soil_liquefaction_during_earthquakes] The report should state these assumptions explicitly, distinguish them from established values in the referenced standards, and present the sensitivity of the conclusions to key parameter choices, so that the engineering judgement embedded in the analysis is transparent and auditable. The absence of a codified blast-liquefaction screening procedure in any of the authoritative guidance documents means that the report cannot defer responsibility for those assumptions to a named standard; they are engineering choices that require explicit justification.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The requirement to adapt earthquake-based liquefaction frameworks to blast loading by selecting equivalent cycle counts and other parameters is confirmed by the structure of the Youd et al. (2001) and Idriss and Boulanger (2008) frameworks as described in the web search results: those frameworks are explicitly earthquake-oriented, and blast-specific adaptations are not codified in any universal standard identified in this research. The list of project-specific assumptions is consistent with standard geotechnical practice for applying simplified liquefaction methods.]\n\n---\n\n### SLOT 5: Source-Backed Terminology for TSF Response to Blasting\n\nThe term \"dynamic loading\" appears in the ICOLD bulletins on tailings dams and in the ANCOLD guidelines with a consistent meaning: any time-varying force or acceleration applied to the structure, its embankment, or its foundation, encompassing earthquake-induced ground motion, blast-induced ground vibration, and in some formulations operational loads from heavy equipment. [WEB:https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/] [WEB:https://klohn.com/wp-content/uploads/B194-ICOLD-Tailings-Dam-Safety.pdf] In the tailings dam literature, dynamic loading is treated as a stability-relevant load case that must be considered alongside static loading in the design and performance assessment of the facility and that may govern design where the tailings or foundation material is susceptible to strength loss under cyclic excitation. The term does not carry an implication about the source of the loading; it is applicable to both seismic and blast events provided the dynamic nature of the loading is established.^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The use of \"dynamic loading\" in the context of ANCOLD and ICOLD tailings dam guidance is consistent with the content of those documents as confirmed by web search results. However, the specific application to both seismic and blast events in the same ANCOLD or ICOLD text is not directly quoted in the retrieved search results, which describe seismic loading primarily. The characterisation is a reasonable and professionally defensible extrapolation of the term's standard technical meaning in geotechnical engineering.]\n\nThe term \"transient vibration\" is used in the vibration measurement literature, including ISO 4866:2010, to describe a vibration event of finite duration that does not persist as continuous or steady-state excitation. [KB:T1.compile.research.md] Blast-induced ground motion constitutes a transient vibration event: it consists of a short-duration stress-wave sequence generated by detonation, characterised by a rapidly rising wavefront, a frequency spectrum determined by the charge weight, delay pattern, and geological propagation path, and a decay envelope that is a function of the scaled distance to the receptor. The term is appropriate for characterising the nature of the blast-induced ground motion as a physical input to the TSF assessment but carries no connotations about the structural response or the stability outcome; those are assessed through the geotechnical analysis.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The characterisation of ISO 4866:2010 and the term \"transient vibration\" is drawn directly from the KB (T1.compile.research.md), which is treated as authoritative. The description of blast-induced ground motion as a transient vibration event with a rising wavefront, frequency content, and decay with scaled distance is consistent with standard blast seismology practice and is supported implicitly by the ISO 4866 coverage of explosive sources described in the KB.]\n\n\"Pore-pressure generation\" and \"excess pore pressure\" are the standard geotechnical terms for the increase in pore-water pressure above the in-situ hydrostatic equilibrium produced by undrained cyclic or transient loading. ICOLD Bulletin 98 (1995) and the Idriss and Boulanger (2008) monograph use this terminology to describe the fundamental mechanism through which dynamic loading may degrade the effective stress and shear strength of saturated tailings, with the normalised excess pore-pressure ratio defined as:^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The terminology \"pore-pressure generation\" and \"excess pore pressure\" and their role in degrading effective stress under dynamic loading are consistent with the cited monograph (Idriss and Boulanger, 2008), confirmed by web search results. The attribution to ICOLD Bulletin 98 is consistent with that bulletin's documented subject matter (tailings dams and seismicity) as confirmed in the web search results. [WEB:https://www.researchgate.net/publication/288624104_Soil_liquefaction_during_earthquakes] This paragraph introduces the display equation below.]\n\n$$r_u = \\frac{\\Delta u}{\\sigma'_{v0}}$$\n\nwhere $\\Delta u$ is the excess pore-water pressure generated by the dynamic event and $\\sigma'_{v0}$ is the initial effective vertical stress. The ratio $r_u$ is the key performance indicator in both instrumented piezometric monitoring and in constitutive models used for dynamic numerical analysis. As $r_u$ approaches unity, the effective stress approaches zero and the undrained shear strength is substantially reduced, with the consequence for stability depending on whether the residual state is contractive or dilative.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The definition $r_u = \\Delta u / \\sigma'_{v0}$ and its interpretation as a measure of effective-stress degradation are standard in geotechnical engineering and are consistent with the Idriss and Boulanger (2008) monograph, confirmed by web search results. The physical consequence - near-zero effective stress as $r_u$ approaches unity - follows from the principle of effective stress and is universally accepted in the literature. The dependence of the stability consequence on contractive or dilative material state is consistent with the flow liquefaction versus cyclic mobility distinction addressed in the following paragraph.]\n\n\"Cyclic mobility\" and \"flow liquefaction\" are the two principal distinct failure modes associated with excess pore-pressure generation in saturated soils under cyclic loading, and their distinction is essential for accurate TSF reporting. Flow liquefaction occurs in contractive soils - including loose, saturated, fine-grained tailings - where the residual undrained shear strength mobilised after triggering is insufficient to maintain stability under the existing static shear stresses, resulting in a catastrophic, sustained loss of resistance and runout. Cyclic mobility occurs in soils that retain a positive residual undrained shear strength after triggering, producing transient loss of effective stress and large cyclic strains without a sustained flow failure. [WEB:https://itascasoftware.com/resources/when-cyclic-mobility-isnt-flow-liquefaction/] ICOLD Bulletin 98 and the Idriss and Boulanger (2008) framework distinguish these mechanisms, and ANCOLD (2019) recommends conservatively assuming flow liquefaction for saturated, brittle, contractive tailings absent evidence supporting a less severe classification. [WEB:https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/] In a technical report, the failure mode being assessed should be named explicitly, and the material evidence - penetration resistance profile, gradation, plasticity index - that supports the classification should be cited.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The distinction between flow liquefaction and cyclic mobility is a well-established geotechnical concept confirmed by the web search results, including the Itasca resource and the general liquefaction literature. The ANCOLD recommendation to assume flow liquefaction conservatively for contractive saturated tailings is confirmed by the ANCOLD search result, which directly quotes this provision. The ICOLD Bulletin 98 attribution is confirmed by the search results identifying that bulletin's subject matter as tailings dams and seismicity.]\n\n\"Liquefaction potential\" is used in the Youd et al. (2001) framework and in the ICOLD tailings dam bulletins to describe the susceptibility of a saturated soil deposit to liquefaction triggering under a specified dynamic loading. [WEB:https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/23474/chapter/6] The quantitative measure of liquefaction potential is the factor of safety against triggering:\n\n$$FS_L = \\frac{CRR_{7.5} \\cdot MSF \\cdot K_\\sigma}{CSR}$$\n\nwhere $CRR_{7.5}$ is the cyclic resistance ratio corrected to a reference earthquake magnitude of $M_w = 7.5$, $MSF$ is the magnitude scaling factor that adjusts for the duration and equivalent cycles of the actual event, and $K_\\sigma$ is the overburden correction factor. When a blast event rather than an earthquake provides the dynamic demand, the CSR must be estimated from the blast-induced ground motion parameters and a representative equivalent number of uniform stress cycles must be assigned to the transient loading, making $FS_L$ a conditional quantity whose value is governed by these project-specific input choices. Values of $FS_L > 1$ indicate that the resistance exceeds the demand for the stated assumptions, while $FS_L \\leq 1$ indicates a triggering condition; the margin separating these states should be reported alongside the governing assumptions.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The Youd et al. (2001) framework, the definition of $FS_L$ in terms of $CRR_{7.5}$, $MSF$, $K_\\sigma$, and CSR, and the characterisation of $FS_L$ as a conditional output are confirmed by multiple web search results including the National Academies Press and University of California Davis resources. The formula structure is consistent with standard presentations. The conditional qualification for blast loading is consistent with the absence of a codified blast-adaptation procedure in any source retrieved.]\n\n\"Deformation\" and \"stability\" are used in the ANCOLD guidelines and ICOLD bulletins as distinct but related performance parameters for tailings dam assessment under dynamic loading. Stability refers to the resistance of the TSF embankment and its foundation to slope failure, evaluated as a factor of safety against global instability; ANCOLD (2019) prescribes a minimum value of 1.1 under transient seismic conditions as a quantitative performance target. [WEB:https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/] Deformation refers to the permanent displacements accumulated during a dynamic event, evaluated through analyses such as the Newmark sliding-block method or fully coupled dynamic numerical analysis; it is possible for a structure to remain nominally stable - maintaining a factor of safety above unity throughout the event - while accumulating permanent deformations that damage the crest, impair drainage elements, or compromise the freeboard required for safe operation. A technical report should distinguish stability assessment from deformation assessment and should state explicitly which analyses have been performed, which have been omitted, and why.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The distinction between deformation and stability as performance parameters in dam engineering is a standard professional convention supported by the ANCOLD guidelines (confirmed via web search) and consistent with ICOLD practice. The ANCOLD FS of 1.1 for seismic conditions is confirmed by both the ANCOLD product page search result and the SANCOLD search result. The Newmark sliding-block method for deformation estimation is a standard geotechnical reference that is consistent with the ANCOLD search result content.]\n\n\"Monitoring\" in the TSF context encompasses instrumented surveillance programs designed to detect developing adverse conditions before they progress to a breach or failure event. [WEB:https://mining.ca/resources/guides-manuals/a-guide-to-the-management-of-tailings-facilities-version-3-1-2019/] [KB:T1.compile.research.md] The GISTM and the IFC EHS Mining Guidelines require integrated performance monitoring programs that include piezometric measurement of pore pressure within the embankment and tailings mass, geotechnical surface monitoring of crest and slope displacement, and vibration monitoring of blast events affecting the facility. In the specific context of blasting effects on a TSF, monitoring serves two distinct functions: a compliance-verification function, demonstrating that measured blast-vibration parameters do not exceed the project-specific thresholds documented in the OMS manual and required by the MHSA COP; and a geotechnical feedback function, providing measured pore-pressure response data that can be compared with model predictions and used to update or validate the analytical assessment of liquefaction potential. A report that presents monitoring results should identify which of these functions the presented data serve and should confirm that the monitoring instrumentation, acquisition parameters, and analysis method are consistent with the requirements of the applicable standard or guidance document, including ISO 4866:2010 for vibration data and ICOLD Bulletin 104 for piezometric data.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The dual function of monitoring - regulatory compliance verification and geotechnical feedback - is consistent with the combined requirements of GISTM (confirmed via KB and ICMM web search result), IFC EHS Mining (confirmed via KB and IFC website search results), MAC OMS framework (confirmed via MAC website), and the MHSA COP requirement (confirmed via DMRE 2024 guideline web search result). The reference to ISO 4866 for vibration data and ICOLD Bulletin 104 for piezometric data is consistent with the content of those documents as confirmed in the KB and web search results respectively.]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":74,"oldLines":11,"newStart":74,"newLines":11,"lines":[" "," \"Cyclic mobility\" and \"flow liquefaction\" are the two principal distinct failure modes associated with excess pore-pressure generation in saturated soils under cyclic loading, and their distinction is essential for accurate TSF reporting. Flow liquefaction occurs in contractive soils - including loose, saturated, fine-grained tailings - where the residual undrained shear strength mobilised after triggering is insufficient to maintain stability under the existing static shear stresses, resulting in a catastrophic, sustained loss of resistance and runout. Cyclic mobility occurs in soils that retain a positive residual undrained shear strength after triggering, producing transient loss of effective stress and large cyclic strains without a sustained flow failure. [WEB:https://itascasoftware.com/resources/when-cyclic-mobility-isnt-flow-liquefaction/] ICOLD Bulletin 98 and the Idriss and Boulanger (2008) framework distinguish these mechanisms, and ANCOLD (2019) recommends conservatively assuming flow liquefaction for saturated, brittle, contractive tailings absent evidence supporting a less severe classification. [WEB:https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/] In a technical report, the failure mode being assessed should be named explicitly, and the material evidence - penetration resistance profile, gradation, plasticity index - that supports the classification should be cited.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The distinction between flow liquefaction and cyclic mobility is a well-established geotechnical concept confirmed by the web search results, including the Itasca resource and the general liquefaction literature. The ANCOLD recommendation to assume flow liquefaction conservatively for contractive saturated tailings is confirmed by the ANCOLD search result, which directly quotes this provision. The ICOLD Bulletin 98 attribution is confirmed by the search results identifying that bulletin's subject matter as tailings dams and seismicity.]"," ","-\"Liquefaction potential\" is used in the Youd et al. (2001) framework and in the ICOLD tailings dam bulletins to describe the susceptibility of a saturated soil deposit to liquefaction triggering under a specified dynamic loading. [WEB:https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/23474/chapter/6] The quantitative measure of liquefaction potential is the factor of safety against triggering:","+\"Liquefaction potential\" is used in the Youd et al. (2001) framework and in the ICOLD tailings dam bulletins to describe the susceptibility of a saturated soil deposit to liquefaction triggering under a specified dynamic loading, with the quantitative measure expressed as the factor of safety against triggering:^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The use of the term \"liquefaction potential\" in the Youd et al. (2001) framework and in ICOLD tailings bulletins is confirmed by web search results including the National Academies Press summary. The characterisation of the factor of safety as the standard quantitative measure of liquefaction potential is consistent with those sources. [WEB:https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/23474/chapter/6] This paragraph introduces the display equation below.]"," "," $$FS_L = \\frac{CRR_{7.5} \\cdot MSF \\cdot K_\\sigma}{CSR}$$"," ","-where $CRR_{7.5}$ is the cyclic resistance ratio corrected to a reference earthquake magnitude of $M_w = 7.5$, $MSF$ is the magnitude scaling factor that adjusts for the duration and equivalent cycles of the actual event, and $K_\\sigma$ is the overburden correction factor. When a blast event rather than an earthquake provides the dynamic demand, the CSR must be estimated from the blast-induced ground motion parameters and a representative equivalent number of uniform stress cycles must be assigned to the transient loading, making $FS_L$ a conditional quantity whose value is governed by these project-specific input choices. Values of $FS_L > 1$ indicate that the resistance exceeds the demand for the stated assumptions, while $FS_L \\leq 1$ indicates a triggering condition; the margin separating these states should be reported alongside the governing assumptions.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The Youd et al. (2001) framework, the definition of $FS_L$ in terms of $CRR_{7.5}$, $MSF$, $K_\\sigma$, and CSR, and the characterisation of $FS_L$ as a conditional output are confirmed by multiple web search results including the National Academies Press and University of California Davis resources. The formula structure is consistent with standard presentations. The conditional qualification for blast loading is consistent with the absence of a codified blast-adaptation procedure in any source retrieved.]","+where $CRR_{7.5}$ is the cyclic resistance ratio corrected to a reference earthquake magnitude of $M_w = 7.5$, $MSF$ is the magnitude scaling factor that adjusts for the duration and equivalent cycles of the actual event, and $K_\\sigma$ is the overburden correction factor. When a blast event rather than an earthquake provides the dynamic demand, the CSR must be estimated from the blast-induced ground motion parameters and a representative equivalent number of uniform stress cycles must be assigned to the transient loading, making $FS_L$ a conditional quantity whose value is governed by these project-specific input choices. Values of $FS_L > 1$ indicate that the resistance exceeds the demand for the stated assumptions, while $FS_L \\leq 1$ indicates a triggering condition; the margin separating these states should be reported alongside the governing assumptions.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The definition of $FS_L$ in terms of $CRR_{7.5}$, $MSF$, $K_\\sigma$, and CSR, and the characterisation of $FS_L$ as a conditional output for blast loading scenarios, are confirmed by multiple web search results including the National Academies Press and University of California Davis resources. The formula structure is consistent with standard presentations of the Youd et al. (2001) and Idriss and Boulanger (2008) frameworks. The conditional qualification for blast loading is consistent with the absence of a codified blast-specific adaptation procedure in any source retrieved during this research.]"," "," \"Deformation\" and \"stability\" are used in the ANCOLD guidelines and ICOLD bulletins as distinct but related performance parameters for tailings dam assessment under dynamic loading. Stability refers to the resistance of the TSF embankment and its foundation to slope failure, evaluated as a factor of safety against global instability; ANCOLD (2019) prescribes a minimum value of 1.1 under transient seismic conditions as a quantitative performance target. [WEB:https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/] Deformation refers to the permanent displacements accumulated during a dynamic event, evaluated through analyses such as the Newmark sliding-block method or fully coupled dynamic numerical analysis; it is possible for a structure to remain nominally stable - maintaining a factor of safety above unity throughout the event - while accumulating permanent deformations that damage the crest, impair drainage elements, or compromise the freeboard required for safe operation. A technical report should distinguish stability assessment from deformation assessment and should state explicitly which analyses have been performed, which have been omitted, and why.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The distinction between deformation and stability as performance parameters in dam engineering is a standard professional convention supported by the ANCOLD guidelines (confirmed via web search) and consistent with ICOLD practice. The ANCOLD FS of 1.1 for seismic conditions is confirmed by both the ANCOLD product page search result and the SANCOLD search result. The Newmark sliding-block method for deformation estimation is a standard geotechnical reference that is consistent with the ANCOLD search result content.]"," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
