{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_011k3HiSHiaHPxwu7PaqKSbi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012psTKZ5uutPKb7UoChPbxv","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"grep -n \"\\$\\$\" /Users/averrik/Cloud/github/projects/AR-S2J2J.R/TITO/sessions/run-20260706_164057_11000/T8.research.md","description":"List all display math block markers to verify equation placement"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5743,"cache_read_input_tokens":112158,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5743,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":29,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"193e9b03-f0e0-48bb-82cd-24114a1d1cbc","uuid":"0d268d81-0d37-437f-bde2-8ed3fc464b77","request_id":"req_011CcmPcjuLxoA6vaRnHftdt"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012psTKZ5uutPKb7UoChPbxv","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (49.2KB). Full output saved to: /Users/averrik/.claude/projects/-Users-averrik-Cloud-github-projects-AR-S2J2J-R-TITO-sessions-run-20260706-164057-11000/193e9b03-f0e0-48bb-82cd-24114a1d1cbc/tool-results/bq57y4f84.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\n1:# Guidance Documents for Blasting Effects on Tailings Storage Facilities: AR-S2J2J.R\n2:\n3:---\n4:\n5:### SLOT 1: Authoritative Guidance Documents Covering Blasting Effects on TSFs\n6:\n7:The international industry standards that most directly address dynamic loading, vibration monitoring, pore-pressure response, and liquefaction screening for tailings storage facilities are the Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management (GISTM, 2020) and the IFC Environmental, Health, and Safety Guidelines for Mining (2007). The GISTM, developed under the auspices of the United Nations Environment Programme, the International Council on Mining and Metals, and the Principles for Responsible Investment, requires operators to establish an interdisciplinary knowledge base encompassing geotechnical and seismicity information, to document design criteria explicitly, and to implement integrated performance monitoring throughout the facility life cycle. [KB:T1.compile.research.md] The standard does not prescribe fixed numerical vibration thresholds for blast-induced loading on TSFs; it frames safety as a risk-management obligation demanding project-specific assessment and continuous monitoring as the means of demonstrating performance. The IFC EHS Guidelines for Mining (2007) require that tailings impoundments and dams be planned, designed, and operated such that geotechnical risks are assessed and managed throughout the mine life cycle, and they require designs to reflect internationally recognised engineering practices and local seismic conditions, treating geotechnical risk assessment - including blast-induced ground motion effects on tailings facilities - as a distinct discipline rather than an extension of building-vibration compliance. [KB:T1.compile.research.md]^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: Both GISTM and IFC EHS Mining Guidelines are confirmed in the KB (T1.compile.research.md) with content that is consistent with established characterisations of these documents. The claim that \n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"193e9b03-f0e0-48bb-82cd-24114a1d1cbc","uuid":"7424407d-cab3-4b42-a55e-d72d43e36811","timestamp":"2026-07-06T19:54:51.678Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"1:# Guidance Documents for Blasting Effects on Tailings Storage Facilities: AR-S2J2J.R\n2:\n3:---\n4:\n5:### SLOT 1: Authoritative Guidance Documents Covering Blasting Effects on TSFs\n6:\n7:The international industry standards that most directly address dynamic loading, vibration monitoring, pore-pressure response, and liquefaction screening for tailings storage facilities are the Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management (GISTM, 2020) and the IFC Environmental, Health, and Safety Guidelines for Mining (2007). The GISTM, developed under the auspices of the United Nations Environment Programme, the International Council on Mining and Metals, and the Principles for Responsible Investment, requires operators to establish an interdisciplinary knowledge base encompassing geotechnical and seismicity information, to document design criteria explicitly, and to implement integrated performance monitoring throughout the facility life cycle. [KB:T1.compile.research.md] The standard does not prescribe fixed numerical vibration thresholds for blast-induced loading on TSFs; it frames safety as a risk-management obligation demanding project-specific assessment and continuous monitoring as the means of demonstrating performance. The IFC EHS Guidelines for Mining (2007) require that tailings impoundments and dams be planned, designed, and operated such that geotechnical risks are assessed and managed throughout the mine life cycle, and they require designs to reflect internationally recognised engineering practices and local seismic conditions, treating geotechnical risk assessment - including blast-induced ground motion effects on tailings facilities - as a distinct discipline rather than an extension of building-vibration compliance. [KB:T1.compile.research.md]^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: Both GISTM and IFC EHS Mining Guidelines are confirmed in the KB (T1.compile.research.md) with content that is consistent with established characterisations of these documents. The claim that neither prescribes fixed blast vibration thresholds for TSFs is directly supported by the KB statement that GISTM requires an interdisciplinary knowledge base and performance monitoring rather than fixed limits, and by the IFC guidance treating tailings facilities as requiring risk assessment and internationally recognised engineering practice.]\n8:\n9:The ANCOLD Guidelines on Tailings Dams, Revision 1 (2019), published by the Australian National Committee on Large Dams, provide the most comprehensive single-document treatment of dynamic and seismic loading for tailings storage facilities among the available authoritative sources. [WEB:https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/] The 2019 revision explicitly aligns tailings dam seismic design criteria with the companion ANCOLD Guidelines for Design of Dams and Appurtenant Structures for Earthquake (2019), addresses both static and cyclic liquefaction of saturated tailings, and recommends conservatively assuming that static liquefaction will occur for brittle, contractive, saturated tailings unless site-specific evidence supports a less conservative classification. The document addresses planning, design, construction, operation, and closure, treating earthquake loading and factor-of-safety requirements under dynamic conditions as subjects requiring engineering analysis throughout the facility life cycle rather than as compliance thresholds satisfied by reference to a look-up table.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The ANCOLD Guidelines on Tailings Dams Revision 1 (2019) are confirmed by web search results including the official ANCOLD product page and the publicly available 2019 addendum text. The specific content claims - alignment with earthquake design guidelines, treatment of static and cyclic liquefaction, conservative assumption of static liquefaction for contractive tailings - are each corroborated by the search results, which quote these provisions directly.]\n10:\n11:ICOLD has produced a series of bulletins that collectively constitute the authoritative international technical foundation for seismic assessment of tailings dams. ICOLD Bulletin 98 (1995), Tailings Dams and Seismicity, addresses the seismic behavior of tailings facilities specifically, covering liquefaction potential, dynamic stability, and modes of failure associated with cyclic ground motion. ICOLD Bulletin 104 (1996), Monitoring of Tailings Dams, establishes the framework for instrumented monitoring programs, including piezometric surveillance of pore-pressure response in TSF embankments. ICOLD Bulletin 121 (2001), Tailings Dams - Risk of Dangerous Occurrences, documents lessons from actual failures including seismically triggered and liquefaction-related events. ICOLD Bulletin 148 (2011), Selecting Seismic Parameters for Large Dams, provides guidance on seismic hazard characterisation and the selection of design earthquake parameters applicable to large storage facilities including tailings dams. [WEB:https://www.icoldchile.cl/boletines/148.pdf] ICOLD Bulletin 194 (2022), Tailings Dam Safety, is the most recent unified ICOLD statement on tailings dam governance and engineering, consolidating seismic requirements alongside operational and monitoring obligations in a form intended to supersede and integrate the earlier bulletins for contemporary professional practice. [WEB:https://klohn.com/wp-content/uploads/B194-ICOLD-Tailings-Dam-Safety.pdf]^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The ICOLD bulletins listed (98, 104, 121, 148, 194) are confirmed through multiple independent web search results. Bulletins 98 and 104 are specifically named in the ICOLD bulletin sequence summaries returned by the search. Bulletin 148 is available as a PDF from the ICOLD Chile chapter. Bulletin 194 is referenced in a UNECE presentation and available through the Klohn Crippen Berger website. The attribution of topics (seismicity, monitoring, risk of dangerous occurrences, seismic parameters, tailings safety) to the respective bulletins is consistent across multiple search results.]\n12:\n13:ISO 4866:2010, Mechanical Vibration and Shock - Vibration of Fixed Structures, provides the foundational measurement methodology for blast-vibration monitoring programs. [KB:T1.compile.research.md] The standard applies to structures above and below ground including earth structures such as dykes and embankments, and addresses sources including explosions and construction activities; however, it explicitly excludes certain special structures, including dams, from its scope. Because a TSF is typically classified as a dam for regulatory and engineering purposes, ISO 4866:2010 is directly applicable as the technical framework for blast-vibration transducer placement, waveform acquisition, and frequency-content characterisation in TSF monitoring programs, while falling short of providing structural acceptance criteria for the facility itself. The standard's exclusion of dams from its direct scope is relevant to report framing: ISO 4866:2010 supports the measurement methodology but does not establish whether a measured vibration level is acceptable for a particular TSF, which remains a matter of project-specific engineering assessment.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The characterisation of ISO 4866:2010 - its applicability to earth structures including dykes and embankments, its coverage of explosion sources, and its specific exclusion of dams - is drawn directly from the KB (T1.compile.research.md), which is treated as authoritative for this audit step. The inference that a TSF classified as a dam falls outside the direct compliance scope of ISO 4866 follows from the stated scope exclusion in the standard itself and is not contradicted by any other source cited in this document.]\n14:\n15:The geotechnical liquefaction-assessment literature constitutes an integral part of the analytical framework for evaluating the response of saturated tailings to blast-induced dynamic loading, even though that literature was developed primarily for earthquake loading scenarios. Youd et al. (2001), summarising the consensus reached at the 1996 NCEER and 1998 NCEER/NSF workshops on evaluation of liquefaction resistance of soils, established the simplified stress-based framework in which the cyclic stress ratio (CSR) representing dynamic demand is compared with the cyclic resistance ratio (CRR) representing soil resistance to produce a factor of safety against liquefaction triggering. [WEB:https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/23474/chapter/6] Idriss and Boulanger (2008), Soil Liquefaction During Earthquakes, updated and refined those procedures with revised magnitude scaling factors, improved handling of low-plasticity fine-grained soils of the type commonly encountered in tailings, and updated CPT and SPT-based CRR correlations. [WEB:https://www.researchgate.net/publication/288624104_Soil_liquefaction_during_earthquakes] Both frameworks are designed for earthquake-induced cyclic loading, and their application to blast-induced ground motion requires project-specific adaptation of the loading characterisation, particularly the estimation of equivalent uniform cycles associated with a transient blast event.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The Youd et al. (2001) and Idriss and Boulanger (2008) frameworks are widely documented authoritative references in the geotechnical engineering literature. Their content - CSR/CRR framework, magnitude scaling factors, CPT and SPT correlations, treatment of fine-grained soils - is confirmed by multiple web search results, including the National Academies Press summary and multiple faculty PDFs. The qualification that adaptation is needed for blasting scenarios is standard geotechnical practice and is consistent with the absence of any blast-specific version of these frameworks in the search results.]\n16:\n17:The Mining Association of Canada's Guide to the Management of Tailings Facilities, Version 3.1 (2019), provides an operations-oriented framework for TSF safety that complements the design-phase guidance of ANCOLD and ICOLD. [WEB:https://mining.ca/resources/guides-manuals/a-guide-to-the-management-of-tailings-facilities-version-3-1-2019/] The MAC guide structures TSF management through an Operation, Maintenance and Surveillance (OMS) manual framework that requires documented monitoring of all identified performance indicators, including instrumented pore-pressure and vibration responses where blasting occurs in proximity to a TSF. While the MAC guide does not prescribe fixed quantitative vibration thresholds for blast events, its OMS framework establishes the operational mechanism by which project-specific monitoring trigger levels and alarm thresholds are defined, communicated to operators, and updated in response to observed performance.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The MAC Guide to the Management of Tailings Facilities Version 3.1 (2019) is confirmed via the official Mining Association of Canada website URL found in the web search results. The OMS framework characterisation is consistent with publicly available descriptions of the MAC guide and with the general practice in tailings management. The claim that the guide does not prescribe fixed vibration thresholds is consistent with its governance-and-management focus, which is confirmed by the search result descriptions of its content.]\n18:\n19:---\n20:\n21:### SLOT 2: South African versus International Applicability of Sources\n22:\n23:Within the South African regulatory environment, the primary legislative instrument governing mining blasting is the Mine Health and Safety Act 29 of 1996 (MHSA), which requires mine operators to prepare and implement a site-specific mandatory Code of Practice addressing health and safety risks associated with their particular operations. The Department of Mineral Resources and Energy issued the Guideline for a Mandatory Code of Practice for Minimum Standards on Ground Vibrations, Noise, Air-blast, and Flyrock near Surface Structures and Communities to be Protected in August 2024, establishing the current national baseline for blast-vibration control in South African mining under sections 9(2) and 9(3) of the MHSA. [WEB:https://cer.org.za/virtual-library/legislation/national/mining/mine-blasting-guideline-code-of-practice-for-minimum-standards-on-ground-vibrations-noise-air-blast-and-flyrock-near-surface-structures-and-communities-to-be-protected-2024] This guideline defines protected structures and communities in the context of surface mining, and operators must demonstrate compliance through a site-specific COP that describes monitoring arrangements and acceptable vibration levels for identified sensitive receivers. The 2024 DMRE blasting guideline is South African in origin and legally mandated, making it the primary regulatory compliance reference for blasting vibration limits in a South African mining project, including projects where blasting occurs in proximity to a TSF.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The existence and content of the South African MHSA (1996) and the DMRE August 2024 Guideline for a Mandatory Code of Practice are confirmed by web search results, including the Centre for Environmental Rights website entry and the Government Gazette reference. The MHSA sections cited (9(2) and 9(3)) are consistent with multiple South African legal references found in the search results. The characterisation of the guideline as defining protected structures and requiring site-specific COPs is consistent with the regulatory framework described in the search results.]\n24:\n25:The South African National Committee on Large Dams (SANCOLD) has produced guidance specifically directed at the South African context for tailings dam management. The SANCOLD document \"Your Tailings Dam\" (2020) addresses design, operation, and safety monitoring of tailings facilities within the South African legislative environment and explicitly notes two significant gaps in the local regulatory framework: first, that South African legislation does not prescribe the severity or return period of a seismic event that a tailings dam must withstand, leaving that determination as a matter of engineering judgement by the designer and owner; and second, that South African legislation does not yet directly address the GISTM requirements. [WEB:https://sancold.org.za/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/SANCOLD-Your-Tailings-Dam-2020.pdf] These observations are directly relevant to report framing: a project operating under South African jurisdiction cannot assume that the seismic or dynamic-loading acceptance criteria in any international standard have been adopted directly into South African law, and the engineer of record must state the basis on which any seismic performance target has been chosen.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The SANCOLD \"Your Tailings Dam\" (2020) document is confirmed by a web search result citing the SANCOLD website URL. The specific claims - that South African legislation does not prescribe seismic return periods for tailings dams and that it does not yet address GISTM - are drawn from the web search result text, which directly quotes or paraphrases these findings from the SANCOLD document. These are material observations specific to the South African regulatory context.]\n26:\n27:The international sources - GISTM, ANCOLD (2019), ICOLD bulletins 98, 104, 121, 148, and 194, the IFC EHS Guidelines for Mining, and ISO 4866:2010 - are applicable across jurisdictions as statements of good international practice and are accepted as technical reference documents for independent review in South Africa. [KB:T1.compile.research.md] [WEB:https://www.icmm.com/en-gb/our-principles/tailings/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management] Their adoption in a South African TSF report is appropriate as technical framing for engineering assessment, and lenders, financiers, and independent review panels routinely require alignment with GISTM and ICOLD guidance. However, the specific numerical targets in those documents - such as the ANCOLD minimum factor of safety of 1.1 under seismic loading, or earthquake return periods referenced in ICOLD Bulletin 148 - derive from Australian or international regulatory contexts and should be treated as engineering references rather than as South African legal requirements unless the project permit, lender technical schedules, or the Engineer of Record explicitly adopts them as binding project standards.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The categorisation of GISTM, ANCOLD, ICOLD, IFC EHS, and ISO 4866 as international references applicable across jurisdictions is well-established and confirmed by multiple web sources. The SANCOLD search result specifically confirms that South African legislation does not adopt GISTM directly, supporting the qualification that international numerical criteria are not automatically legally binding in South Africa. The mention of lender requirements for GISTM alignment is consistent with general knowledge of equator principles and DFI practice, though no specific URL confirming that point appears in the search results; the confidence rating reflects the direct documentary support for the primary regulatory gap claim.]\n28:\n29:The U.S. Office of Surface Mining Reclamation and Enforcement rules in 30 CFR Part 816 (Sections 816.67 and 816.68) are a mature regulatory example of how a blasting framework distinguishes fixed PPV thresholds for standard occupied structures from a project-specific approval process for other structures, including dams, impoundments, utilities, and underground mines. [KB:T1.compile.research.md] Under 30 CFR 816.67, the vibration limits for dams and impoundments are not automatically derived from the tabulated building thresholds but are submitted in the blasting plan and approved by the regulator on a project-specific basis. This regulatory architecture - separate fixed limits for standard receivers and a project-specific submission process for engineered earth structures - illustrates the principle that South African practice approximates through the MHSA COP process, even though the South African framework is articulated differently and does not reproduce the specific numerical values or definitional conventions of the U.S. regulations. Citing 30 CFR in a South African TSF report is appropriate as an international regulatory analogue illustrating best-practice structure, provided the report clearly identifies it as a U.S. federal rule rather than as a standard applicable in South Africa.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The characterisation of 30 CFR 816.67 and 816.68 is drawn directly from the KB (T1.compile.research.md), which is treated as authoritative for this audit step. The KB explicitly states that 30 CFR 816.67 provides project-specific limits for dams and impoundments submitted in the blasting plan rather than adopting the building-level thresholds. The inference that the South African COP process approximates this architecture is a supported analogy, not a legally equivalent statement, and is appropriately qualified.]\n30:\n31:---\n32:\n33:### SLOT 3: Quantitative Acceptance Criteria versus Project-Specific Assessment\n34:\n35:None of the primary authoritative sources for TSF safety - GISTM, ANCOLD (2019), ICOLD bulletins 98, 104, 121, 148, and 194, or the IFC EHS Guidelines for Mining - prescribes a universal fixed blast-vibration threshold expressed as a limiting peak particle velocity for protecting a tailings storage facility. The absence of a universal vibration limit for TSFs reflects the established recognition in geotechnical dam engineering that the dynamic response of a saturated tailings facility depends on material properties, fabric, degree of saturation, drainage conditions, confining stress, pore-pressure history, the geometry of the embankment, and the proximity and characteristics of the blasting source. None of these site-specific factors are captured by a generic PPV criterion derived from research on vibration effects in rigid or masonry building structures. [KB:T1.compile.research.md] [WEB:https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/]^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The claim that none of the listed authoritative TSF documents prescribes a universal blast PPV limit is strongly supported. The KB (T1.compile.research.md) states that 30 CFR requires project-specific plan submission for dams and impoundments rather than a tabulated threshold; GISTM and IFC EHS are characterised as requiring risk assessment and project-specific criteria. The ANCOLD web search results describe factor-of-safety and earthquake-return-period criteria but no universal PPV limit for blasting on TSFs. No web source retrieved a fixed PPV acceptance criterion for TSFs from any of the listed documents.]\n36:\n37:ANCOLD (2019) provides the most explicit quantitative framing available for TSF seismic performance, but its criteria are expressed in terms of structural stability factors of safety and design earthquake return periods rather than in terms of blast-vibration amplitude. The guidelines prescribe a minimum factor of safety of 1.1 for transient and seismic loading conditions, and recommend evaluating the facility against a 1:10,000 annual exceedance probability earthquake where liquefaction of tailings is likely to affect the stability of the embankment, unless a site-specific risk assessment demonstrates that an adequate risk profile is achieved with a lower seismic loading. [WEB:https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/] These quantitative targets are outputs of a project-specific engineering process - geotechnical characterisation, in-situ testing, liquefaction potential assessment, and dynamic stability analysis - and are not simplified look-up values. Applying them to blast-induced loading requires translating the blast-vibration record into equivalent dynamic demand parameters consistent with the geotechnical analysis framework, which involves project-specific assumptions about wave attenuation, loading duration, and equivalent cyclic cycles.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The ANCOLD (2019) factor-of-safety value of 1.1 for seismic conditions and the 1:10,000 AEP earthquake recommendation for liquefiable tailings are confirmed by two independent web search results: the ANCOLD product page summary and the SANCOLD web search result, which cites a minimum FOS of 1.1 for transient/seismic conditions in South African guidance citing ANCOLD practice. The conclusion that these criteria presuppose project-specific geotechnical analysis follows directly from the stated content of ANCOLD.]\n38:\n39:Liquefaction screening for saturated tailings under dynamic loading is performed within the simplified stress-based framework of Youd et al. (2001) or Idriss and Boulanger (2008), in which the cyclic stress ratio (CSR), representing the demand imposed by the dynamic event, is compared with the cyclic resistance ratio (CRR), representing the soil resistance to liquefaction triggering derived from in-situ penetration tests such as the SPT or CPT. [WEB:https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/23474/chapter/6] [WEB:https://www.researchgate.net/publication/288624104_Soil_liquefaction_during_earthquakes] The factor of safety against liquefaction is defined as:^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The simplified stress-based liquefaction screening framework of Youd et al. (2001) and Idriss and Boulanger (2008) is confirmed by multiple web search results including the National Academies Press and University of California Davis faculty documents. The description of SPT and CPT as the standard in-situ test methods for deriving CRR is consistent with those sources. This paragraph introduces the display equation below and contains no independent factual claim beyond the framework description, which is well-supported.]\n40:\n41:$$FS_L = \\frac{CRR_{7.5} \\cdot MSF \\cdot K_\\sigma}{CSR}$$\n42:\n43:where $CRR_{7.5}$ is the cyclic resistance ratio normalised to a reference earthquake magnitude of 7.5, $MSF$ is the magnitude scaling factor correcting for earthquake duration, and $K_\\sigma$ is the overburden correction factor. When blast-induced loading rather than earthquake loading is the triggering mechanism, the CSR must be estimated from the blast-induced ground motion waveform and a representative equivalent number of uniform loading cycles must be assigned to the transient event - a parameter that is far smaller for a blast than for a sustained earthquake of similar peak amplitude. This adaptation of the earthquake-based framework to blast loading requires project-specific assumptions on loading characterisation that are not codified in any universally adopted standard, making $FS_L$ a conditional engineering output dependent on those documented choices.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The definition of $FS_L$ with $CRR_{7.5}$, $MSF$, $K_\\sigma$, and CSR is consistent with the standard presentation in Youd et al. (2001) and Idriss and Boulanger (2008), confirmed by web search results. The qualification that blast loading requires adaptation of the number-of-cycles parameter is consistent with the documented shorter duration of blast events compared with earthquake events, as referenced in the blast-induced liquefaction literature found in the web search results. No codified universal blast-liquefaction screening procedure was identified in any source retrieved.]\n44:\n45:Pore-pressure monitoring programs serve as the empirical counterpart to analytical liquefaction screening. ICOLD Bulletin 104 (1996) and the MAC OMS framework require piezometer instrumentation within TSF embankments and tailings deposits to measure in-situ pore pressure, detect excess pore-pressure generation following dynamic events, and track pore-pressure dissipation rates. [WEB:https://mining.ca/resources/guides-manuals/a-guide-to-the-management-of-tailings-facilities-version-3-1-2019/] The monitoring data serve to calibrate and validate analytical models, to detect anomalous pore-pressure responses that may indicate incipient instability, and to confirm that measured vibration events do not produce lasting pore-pressure accumulation above the thresholds defined in the OMS manual. Acceptance criteria for pore-pressure response are project-specific, defined within the OMS manual or instrumentation response plan as trigger values and alarm thresholds derived from site-specific stability calculations rather than adopted from any generic standard or building-vibration criterion.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: ICOLD Bulletin 104 (1996) as a monitoring guideline for tailings dams is confirmed by the web search results. The MAC OMS framework and its requirement for documented monitoring including piezometric response is confirmed by the MAC Guide URL and summary descriptions. The characterisation of acceptance criteria as project-specific is consistent with all surveyed guidance documents, none of which supply a generic pore-pressure threshold for blast-event monitoring at TSFs.]\n46:\n47:---\n48:\n49:### SLOT 4: Report Framing to Distinguish Criteria, Monitoring Requirements, and Assumptions\n50:\n51:A professional technical report on the effects of nearby blasting on a TSF must clearly delineate four distinct categories of statement: published reference criteria drawn from identified standards; project-specific criteria established for the facility in question by the Engineer of Record; monitoring requirements arising from regulatory obligations, best-practice standards, and design documentation; and analysis assumptions made in the assessment. Conflating these categories - in particular, applying a criterion developed for a different receiver type, such as a building-damage PPV threshold from a vibration standard, as though it governs TSF structural performance - misrepresents the state of professional practice and may produce technically indefensible conclusions that expose the report to challenge during regulatory review or independent assessment. [KB:T1.compile.research.md] [WEB:https://klohn.com/wp-content/uploads/B194-ICOLD-Tailings-Dam-Safety.pdf]^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The four-category framework for report organisation is consistent with the practice distinctions described throughout the cited sources: the KB (T1.compile.research.md) explicitly distinguishes structural damage criteria from human-exposure criteria and from monitoring/reporting requirements, and from project-specific plan submissions. ICOLD Bulletin 194 (2022), referenced via the Klohn Crippen Berger URL, consolidates governance and engineering requirements in a manner that requires explicit documentation of design criteria as a distinct category. The characterisation of conflation as a reportable risk is a professional judgement consistent with the regulatory framing described in the cited sources.]\n52:\n53:Published reference criteria from building-vibration standards such as BS 7385-2:1993 and from human-comfort and human-exposure standards such as BS 6472-2:2008 or ISO 2631-2 do not govern TSF structural performance and are outside the scope of the present assessment. Those documents are explicitly scoped to building structures and to occupant physiological response respectively; none establishes an acceptance criterion for an earth structure such as a tailings embankment or impoundment. Presenting a PPV threshold derived from those standards as the criterion for protecting a TSF implies a scope of applicability that those standards do not claim, and it obscures the absence of a published fixed vibration threshold for the engineering question actually under assessment. ISO 4866:2010, which does include earth structures within certain parts of its scope, provides measurement and evaluation methodology","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"persistedOutputPath":"/Users/averrik/.claude/projects/-Users-averrik-Cloud-github-projects-AR-S2J2J-R-TITO-sessions-run-20260706-164057-11000/193e9b03-f0e0-48bb-82cd-24114a1d1cbc/tool-results/bq57y4f84.txt","persistedOutputSize":50337}}
