{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01MYM9bsJbaN8J5sjRR4rWF9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The manuscript is complete. Here is a summary of what was produced:\n\n**Manuscript written to `/Users/averrik/Cloud/github/projects/AR-S2J2J.R/TITO/sessions/run-20260706_164057_11000/T8.research.md`**\n\n---\n\nThe document is 85 lines, 6,531 words, and covers all five slots in flowing paragraphs without subheadings or bullet points:\n\n**SLOT 1** surveys the authoritative guidance document landscape: GISTM (2020), IFC EHS Guidelines for Mining (2007), ANCOLD Guidelines on Tailings Dams Rev. 1 (2019), the full ICOLD tailings bulletin series (B98, B104, B121, B148, B194), ISO 4866:2010, and the Youd et al. (2001) / Idriss & Boulanger (2008) liquefaction frameworks - with each source's scope relative to dynamic loading, vibration monitoring, pore-pressure response, and liquefaction screening characterised.\n\n**SLOT 2** distinguishes South African sources (MHSA, DMRE 2024 COP, SANCOLD 2020) from international references, noting explicitly that South African legislation does not prescribe seismic return periods for TSFs and has not adopted GISTM, which has direct implications for how international numerical criteria must be presented.\n\n**SLOT 3** establishes that no authoritative TSF guidance supplies a universal fixed PPV blast limit; quantitative criteria exist in the form of stability factors of safety (ANCOLD FS >= 1.1) and the $FS_L = CRR_{7.5} \\cdot MSF \\cdot K_\\sigma / CSR$ liquefaction screening output, both of which are project-specific engineering results requiring site characterisation.\n\n**SLOT 4** frames the four distinct reporting categories (published reference criteria, project-specific criteria, monitoring requirements, and analysis assumptions) and explicitly calls out that building-damage and human-comfort standards (BS 7385-2, BS 6472-2, ISO 2631-2) do not govern TSF structural performance and must not be presented as though they do.\n\n**SLOT 5** documents source-backed terminology: dynamic loading, transient vibration, pore-pressure generation ($r_u = \\Delta u / \\sigma'_{v0}$), cyclic mobility, flow liquefaction, liquefaction potential, deformation, stability, and monitoring - each term traced to the specific guidance document that uses it.\n\nAll 29 prose paragraphs carry audit footnotes; the 3 display math blocks are correctly flanked by footnoted lead-in and interpretation paragraphs; citations are in `[KB:...]` and `[WEB:...]` token form throughout."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1549,"cache_read_input_tokens":119243,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1549,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":36,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"193e9b03-f0e0-48bb-82cd-24114a1d1cbc","uuid":"d4016a96-aa88-4427-8449-10349928f5d9","request_id":"req_011CcmPffGdBofSfM5UVCCx4"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":886827,"duration_api_ms":916795,"ttft_ms":2582,"ttft_stream_ms":2215,"time_to_request_ms":55,"num_turns":42,"result":"The manuscript is complete. Here is a summary of what was produced:\n\n**Manuscript written to `/Users/averrik/Cloud/github/projects/AR-S2J2J.R/TITO/sessions/run-20260706_164057_11000/T8.research.md`**\n\n---\n\nThe document is 85 lines, 6,531 words, and covers all five slots in flowing paragraphs without subheadings or bullet points:\n\n**SLOT 1** surveys the authoritative guidance document landscape: GISTM (2020), IFC EHS Guidelines for Mining (2007), ANCOLD Guidelines on Tailings Dams Rev. 1 (2019), the full ICOLD tailings bulletin series (B98, B104, B121, B148, B194), ISO 4866:2010, and the Youd et al. (2001) / Idriss & Boulanger (2008) liquefaction frameworks - with each source's scope relative to dynamic loading, vibration monitoring, pore-pressure response, and liquefaction screening characterised.\n\n**SLOT 2** distinguishes South African sources (MHSA, DMRE 2024 COP, SANCOLD 2020) from international references, noting explicitly that South African legislation does not prescribe seismic return periods for TSFs and has not adopted GISTM, which has direct implications for how international numerical criteria must be presented.\n\n**SLOT 3** establishes that no authoritative TSF guidance supplies a universal fixed PPV blast limit; quantitative criteria exist in the form of stability factors of safety (ANCOLD FS >= 1.1) and the $FS_L = CRR_{7.5} \\cdot MSF \\cdot K_\\sigma / CSR$ liquefaction screening output, both of which are project-specific engineering results requiring site characterisation.\n\n**SLOT 4** frames the four distinct reporting categories (published reference criteria, project-specific criteria, monitoring requirements, and analysis assumptions) and explicitly calls out that building-damage and human-comfort standards (BS 7385-2, BS 6472-2